{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if 21 <= x[\\\"article\\\"] <= 30]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if 1 <= x[\\\"article\\\"] <= 10]'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_2","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if 31 <= x[\\\"article\\\"] <= 40]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"article\": 31, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"tomshardware.com\", \"title\": \"Unannounced Nvidia RTX 50 Super GPUs appear in Seasonic PSU calculator — unreleased graphics cards shown with 10-17% higher TGP over original models\", \"text\": \"Unannounced Nvidia RTX 50 Super GPUs appear in Seasonic PSU calculator — unreleased graphics cards shown with 10-17% higher TGP over original models | Tom's Hardware\\nUnannounced Nvidia RTX 50 Super GPUs appear in Seasonic PSU calculator — unreleased graphics cards shown with 10-17% higher TGP over original models\\nBy Jeffrey Kampman\\nMore memory, more performance, more power. Maybe.\\nThe GeForce RTX 50-series desktop graphics lineup has remained unchanged for just over a year now (since the introduction of the RTX 5050), and none of the conversations we've had at major trade shows suggests that a mid-cycle Super refresh will occur any time soon. But there are still signs that such an update could still happen at some point, and the latest sign comes from Seasonic, which has listed an RTX 5080 Super, an RTX 5070 Ti Super, and RTX 5070 Ti Super in its PSU wattage calculator .\\nYou can click through the calculator and assemble a hypothetical system with these unannounced products inside.\\nTo be useful, this calculator also provides board power numbers for these as-yet-unreleased cards, and that gives us another bit of juicy info. Given that no official specifications for these GPUs exist, it's impossible to say whether these figures are accurate. But it does allow us to speculate a bit on how they might stack up to existing products.\\nUnannounced RTX 50 Super-series TGPs\\n*As listed in Seasonic PSU calculator. Unconfirmed by Nvidia.\\nSeasonic gives this purported RTX 5080 Super a board power of 415W in its calculator, or 15% higher than the existing RTX 5080's 360W envelope . That makes sense, because the RTX 5080's GB203 GPU is already fully enabled, so any Super version of that card would have to lean on higher power limits and more aggressive clock speeds to see any baseline performance benefit.\\nThat figure could also partially account for slightly higher power usage from 8 GB more GDDR7 memory on such a card. Past RTX 50 Super-series rumors have suggested that Nvidia will boost VRAM capacity on those products by moving to higher-density GDDR7 modules with 3GB of capacity each. GDDR7's power consumption as part of the overall board picture is relatively small, but more of it will still matter.\\nIf Seasonic's figures are accurate, we should also expect a similarly sized TGP increase out of the RTX 5070 Ti Super, whose 350W rating is 17% higher than that of the RTX 5070 Ti . The RTX 5070 Super, meanwhile, gets only a 10% TGP bump over the RTX 5070 , from 250W to 275W. Both of these cards rely on GPUs that are slightly cut down from their full available resources, so it's possible that Nvidia could boost their performance through a balance of enabling more Streaming Multiprocessors (SMs) in addition to boosting clocks through higher power envelopes.\\nBeware of extrapolating performance improvements directly from these percentages, though. Our own testing has shown that any real-world performance benefits from these power limit increases are likely to be smaller than those figures would suggest.\\nAs our review of the MSI RTX 5090 Lightning Z showed , the largest performance boosts from higher power limits are likely to be concentrated in ray-traced and path-traced games, whose computational intensity is significantly higher than pure raster titles and is more likely to run the GPU into its power limits.\\nIn any event, we shouldn't expect to see these products any time soon. Nvidia has instead been focused on getting more out of existing Blackwell silicon with software improvements such as DLSS 4.5 upscaling and Multi-Frame Generation multipliers up to 6X.\\nThese technologies enable higher output frame rates and image quality with lower input resolution than past DLSS technologies, and the boost to both performance and image quality from those technologies in tandem has certainly made existing Blackwell products more appealing than they were at launch.\\nBut as monitor refresh rates continue to climb thanks to ongoing improvements to OLED and LCD panels, and next-generation HDMI 2.2 connectors looming over 2027, a hardware update of some kind that boosts baseline performance and potentially implements support for those standards seems practical at some point. Given these primarily consumer-focused improvements, an announcement at CES 2027 or Computex 2027 might make sense. As with any future product rumors, however, only time will truly tell.\\nPrevious leaks indicated these higher TDP/TBPs. Also a slightly higher core count for the RTX 5070 Super, I think everything else was the same though.\\nI'll believe it when I actually see it.\\nThe real question is the price: Are we going to see a starting point below $1800? I doubt it. Given the existing climate of hardware shortages and increased pricing for everything, we're not getting a redux of the 40 series when it comes to price.\\nWell, they need spare 3 GB GDDR7 modules... as well as allocating TSMC 4N wafers to make 50 Super happen. But this could be an easy way for Nvidia to delay the 60 series by a year or more, without completely throwing in the towel on consumer hardware.\\nThe price... will not be nice.\\nWonder if this is a signal that there is a softening on the ai orders and they are allocating back. Seems like a tiny upgrade so wonder how this works for them strategically\\nBlackwell is made on TSMC 4nm nodes, Rubin on 3nm. Production is shifting to Rubin for a Q3 launch. They may have more 4nm wafers available, assuming they are going to dial down Blackwell datacenter production.\\nThey may have accumulated the necessary volume of 3 GB GDDR7 modules. These are used in RTX Pro Blackwell to hit up to 96 GB on 512-bit in workstations, whereas the datacenter cards are using HBM, and DGX/RTX Spark are using LPDDR5X. So most of Nvidia's AI-oriented products don't use GDDR7. The 5090 is basically an AI product, but it uses 2 GB GDDR7 modules to get to 32 GB and there haven't been indications of a 48 GB variant coming.\\n50 Super series began to leak as early as April 2025, and then in November 2025 it was claimed to be delayed to Q3 2026. So this has been kicked around for a long time.\\nNvidia can use 50 Super to tide buyers over, so they don't have to release a 60 series (probably Rubin-based on 3nm) for consumers anytime soon. It looks like a relatively small upgrade, but the increased TDPs could boost performance by 5-10%. The 5070 gets ~4% more cores, and 18 GB, which is a nice amount and makes it unambiguously better than the 5060 Ti 16 GB. If Nvidia does release a 5060 12 GB, that card's VRAM problems are fixed.\\nPricing could sour the deal, but it looks good on paper in these trying times.\\nI think an RTX 5050 9 GB would have been fascinating to see, but it's reportedly cancelled. Oh well.\\nWhat the heck is \\\"Total Graphics Power?\\\" Is it total power used by the card while rendering graphics?\\nIt looks more like Max Card Power Consumption.\\nThose two things are the same with one just sounding a bit nicer than the other.\"}\n{\"article\": 32, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"wccftech.com\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER Features A 415W TDP, 15% Higher Than RTX 5080, According to Seasonic\", \"text\": \"NVIDIA's GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER graphics card reportedly features a 415W TDP, which is slightly higher than its Non-Super sibling.\\nNVIDIA Goes 50% More Memory, Higher Bandwidth At The Cost of More Power For Its GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER Graphics Card\\nThe NVIDIA GeForce RTX 50 SUPER series has remained in limbo ever since the first specifications were disclosed last year. This is due to several factors, such as prioritization of AI GPUs over the standard consumer chips, shortages in the DRAM segment, and a general rise in component prices that have led to a sharp decline in both OEM and DIY PC businesses.\\nFor the past few months, the NVIDIA RTX 50 \\\"SUPER\\\" series was said to be essentially put on hold or outright cancelled, but there came a glimmer of hope from a well-known insider who said that the Super family is back on track .\\nNow further hints are landing from a PSU manufacturer who has silently added the GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER to its wattage calculator . The last update from Seasonic came last year when it added RTX 5070 Ti SUPER and RTX 5070 SUPER to the same list. Videocardz managed to find this new entry & it looks like there are signals that NVIDIA is going ahead with the lineup instead of canceling it.\\nAs per the listing, the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER has a rated TDP of 415W. That's around 15 percent higher than the RTX 5080, which is rated at 360W. The higher TDP may come from higher clocks, which is expected to be the case for the memory, which has also increased in density. The RTX 4080 SUPER also saw a 10% lift in TDP, but that was due to a higher GPU spec rather than the memory configuration update.\\nWe also checked if the RTX 5070 Ti and RTX 5070 wattages have been updated, but they remain the same at 350W and 300W, respectively.\\nNVIDIA RTX 5080 SUPER Specs at a Glance\\nSo, coming to the memory specifications, it is stated that the NVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER will feature 24 GB of VRAM across a 256-bit bus while running at 32 Gbps, a 2 Gbps bump over the 30 Gbps used by the non-SUPER variant. These will be the latest 3 GB GDDR7 modules, and this gives us the full 1 TB/s of bandwidth, which we had hinted at a long time ago .\\nRTX 5080 SUPER vs RTX 5080 Non-SUPER:\\nSame Cores but Different GB203 SKU (450 vs 400)\\n24 GB VRAM vs 16 GB VRAM (+50%)\\nSame 256-bit Bus Interface\\n6.6% Higher GDDR7 speeds (32 Gbps vs 30 Gbps)\\n6.6% More Bandwidth (1024 GB/s vs 960 GB/s)\\n15% Higher TBP (415W vs 360W)\\nCurrently, there's no word on when NVIDIA will launch the RTX 50 SUPER series, and AIBs haven't started talking about these cards yet, not even at the Computex show floor, but it looks like we will get updates in the second half of this year, with a launch possibly taking place in late 2026 or at CES 2027.\\nNVIDIA RTX 50 SUPER GPU Lineup (Preliminary):\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080 SUPER\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5080\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Ti SUPER\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 Ti\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070 SUPER\\nNVIDIA GeForce RTX 5070\"}\n{\"article\": 33, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Corp. (NVDA) Announces a Profit-Sharing Program For Its Computing Framework Access\", \"text\": \"NVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ: NVDA ) is one of the top 10 AI stocks that will skyrocket .\\nOn July 1, Nvidia Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) disclosed a profit-sharing program developed to give model builders, AI startups, and businesses access to Nvidia's computing framework, without the need for these entities to fund hardware buildouts. Under this arrangement, NVIDIA, in addition to product revenue, will receive a share of cloud revenue based on the capacity it supports, as AI cloud providers deliver NVIDIA-powered solutions to their end users.\\nIn a blog post, the company's Executive Vice President and Chief Financial Officer, Colette Kress, defined income as a repeated, usage-based revenue stream, while noting that it eliminates the need for location selection, building, energy procurement, and hardware installation before accessing hyperscale computing.\\nNvidia said that this plan highlights a transition in AI demand from model building towards production inference tasks, needing continuous running of infrastructure at scale. The company also cited that access to high-cost computing resources was restricted historically, even with long-duration commitments.\\nThe move comes as the company faces rising competition from specialized AI chips from cloud providers, including Amazon and Alphabet, which are extending access to in-house processors. Nvidia stock dropped 0.5% in premarket trading on July 2. According to the company statement, the model also intensifies the engagement as compared to ownership stakes in next-generation cloud firms like Nebius and CoreWeave.\\nNVIDIA Corp. (NASDAQ:NVDA) is a computing infrastructure company that has transitioned from PC graphics chips towards full-scale compute and networking solutions. It has now become a leading player within the AI and high-performance computing space. The company offers Data Center accelerated computing and networking platforms, along with automotive platforms and electric vehicle solutions.\"}\n{\"article\": 34, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Can Micron Technology Become the New Nvidia?\", \"text\": \"Micron Technology ( MU +4.26% ) has had a phenomenal 2026 so far, rising around 240%, though it was up around 300% until a few days ago before artificial intelligence (AI)-centric stocks started to sell off. Regardless, Micron has had a great run, and it isn't looking to slow down anytime soon.\\nMicron recently announced another jaw-dropping quarter with huge growth and incredible expectations for the following quarter, dropping huge news that tight market conditions could last until 2028. If that occurs, Micron's stock could be primed for a major run, potentially positioning it to become the next Nvidia ( NVDA +2.56% ) .\\nBut is that possible? Let's see what it would take.\\nNvidia will be tough to catch, but Micron is trying its hardest\\nMicron fabricates both NAND and DRAM memory. NAND memory is used in long-term storage devices such as solid-state drives (SSDs). In contrast, DRAM is used for high-speed memory, as needed by Nvidia's GPUs to rapidly access information as it's processed.\\nThere has been strong demand for both types of memory due to the massive data center build-out, but supply hasn't kept up with this unprecedented demand. As a result, prices for memory chips have skyrocketed, allowing Micron to profit from the shortage.\\nAs mentioned, new capacity for many in this industry won't be online until 2027 or later, creating a low supply of inventory for at least the next year and a half. That could lead to even higher memory chip prices, as demand for these devices isn't slowing down.\\nNvidia has a strong read on data center build-out plans, as many clients are placing orders for devices well in advance of when they need them so they can quickly get them online once the data center infrastructure is ready. While the AI hyperscalers plan to spend $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year, next year that figure could be over $1 trillion. That will lead to even higher demand for memory chips, as well as for Nvidia GPUs.\\nIn Micron's latest quarter, DRAM accounted for around 76% of total sales, with NAND accounting for the rest. So, for Micron to surpass Nvidia, its NAND sales would need to rise much faster than DRAM, since DRAM will likely grow at a similar rate to GPU demand.\\nI don't see that happening, but that doesn't mean Micron won't be a great stock to own. Next quarter, it expects $50 billion in revenue, or about half of what Nvidia is expected to generate. Those are still impressive figures, and I won't be surprised to see Micron keep climbing the ladder toward Nvidia. However, it will likely never catch up, as Nvidia benefits from similar tailwinds to Micron.\\nBut how close can it get?\\nMicron could be a much larger company by this time next year\\nBecause the memory chip market is cyclical, it is often difficult to assign it a regular valuation. This plays into the analysis, as Micron would fare far better if the market assigned it a normal premium. So, let's analyze Micron's stock in two ways. I'll assign it a 15x earnings premium and a 25x earnings premium to see what the range of outcomes could be.\\nMicron operates on a non-standard fiscal year calendar, and its FY 2026 ends in August. So, I'll utilize FY 2027 projections. Wall Street analysts project earnings per share (EPS) of $149.64 next year, with the highest estimate at $221.27. At the midpoint, Micron's stock would trade between $2,244 and $3,741 per share, depending on whether it trades at 15 to 25 times earnings.\\nThat equates to a market capitalization of about $2.5 trillion to $4.2 trillion -- not far off from Nvidia's current $4.7 trillion. Now, if the earnings come in at the high end of the projection and the stock trades for 25 times earnings, Micron's stock could be valued at $6.2 trillion -- a greater figure than Nvidia.\\nHowever, this won't happen in a vacuum because Nvidia's stock will likely rise if Micron's does. I don't think Micron can catch Nvidia, but it still a great company to invest in .\\nBy Keithen Drury – Jul 7, 2026 at 9:30AM EST\"}\n{\"article\": 35, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 17% From Its High. Is the Artificial Intelligence (AI) Leader Finally Cheap?\", \"text\": \"The largest company in the world, Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA), has had a bit of a rough stretch in recent months. Its stock peaked in May, but declined around 17% since then. That's not an insignificant decline, and leaves investors wondering when the next rally could be coming.\\nI think there are some catalysts later on in July that could cause Nvidia's stock to rocket back to all-time highs, and investors would be smart to load up on shares before the rally occurs.\\nThe next month will prove the AI build-out is as strong as ever\\nNvidia makes graphics processing units (GPUs) along with various products to support its accelerated computing ecosystem. GPUs are still the top choice for running AI workloads, and Nvidia dominates the market share of GPUs sold for data center computing. There's a very strong correlation between increasing data center spending and Nvidia's sales, and that trend will likely accelerate as data centers shift from construction costs to computing costs.\\nThis year, the big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures . Next year, Nvidia claims this group will spend more than $1 trillion.\\nThat's a major increase and should boost Nvidia's revenue along the way. In July, all of the big four AI hyperscalers report earnings, which will likely include commentary surrounding AI spending and how these companies are monetizing AI. I'll be paying attention to all of them, but the biggest one I'll be watching is Microsoft (NASDAQ: MSFT), as its fiscal year ended on June 30. Microsoft will provide investors with fiscal year (FY) 2027 capital expenditures guidance, and a huge jump over last year's figure could confirm the bull case behind Nvidia's stock. That could help propel it back to all-time highs, which is why I think Nvidia is a strong buy now.\\nAnother key announcement will come from Taiwan Semiconductor Manufacturing Company (NYSE: TSM), Nvidia's primary logic chip fabricator. If TSMC reports strong growth from AI semiconductor sales, it will be easy to draw a conclusion that Nvidia is also doing well.\\nI think both of these companies will report strong earnings with great forward guidance, and that could cause Nvidia's stock to rise. Even if it doesn't, Nvidia reports earnings in late August and will likely report another blowout quarter, as there hasn't been any shift in actual spending habits from Nvidia's core clients yet.\\nAs for a price tag, Nvidia trades for just 21.7 times forward earnings -- the same price tag as the S&P 500 (SNPINDEX: ^GSPC).\"}\n{\"article\": 36, \"day\": \"2026-07-08\", \"publisher\": \"straitstimes.com\", \"title\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\", \"text\": \"China to let AI firms buy Nvidia H200s, says report\\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models.\\nChina will allow top AI companies like Alibaba and ByteDance to buy limited amounts of Nvidia's H200 AI chips, easing previous restrictions.\\nChinese firms must request approval specifying the number of chips and reasons for purchase, with a possible total below 200,000.\\nThe move responds to high AI demand but aims to protect China's chip industry and address cybersecurity concerns amid US-China tech tensions.\\nChina plans to allow top artificial intelligence companies to buy a limited amount of H200 chips from Nvidia, a sign the country is easing restrictions on the coveted US technology, according to the Information news site.\\nChinese officials have informed companies such as Alibaba Group, ByteDance and DeepSeek that they will have permission to purchase some of the processors, which are used to develop AI models, the news site said on July 8 .\\nThe companies need to say how many chips they need – and why – to get approval, according to the Information.\\nThe chips have emerged as a geopolitical flashpoint for the world’s two largest economies.\\nThough US President Donald Trump granted Nvidia permission to sell the processors to China in December, the Chinese authorities have been slow to allow the technology into the country.\\nNvidia shares climbed 3.7 per cent to US$204.12 (S$264) on July 8 , part of a broader chip rally. It marked the stock’s biggest one-day rise in more than a month.\\nThough Nvidia is now up 9.4 per cent in 2026 , that pales in comparison with the gains of most major semiconductor peers.\\nIn a statement, Chinese embassy spokesperson Liu Chang said that the country’s position on US chip exports has been consistent.\\n“We advocate that China and the United States achieve mutual benefit and win-win outcomes through cooperation, and we oppose the politicisation, instrumentalisation, and weaponisation of technological and economic issues,” he said. “We stand ready to work with all parties to jointly safeguard the stability of global industrial and supply chains.”\\nA representative for Santa Clara, California-based Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment.\\nThe H200 import snags hinged in part on Chinese concerns that a flood of American-designed AI processors would hinder the government’s long-held goal of developing an indigenous chip industry. Beijing has also cited fears that allowing American-made chips into the local market could pose a cybersecurity risk.\\nBut soaring demand for AI processors helped spur the Chinese authorities to allow some H200 purchases, according to the Information, citing two unidentified people familiar with the matter.\\nLabs in China have been struggling, like their US counterparts, to find enough computing power to meet their growing needs to train and run new models.\\nNvidia’s H200 hails from the company’s Hopper line of processors used for training and running AI models. It was the most powerful AI chip on the market until the introduction of the company’s next-generation Blackwell models in late 2024.\\nNvidia is now working to release an even more powerful line-up called Rubin in the second half of 2026 . Washington has continued to restrict sales of more capable AI chips to China on national security grounds.\\nThe Chinese government, meanwhile, is still figuring out how many H200 chips to allow the companies to buy, but the total may end up being fewer than 200,000, the Information reported. When it comes to AI infrastructure, that is not a large number. A single data centre can use more than 400,000 Blackwell processors.\\nIn May, Nvidia chief financial officer Colette Kress said the H200 chips had yet to generate any revenue for the company in China.\\n“We are uncertain whether any imports will be allowed into the country,” she said.\"}\n{\"article\": 37, \"day\": \"2026-07-09\", \"publisher\": \"technologyreview.com\", \"title\": \"The Download: a nuclear landmark, and China eyes Nvidia chips\", \"text\": \"The Download: a nuclear landmark, and China eyes Nvidia chips\\nPlus: NATO is building a network to stop Russian attackers in their tracks.\\nFour nuclear reactors hit a big milestone in the US\\nI was really looking forward to July 4, and not just because I love a poolside barbecue. This year the American holiday also marked a big symbolic deadline for US nuclear power.\\nLast year the Trump administration set a goal to see three new microreactors achieve criticality, a technical milestone establishing that a reactor can sustain a chain reaction, by the nation’s 250th birthday. And just in time, not just three, but four reactors did so.\\nIt’s a positive sign for nuclear technologies at a time of increasing need for electricity and emissions-free energy sources. But achieving criticality doesn’t mean a reactor is ready to provide electricity for the grid (or at all, for that matter).\\nI’ve combed the internet to find you today’s most fun/important/scary/fascinating stories about technology.\\n1 China plans to let its top AI firms buy Nvidia H200 chips\\nAlibaba, ByteDance, and DeepSeek are set to get permission. ( Information $)\\n+ China had previously withheld approval despite US authorization. ( Reuters $)\\n2 NATO is building a network to stop Russian attackers in their tracks\\nIt will use sensors, drones, satellites, and AI to detect them. ( Business Insider )\\n+ Troops are donning odd camouflage to elude drones. ( Economist $)\\n+ The US wants cheaper drones as Iran's wrecking its Reapers. ( Ars Technica )\\n3 Researchers have a new idea to fight future El Niños: dimming the sun\\nDeflecting solar energy could cool the ocean and mitigate the risks. ( Wired $)\\n+ But there could be unexpected consequences. ( New Scientist $)\\n+ And geoengineering as a field is getting a reality check. ( MIT Technology Review )\\n4 Meta is patenting an AI device that records users to analyse emotions\\nIt ostensibly aims to tailor workout plans to the user’s mood. ( 404 Media )\\n+ AI memory is privacy’s next frontier. ( MIT Technology Review )\\n5 Chipmakers are going vertical as Moore’s Law slows\\nThey’re stacking transistors to keep chips advancing. ( Economist $)\\n+ IBM is betting on the technique. ( MIT Technology Review )\\n6 Ivy League students suspected of AI cheating saw scores fall in person\\nFrom 96% all the way down to 48%. ( Ars Technica )\\n+ AI giants want to take over the classroom. ( MIT Technology Review )\\n7 A new study says parents’ phone addictions damage bonds with kids\\nIt can exacerbate “insecure attachment” for life. ( Bloomberg $)\\n+ And make children more anxious and avoidant. ( Gizmodo )\\n8 A judge approved Musk’s $1.5 million Twitter settlement with the SEC\\nDespite what she called “serious misgivings” and “red flags.” ( Reuters $)\\n+ Musk was accused of skirting stock disclosure rules. ( Fortune )\\n9 Shoebox-sized “detector satellites” could find nuclear bombs in space\\nCubesats carrying the detector could sense a bomb’s radiation. ( Space )\\n+ Russia is suspected of developing space-based nukes. ( Reuters $)\\n10 A World Cup match drove Google Search traffic to a new record\\nThe milestone came after Argentina’s comeback against Egypt. ( CNBC )\\n“I talk about it on Tic Tac.”\\n—President Donald Trump tells the public where to find his insights on the dangers of communism, Gizmodo reports.\\nRobots are bringing new life to extinct species\\nPaleontologists aren’t easily deterred by evolutionary dead ends or a sparse fossil record. And in the last few years, they’ve developed a new trick for turning back time and studying prehistoric animals: building experimental robotic models of them.\\nIn the absence of a living specimen, an ambling, flying, swimming, or slithering automaton is the next best thing for studying the behavior of extinct organisms. Learning more about how they moved can in turn shed light on their lives, such as their historic ranges and feeding habits. Scientists can simply sit back and observe their behavior in different environments.\\nRead the full story on the rise of paleo-inspired robots—and four examples that are shedding light on creatures of yore .\\nA place for comfort, fun, and distraction to brighten up your day. (Got any ideas? Drop me a line .)\\n+ Georgia Hill’s monochrome artworks are filled with visual harmony.\\n+ AI has salvaged text from a papyrus scroll burned to a crisp when Mount Vesuvius erupted 2,000 years ago.\\n+ Rare images taken by a Japanese space probe show a near-Earth asteroid resembling a cuddly snowman .\\n+ “Another One Bites the Bee Gees” smoothly merges two classic tracks with a 4/4 time signature into the perfect song for applying CPR .\\nPlus: 22 nations have called for a new global body to oversee AI.\\nThe Download: inside OpenAI’s Hugging Face hack, and a new EV takes on the US\\nPlus: Meta will pay up to $18 billion to settle a landmark child-safety case.\\nThe Download: weight-loss drugs slowing aging and carbon dioxide batteries\\nPlus: OpenAI has released 377 new math findings, further rattling the field.\\nThe Download: 10 climate tech companies to watch\\nPlus: Musk’s new Pentagon role has raised conflict-of-interest fears.\\nupcoming events, and more.\\nFounded at the Massachusetts Institute of Technology in 1899, MIT Technology Review is a world-renowned, independent media company whose insight, analysis, reviews, interviews and live events explain the newest technologies and their commercial, social and political impact.\\nElevate your brand to the forefront of conversation around emerging technologies that are radically transforming business. From event sponsorships to custom content to visually arresting video storytelling, advertising with MIT Technology Review creates opportunities for your brand to resonate with an unmatched audience of technology and business elite.\"}\n{\"article\": 38, \"day\": \"2026-07-09\", \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\", \"title\": \"DeepSeek Just Dealt Nvidia Stock a Potentially Major Blow\", \"text\": \"DeepSeek has already rattled Nvidia Corporation (NVDA) investors once with its low-cost artificial intelligence (AI) models. Now, the Chinese startup may be posing a far more direct competitive threat. Reports that DeepSeek is developing its own inference-focused AI chip could reduce its reliance on Nvidia's GPUs, highlighting a broader industry trend in which leading AI developers are increasingly designing custom silicon to lower costs, improve efficiency, and gain greater control over their AI infrastructure.\\nThe project is still in its early stages, with the company engaging chip design, foundry, and memory partners while quietly expanding its chip engineering team, but the implications could be significant.\\nSpaceX Has Massive Multiyear Put Options Volume As SPCX Falls Below IPO Price\\nJeff Bezos Says 'We Don't Have a Revenue Problem' in America — Bottom Half Paying Just 3% of Taxes Means 'We Can Find 3%'\\nIntel Stock Is 'Too Good to Ignore' as HSBC Sets a New Street-High Price Target\\nNvidia has built its AI dominance on supplying the computing power behind the world's largest AI models, but the rise of custom chips from major AI players raises questions about the durability of that business model over the long term. Although experts note that DeepSeek's move is unlikely to materially impact Nvidia's near-term revenue, particularly given China's shrinking contribution to Nvidia's sales, it reinforces concerns that custom silicon could gradually erode the company's pricing power and margins.\\nNow it remains to be seen whether this represents an isolated response to U.S. export restrictions or another sign that the AI industry is entering a new phase where more customers choose to build rather than buy their most critical AI hardware.\\nNvidia Corporation is a global leader in accelerated computing and AI, renowned for pioneering the GPU that revolutionized gaming, data centers, and AI-driven computing. Headquartered in Santa Clara, California, Nvidia's technology now powers everything from high-performance gaming and cloud computing to autonomous vehicles and generative AI applications. With a market cap of $4.8 trillion, Nvidia stands among the world's most valuable companies, driven by its dominance in AI infrastructure and continued innovation in next-generation chip design.\\nNvidia has been one of the market's standout long-term performers, evolving from a gaming GPU manufacturer into the world's leading provider of AI infrastructure. Surging demand for AI accelerators and data center chips has fueled a remarkable 984.4% gain in the stock over the past five years, far outpacing the broader market and helping Nvidia become one of the world's most valuable companies. Its dominant position in AI chips and deep partnerships with hyperscale cloud providers have reinforced investor confidence in its long-term growth prospects.\\nHowever, that momentum has eased in recent months. Despite continuing to deliver strong operating performance, the stock is up just 9.45% year-to-date (YTD) and 27.57% over the past 52 weeks, lagging the broader technology sector.\\nThe stock is currently trading 13.7% below its 52-week high of $236.54 reached on May 14. Investor sentiment has been pressured by concerns over the sustainability of AI infrastructure spending, intensifying competition, U.S. export restrictions, and uncertainty surrounding the rollout of next-generation AI platforms, including Rubin and Kyber.\\nRecently, Nvidia faced reports that suggested its next-generation Kyber rack-scale AI infrastructure could be delayed until 2028. However, the company denied the speculation, maintaining that its roadmap remains on track. Designed for the Rubin Ultra platform, Kyber is expected to launch in 2027 as an integrated AI infrastructure system and a key component of Nvidia's long-term AI infrastructure strategy.\\nNvidia trades at 22.49 times forward earnings, which is currently a discount compared to industry peers and the historical average.\\nNvidia delivered another exceptional quarter when it reported first-quarter fiscal 2027 results on May 20, further cementing its leadership in the rapidly growing AI infrastructure market.\\nFor the quarter ended April 26, Nvidia posted record revenue of $81.6 billion, an 85% year-over-year (YOY) increase, while net income jumped 211% YOY to $58.32 billion. On a non-GAAP basis, earnings per share (EPS) rose 140% from the prior-year period to $1.87, beating analyst estimates. Non-GAAP gross margin expanded to 75% from 60.8% a year earlier, underscoring the company's strong pricing power and favorable AI product mix.\\nMoreover, the Data Center segment remained Nvidia's biggest growth driver, with revenue surging 92% YOY to a record $75.2 billion. Data Center networking revenue soared 199% YOY to $14.8 billion, while computing revenue accounted for the remaining $60.4 billion.\\nIts Edge Computing revenue increased 29% YOY to $6.4 billion, supported by demand across gaming GPUs, autonomous driving, robotics, and AI-enabled edge devices.\\nManagement also pointed to accelerating adoption of Blackwell systems and highlighted expanding opportunities in agentic AI and enterprise AI infrastructure.\\nAdditionally, Nvidia issued another optimistic outlook for the second quarter of fiscal 2027, forecasting revenue of $91 billion, plus or minus 2%, and a non-GAAP gross margin of about 75%. The guidance assumes no contribution from China Data Center compute revenue due to ongoing U.S. export restrictions.\\nStreet expects Nvidia's momentum to continue, with analysts forecasting EPS growth of 90.2% YOY to $8.69 in fiscal 2027, followed by another 34.3% increase to $11.67 in fiscal 2028.\\nLast month, Morgan Stanley reaffirmed its \\\"Overweight\\\" rating on the stock and maintained its $288 price target, reiterating Nvidia as its top pick in the processor space.\\nPlus, Truist Securities reiterated its \\\"Buy\\\" rating and $307 price target after Nvidia unveiled several next-generation AI products at GTC Taipei.\\nOverall, NVDA has a consensus \\\"Strong Buy\\\" rating. Of the 49 analysts covering the stock, 43 advise a \\\"Strong Buy,\\\" three suggest a \\\"Moderate Buy,\\\" two analysts give it a \\\"Hold\\\" rating, and one offers a \\\"Strong Sell\\\" rating.\\nThe average analyst price target for NVDA is $301.92, indicating a potential upside of 47.9%. Also, the Street-high target price of $500 suggests that the stock could rally as much as 145%.\"}\n{\"article\": 39, \"day\": \"2026-07-10\", \"publisher\": \"tikr.com\", \"title\": \"TD Cowen Names Nvidia Stock a Top Pick on Massive Unpriced AI Upside\", \"text\": \"Price change for Nvidia stock in last 1 year: 24%\\n$NVDA Stock Price as of Jul. 9: $203\\n52-Week High: $236\\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\\nTD Cowen reiterated its Buy rating on Nvidia ( NVDA ) stock and named it a Top Pick, maintaining a $275 price target implying roughly 35% upside from where shares closed.\\nThe call came after the firm hosted an investor lunch with Nvidia’s leadership team, including CEO Jensen Huang, CFO Colette Kress, and Head of Investor Relations Toshiya Hari.\\nAnalyst Joshua Buchalter said Nvidia’s message to investors is clear: computing power remains in short supply, and the market is thinking about Nvidia’s technology far too narrowly.\\nHe pointed out that Nvidia’s hardware and software work together, or “co-design,” in a way that gives the company a real edge as AI keeps evolving from general chatbot-style applications toward more specialized, industry-specific tools.\\nTo put some numbers behind that, Nvidia estimates about 20% of its business comes from supporting frontier AI models built by companies like OpenAI and Anthropic.\\nMeanwhile, TD Cowen estimates that Nvidia’s revenue from enterprise applications across multiple industries is in the low-to-mid-teens range.\\nThat’s a meaningful chunk of business coming from outside the flashy AI chatbot headlines, which is part of Buchalter’s point about Nvidia’s opportunity being underappreciated.\\nTD Cowen also highlighted evidence that computing capacity is genuinely tight right now. Rental prices for older GPU models have been climbing, AI adoption continues to widen across industries, and recent cloud computing deals have been signed at premium prices.\\nAll of that points to demand outpacing supply.\\nNvidia’s own recent quarterly results back up the strength story.\\nThe company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, marking its third straight quarter of accelerating growth.\\nData center revenue alone hit $75 billion, up 92% year over year.\\nNvidia also highlighted a new growth driver: its Vera CPU line, which the company expects to generate $20 billion in sales by the end of this fiscal year, a notable push into a market historically dominated by Intel and AMD.\\nBeyond the CPU expansion, Nvidia has been striking new partnerships, including a deal with Valar Atomics to build a nuclear-powered data center in Utah aimed at reducing water use for AI computing.\\nAI startup Perplexity has also chosen to use Nvidia’s new CPUs for AI agent tasks.\\nSee analysts’ growth forecasts and price targets for Nvidia stock (It’s free) >>>\\nWhat the Market Is Telling Us About Nvidia Stock\\nTD Cowen’s bullish stance reflects growing confidence that AI spending is durable, not a temporary spike, and that it’s spreading across more customers and use cases than investors currently give Nvidia credit for.\\nSome valuation analyses even suggest that Nvidia stock remains undervalued relative to its fair value.\\nThat said, not everyone shares this optimism.\\nInvestor Michael Burry has taken a short position against Nvidia stock, citing valuation concerns tied to overall AI infrastructure spending.\\nComments from Meta’s Mark Zuckerberg about slower-than-expected progress in AI agents have also added caution to the broader AI narrative.\\nStill, firms like Bank of America view the recent semiconductor sector pullback as a temporary adjustment rather than a sign that AI demand is fading, keeping names like Nvidia stock in focus for investors watching this space closely.\\nEstimate a company’s fair value instantly (Free with TIKR) >>>\\nHow Much Upside Does Nvidia Stock Have From Here?\\nWith TIKR’s new Valuation Model tool , you can estimate a stock’s potential share price in under a minute.\\nAll it takes is three simple inputs:\\nIf you’re not sure what to enter, TIKR automatically fills in each input using analysts’ consensus estimates, giving you a quick, reliable starting point.\\nFrom there, TIKR calculates the potential share price and total returns under Bull, Base, and Bear scenarios so you can quickly see whether a stock looks undervalued or overvalued .\\nSee a stock’s true value in under 60 seconds (Free with TIKR) >>>\\nIBM stock crashed 23%, its worst day since 1987, after Q2 earnings and revenue missed estimates on a capex spending shift.\"}\n{\"article\": 40, \"day\": \"2026-07-14\", \"publisher\": \"tomshardware.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\", \"text\": \"Nvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington | Tom's Hardware\\nNvidia slashes list of authorized customers in Asia in a bid to reduce AI chip smuggling, report claims — company sent field inspectors, called customers to check if business is genuine after pressure from Washington\\nBy Jowi Morales\\nIt's now harder to buy Nvidia AI chips in China.\\nAI tech giant Nvidia, which builds some of the most coveted AI chips in the world, has reportedly created a new “whitelist” of verified companies to help prevent its products from getting smuggled into China. According to the Financial Times , this roster cuts the number of authorized clients by more than half, with those remaining having passed tougher compliance inspections to ensure that they are genuine businesses, not shell companies designed to forward Nvidia GPUs and servers into China. Some of the steps that Nvidia took to help safeguard its chips reportedly included sending staff to customer data centers, contract verification, and interviewing end users.\\nSources told the publication that the company made this move after Washington pressured it into tightening its legal compliance, which comes months after the arrest of Supermicro co-founder Yih-Shyan “Wally” Liaw , alongside two other suspects, for allegedly smuggling $2.5 billion worth of Nvidia hardware into China. This clampdown also extended into Singapore, which saw the seizure of a $42-million mansion tied to alleged AI GPU smugglers , and Taiwan, where authorities raided the offices of Supermicro and two supply-chain partners as part of a chip smuggling probe. Nvidia was not immediately available for comment on the news.\\nAlthough the U.S. has banned the latest AI GPUs for export into China since 2022, various investigations showed Chinese companies could still easily get their hands on these coveted chips until recently. Washington’s and its allies’ crackdown on AI GPU smuggling have cut supply in China, which is now making it harder for AI companies to procure the processors they need. President Donald Trump took a 180-degree turn in December 2025 and finally allowed Nvidia to export its H200 GPUs to select customers in the region, which would have alleviated the situation. However, Beijing refused to allow Chinese companies to buy these AI processors — instead, it’s banking on domestic semiconductor manufacturers to make up for the shortfall, but it’s apparently still not enough. One tech executive even told the Financial Times that all domestic suppliers are sold out and that they’re even considering less powerful chips, as long as they could be put to use.\\nAs Nvidia reportedly cleaned up its verified list of clients and made it harder for non-vetted companies to acquire its chips, the company has also told its partners to fix their export control compliance. “We insist our partners are compliant,” Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang told the media last May after Taiwan started its operations against AI chip smuggling into China . “We hope that they will enhance and improve their regulation compliance and prevent that from happening in the future.”\\nJensen: Mr Trump here's your very own gold plated leather jacket!\\nTrump: who needs a whitelist...\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_3","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if 11 <= x[\\\"article\\\"] <= 20]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"article\": 11, \"day\": \"2026-07-04\", \"publisher\": \"datacenters.economictimes.indiatimes.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\", \"text\": \"Nvidia introduces revenue-sharing model for AI cloud financing\\nNvidia has launched a revenue-sharing financing model aimed at AI cloud providers, facilitating access to its computing infrastructure. This initiative is designed to support startups and enterprises in scaling AI operations.\\nNvidia launches a revenue-sharing financing model for AI cloud providers.\\nThe model aims to facilitate access to large-scale AI compute for startups and enterprises.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are early adopters of the financing structure.\\nThe initiative reflects a shift in the AI market towards production inference.\\nNvidia has unveiled a revenue-sharing financing model to assist AI cloud providers in acquiring its computing infrastructure, thereby enhancing access to large-scale AI compute for startups, enterprises, researchers, and regional providers.\\nThis model is particularly relevant as the market transitions from model development to production inference, where computing systems operate continuously to deliver AI outputs at scale.\\nUnder this financing structure, AI cloud operators can procure Nvidia infrastructure with credit support, with revenue sharing linked to the cloud services sold based on that capacity.\\nNvidia will receive its standard product revenue from infrastructure sales, in addition to a share of the cloud revenue generated from the supported capacity. This approach offers AI cloud operators a viable means to finance large installations, which have traditionally been challenging to fund, especially for emerging companies lacking the balance sheet strength typically required for capital-intensive projects.\\nThe initiative reflects a significant shift in the AI market, where demand is increasingly focused not only on training large models but also on running them in production for enterprises, software providers, and AI-native businesses that require constant access to computing resources for inference, fine-tuning, and related workloads.\\nTwo companies are among the first to collaborate with Nvidia under this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is developing a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, which is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs. These projects exemplify the scale of infrastructure Nvidia aims to facilitate through this financing model, which is linked to future usage rather than relying solely on conventional long-term commitments.\\nNvidia describes these facilities as DSX AI factories tailored to customer demand in various regions. For users of AI infrastructure, this model promises faster access to computing capacity, eliminating delays associated with site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware installation typical of traditional buildouts.\\nAccess to compute has been a persistent constraint across the AI sector, with many emerging companies struggling to secure the necessary capital to lock in supply, even when demand is evident. Nvidia's strategy aims to alleviate this bottleneck by aligning its financial interests more closely with cloud providers serving these customers.\\nAs the AI landscape evolves, suppliers like Nvidia are adapting to the changing economics of AI services. With more companies transitioning from experimental phases to commercial products, there is a growing need for computing supply that can scale with usage while maintaining flexibility in payment structures during the shift from pilot projects to full deployment.\\nNvidia highlighted the needs of model builders, inference providers, and enterprises requiring immediate cloud access for various AI-related tasks. The company also pointed to AI-native businesses such as Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI as examples of where demand is heading.\\nThese entities depend on reliable access to accelerated computing as customer usage increases, often requiring more adaptable commercial arrangements than traditional data center buildouts or fixed infrastructure commitments allow.\\nFor Nvidia, this model not only creates a recurring income stream tied to the usage of supported cloud capacity but also deepens its involvement in the AI cloud market as competition intensifies among providers aiming to serve developers, digital-native companies, and established enterprises.\\nSharon AI's co-founder and CEO, James Manning, described the agreement as a milestone for the company's plans, stating, \\\"This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure.\\\"\\nFirmus co-CEO Tim Rosenfield emphasized the importance of infrastructure access for globally competitive AI businesses, noting, \\\"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally.\\\"\\nHe added that the Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory to enable more customers to access the compute necessary for building and scaling AI.\"}\n{\"article\": 12, \"day\": \"2026-07-04\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Nearly Flat for 2026. Time to Cash Out, or Load Up on Shares?\", \"text\": \"Nvidia (NASDAQ: NVDA) investors have had it good for several years. In 2023, 2024, and 2025, it produced market-crushing returns, making it one of the best stocks to own during each of those years. However, 2026 hasn't been that way. The stock is up 5% so far in 2026, which isn't nothing, but compared to the returns that Nvidia investors have enjoyed in recent years, it feels like an absolute failure. On another note, its market-crushing status is in jeopardy for 2026, as the S&P 500 is up nearly 10% for the year.\\nWith Nvidia stock looking like a dud this year, many investors are wondering whether now is the time to cash out and look for other investment opportunities, or to load up on Nvidia shares. Let's take a look, as not everything is as it appears with Nvidia.\\nNvidia was the top AI stock to own over the past three years, due to its market position. Nvidia designs graphics processing units (GPUs) and the hardware that supports them. Although it doesn't own any of the fabrication process itself, it works with several companies that manufacture its GPUs, which it then sells to a wide range of customers.\\nBefore 2023, Nvidia's business was more balanced. However, the massive demand for AI data centers has driven this lineup of GPUs into high demand, and Nvidia's product line has become the go-to for several AI hyperscalers and cloud providers. Nvidia has a complete ecosystem of products that gives its clients everything they need to run an Nvidia-powered data center, locking in clients once they've chosen to use its products.\\nThis bodes well for the future, as AI hyperscalers are spending a massive amount of money this year and beyond to continue building out their AI computing footprints. The big four AI hyperscalers plan to spend around $650 billion on data center capital expenditures this year. Next year, Nvidia projects that this figure could reach $1 trillion. That means more growth is coming to Nvidia, and the investing story is far from complete. Wall Street analysts project 82% revenue growth for this year and 41% next year, so there's another external source that's projecting even more growth for Nvidia.\\nHowever, the stock price doesn't reflect that.\\nNvidia trades at 21.7 times forward earnings, essentially the same price tag as the S&P 500. This conveys that the market believes Nvidia will be a market-average stock after 2026's growth is accounted for. However, Wall Street analysts don't believe that, as they're guiding for major growth next year. That's why the stock looks so cheap when next year's earnings are used to value the stock.\\nAs a result, I think Nvidia is an excellent stock to buy now. None of next year's growth has been priced in, which could lead to strong returns toward the end of this year.\"}\n{\"article\": 13, \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Stock Is Down 13% Over the Last Month. Here's Why That Could Be Good News.\", \"text\": \"It's never fun to see your investments lose money. And when a stock's price falls more than 10% in a month, it's normal to wonder if you should sell to lock in your gains before it falls any further.\\nThat's something investors in Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) need to seriously think about. Shares of the world's leading chipmaker dropped 12.6% between June 2 and July 2, and they're down 17% from May's all-time high of $235.74/share.\\nBut this latest drop might not be such a bad thing, actually. Here's why investors shouldn't panic and why Nvidia's pullback might actually be good news.\\nThere have always been ebbs and flows with Nvidia stock\\nObviously, a pullback in a company's stock price offers new investors the opportunity to buy the dip. That's not the kind of \\\"good news\\\" I'm talking about here, although it's true that now might be a good moment to open a new position in the chipmaker if you don't already own shares.\\nInstead, I'm talking about the natural ebbs and flows of Nvidia's stock price. The company's road to being the largest company in the world with a $4.7 trillion market cap hasn't been a smooth one. In just the last five years, the stock has seen dizzying plunges multiple times.\\nBetween November 2021 and October 2022, Nvidia's share price plunged 66%. It recovered a bit, only to drop by more than 20% again in December 2022. The company went on to experience nine drops of 15% or more between July 2023 and March 2026, about three per year.\\nBut over the last five years, the stock has risen by more than 851%. And if you had sold during any one of those previous 15% dips, you would have missed out on lots of gains .\\nThe biggest of those gains came suddenly and after a prolonged downturn. For example, between October 2025 and March 2026, Nvidia's stock lost 20% of its value over five months. Then, without warning, shares surged 42.7% between March 30 and May 14, hitting all-time highs.\\nWhen a stock rockets upward and never experiences temporary pullbacks like Nvidia's has over the last five years, it can make new investors reluctant to buy shares out of concern that the stock is too expensive. And as the world's largest company by market cap, Nvidia already faces skepticism about its valuation.\\nIt can also set the company up to experience a major share price drop if it reports anything less than stellar earnings. Nvidia doesn't seem to have that problem. In fact, it almost faces the opposite issue: Over the past two years, Nvidia's earnings reports have been stellar, with massive growth in revenue and net income. Yet after five of its last eight earnings reports, the stock has seen an immediate and significant share price decline .\\nIf recent history is any guide, Nvidia's recent share price drop is likely to reverse itself unexpectedly, and the stock should soar to new heights . Smart investors know better than to panic sell this longtime winner that's still at the top of its game.\\nBy John Bromels – Jul 5, 2026 at 2:15PM EST\"}\n{\"article\": 14, \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"publisher\": \"fool.com\", \"title\": \"AMD Stock and Intel Crushed Nvidia in the First Half. Here's My Prediction for the Second Half.\", \"text\": \"Nvidia ( NVDA +2.48% ) has been the \\\"go-to\\\" artificial intelligence (AI) stock for a number of years. Since this tech giant is the No. 1 designer of AI chips, it's generated mind-boggling revenue growth that's reached record levels and proven itself to be a winner in the AI boom. So it's no surprise that investors have turned to the stock, pushing it to a gain of about 900% over the past five years.\\nBut in the first half of this year, a shift took place. Investors rotated out of some of the biggest AI winners -- like Nvidia -- and picked up shares of AI stocks that hadn't yet gained as much. That movement helped fellow chip companies Advanced Micro Devices ( AMD +3.03% ) and Intel ( INTC +5.57% ) soar. Climbing 171% and 278%, respectively, in the first half, they crushed Nvidia. The AI chip giant advanced 7.2%, which is a pretty small gain for this stock.\\nWill AMD and Intel keep crushing Nvidia? Here's my prediction for the second half.\\nBefore we get to my prediction, though, let's catch up on how these companies have approached the AI opportunity so far. Nvidia was the first to market with graphics processing units (GPUs) tailored to the needs of AI, allowing the company to build out its leadership here. Intel and AMD have traditionally been leaders in another type of processor: the central processing unit (CPU), the main processor in computers.\\nIntel has led here for years and holds more than 59% of total CPU market share, though AMD has gained ground, progressing from a low of about 17% back in 2016 to 38% today.\\nThe GPU was the most relevant of the two chips during the early days of AI, driving the most important tasks such as the training of AI models. And that was fantastic news for Nvidia. AMD and Intel entered the GPU market later, and while AMD has successfully delivered growth here, Intel has experienced difficulty.\\nThat said, Intel aims to turn this around and has taken key steps. The company appointed new chief executive officer Lip-Bu Tan a little over a year ago to drive the company's turnaround strategy and strengthen its position in the AI market , and investors like the progress so far and the good news that's emerged. The U.S. government took a 10% stake in Intel last summer, worth about $10 billion -- a sign of confidence that investors appreciated. In the latest quarter, Intel's revenue climbed 7%, and this was the sixth straight quarter of revenue that beat the company's expectations.\\nSo, it's not surprising that AMD and Intel , which greatly lagged behind Nvidia from a stock performance perspective, saw their shares skyrocket in the first half of this year.\\nNow, here's my prediction for the second half. I predict that Nvidia will outpace these rivals. And this is for two reasons: Nvidia's next goal and its valuation. I'll start with the goal, and this is to dominate the $200 billion CPU market -- a market where Nvidia has not been greatly present in the past. In fact, it's launching its first-ever stand-alone CPU this fall as part of the Vera Rubin platform. And at the same time, Nvidia is targeting the personal computing market with a superchip -- also set for fall release -- that includes a GPU and a CPU.\\nWhile it may be difficult for Nvidia to take complete leadership of the entire CPU market, I think the company could clearly dominate in the CPU market for data centers. Nvidia already forecasts $20 billion in stand-alone CPU sales this year. It's important to note that the CPU is the key chip needed to power agentic AI -- and agentic AI is expected to be the next AI growth driver. This involves the application of AI to real-world problems, with the agent taking problem-solving steps.\\nWhile Nvidia's potential CPU market leadership won't happen overnight, a successful launch of the Rubin platform and high demand could lead to positive stock performance for Nvidia in the second half.\\nValuation could also push investors to favor Nvidia over AMD and Intel.\\nAt about 22x forward earnings estimates, Nvidia looks dirt cheap, while the two chip peers look excessively expensive after their recent gains. All of this prompts me to predict that Nvidia, which saw a pause in its stock market momentum in recent months, may soar in the second half -- and crush AMD and Intel.\\nBy Adria Cimino – Jul 5, 2026 at 6:10PM EST\"}\n{\"article\": 15, \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's next-gen AI rack system delayed to 2028 on manufacturing snags, SemiAnalysis says\", \"text\": \"Jensen Huang, chief executive officer of Nvidia Corp., speaks next to a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber compute tray and a Vera Rubin Ultra Kyber NVLink midplane during a keynote address at the Nvidia GTC conference in San Jose, California, March 16, 2026.\\nNvidia 's next marquee product — the Kyber rack-scale architecture designed to house its 2027 Rubin Ultra chips — has been delayed by more than 12 months to 2028, according to research firm SemiAnalysis, the latest in a string of reported setbacks raising questions about the AI giant's product roadmap.\\nNvidia rejected the SemiAnalysis report and said, \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nShares of the artificial intelligence chip giant climbed about 1% on Monday.\\nKyber is a server cabinet that packs 144 of Nvidia's most powerful chips into a single unit so they can work together as one giant computer, providing the horsepower AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design mounts graphics processing units in compute trays that sit vertically instead of horizontally to boost density and reduce latency, and had been slated to debut with Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia's next-generation rack-scale system, in 2027 .\\nThe setback stems from difficulties manufacturing a key circuit board at the heart of the system, SemiAnalysis said in a post on Monday.\\n\\\"Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,\\\" the firm said, referring to a specialized, multi-layer printed circuit board that connects electronic modules within a system.\\nNVL576 — a larger system linking eight racks via optical connections — is also likely delayed or limited to small volumes, the research firm said.\\nThe reported delay adds to mounting strains across Nvidia's product lines, underscoring concerns that Nvidia's breakneck annual release cadence is colliding with manufacturing limits.\\nA backup plan — bolting two of Nvidia's current-generation racks together for similar power — has also been scrapped after cloud customers rejected the design as awkward and costly to operate. \\\"It has since been cancelled due to heavy pushback from CSPs [cloud service providers] and hyperscalers over its odd design and heavy operational burden,\\\" SemiAnalysis said.\\nThat leaves Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" SemiAnalysis said, predicting that could give rivals Advanced Micro Devices and Google , whose in-house chips are already winning business from top AI labs, a rare technical opening at the high end of the market.\\nThe delays \\\"should not be over analyzed as affecting the long-term criticality of Nvidia to AI data infrastructure buildouts,\\\" said Paul Triolo, a partner at consultancy DGA-Albright Stonebridge Group, noting the company \\\"has faced these kinds of challenges before, and has worked with vendors to overcome technical issues.\\\"\\nWith power supply likely to remain the major constraint on American AI data center spending, Triolo said, \\\"delays in getting to more advanced systems could just mean that the new systems are ready by the time the U.S. can work to overcome some of the critical bottlenecks on power now dogging the industry.\\\"\\nNvidia's current-generation Rubin systems are in full production and begin shipping this fall to eight cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure and Google Cloud. SemiAnalysis also projects Nvidia's data-center compute revenue will run 20% above Wall Street consensus in the second half of fiscal 2027.\\nWhile Huawei and other domestic manufacturers \\\"will potentially gain some time,\\\" China's hardware ecosystem is increasingly diverging from — rather than chasing — the Nvidia-led model, Triolo said. \\\"The issue is no longer really catching up, but how good will China's alternative AI stack be by 2030,\\\" he said.\"}\n{\"article\": 16, \"day\": \"2026-07-05\", \"publisher\": \"barchart.com\", \"title\": \"Sentiment Could Be Turning Sour on Nvidia. Here’s Where 1 Analyst Thinks NVDA Stock Is Headed Next.\", \"text\": \"Perhaps the most important stock in the market, Nvidia (NVDA) remains a top stock I continue to watch closely.\\nThere are a few reasons for this. Of course, as the world's largest semiconductor company, the fact that Nvidia powers the world's economy (essentially) via propping up the artificial intelligence (AI) revolution is a massive deal. Indeed, the range of opinions on where NVDA stock could be headed from here varies greatly, and we'll get to that shortly.\\nDear Netflix Stock Fans, Mark Your Calendars for July 16\\nGoogle Just Launched 2 New AI Models. What That Means for GOOGL Stock.\\nThe Microsoft Stock Outlook Remains Upbeat Despite Layoffs\\nThat said, given the incredible volatility we've seen in Nvidia, which has held the title of the world's largest company for quite some time (see above), there's plenty to digest around this stock's recent moves. Let's dive into why one analyst thinks Nvidia could be headed much lower from here.\\nAnalysts at Seaport Research put forward a piece last week that was honestly pretty difficult to read, particularly for bulls. That's because many of the issues these analysts noted align with the idea that Nvidia's balance sheet has growing issues, and there could be plenty of turbulence underneath the surface for investors.\\nNow, that's not to say that Nvidia's underlying fundamentals don't look strong right now. They do. Looking at the summary above, Nvidia's forward price-earnings multiple of just 35 times is well below the average for the sector. That's important, considering the weighting Nvidia has in most indices (implying the rest of the stock market is, on average, more richly valued than the leader).\\nThat said, concerns around a \\\"circular-financing boom\\\" put forward by Seaport analysts in their note are one of the issues bears have contended could lead to issues around earnings quality down the line. The thinking is that the more Nvidia finances the companies that buy its chips (either directly via equity or debt, or simply allowing for financing of these increasingly expensive chips), that could mean that the demand investors are seeing isn't real. At least, not to the extent that Nvidia's earnings releases suggest.\\nI think there could be something to that. Indeed, the sort of circular activity we saw in the dot-com boom among some tech companies, or in the utility buildout surrounding the rise of the internet, did lead to disaster. And I'm not suggesting Seaport is completely buying into this idea, but I've read plenty from a range of analysts that have at least pointed out that circular financing in any time or sector isn't generally a positive when we look at the overall health of any major trend.\\nFrom here, I think data center construction, and the ability for Nvidia's clients (many of the largest tech companies) to continue to support massive construction plans, could be what investors will be watching more closely than Nvidia's earnings reports. That's what I think we're seeing in this recent selloff in NVDA stock, which is now down around 17% from its recent all-time high.\\nThe good news for investors in Nvidia who are willing to look past these concerns is that most analysts on Wall Street tend to have a similarly bullish stance on the world's leading chipmaker.\\nThe consensus price target for NVDA stock sits a little higher than $300 per share, implying more than 50% upside from here. Even more notable is the high target on the street of $500 per share, which suggests Nvidia could more than double over the course of the next year, if all goes well.\\nI'm not so sure that those bullish expectations will come to bear, at least not this year. Sentiment has weakened considerably, given the inflationary pressures on consumers and growing concerns around the business models of the companies that support the artificial intelligence revolution.\\nFor now, I'm not willing to put my own \\\"sell\\\" rating on Nvidia, but I'm cautious. To me, NVDA stock looks more like a hold right now than anything else.\"}\n{\"article\": 17, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"newsletter.semianalysis.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia GPU Debt Backstop Unleashes the AI Project Trinity: Capital, Offtake and Datacenters\", \"text\": \"Up until now the majority of AI buildouts have been primarily cashflow funded by the hyperscalers such as Google, Amazon, Meta, Microsoft, Oracle. Over the last year, that's started to turn with Oracle then Meta, and now even Google turning to debt. Nvidia revenue is skyrocketing, and even 3 years into the build out, the general market is still materially lower on shipment volumes and revenue estimates for Nvidia in the 2nd half of this year versus our through supply chain tracking in the Accelerator Model .\\nAI Debt Financing will become a multi-trillion-dollar credit market, with over $7T of debt outstanding by 2029 driven both by AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex needs for Neoclouds, datacenter builders, and even hyperscalers. This will make it the second largest asset backed debt market after the US mortgage-backed financing market at just over $13T.\\nAnnual AI Capex – including GPUs, networking, storage and attached CPU compute as well as for the Datacenters to house AI compute – will be well north of $2T in 2028. Cumulative AI Capex from 2024 to 2029 will reach ~$11.1T, and credit markets will be the main funding source for this buildout.\\nThe surge in borrowing requirements to date has driven by briskly growing demand from AI labs and hyperscalers, and the construction of AI clusters to service this demand was made financeable by long-term take-or-pay compute contracts backed by hyperscalers, with the most common offtake period being 5 years. Though this article will mainly focus on financing AI IT Capex - that is, GPUs and related capex like storage, networking and CPUs attached - there is much that also needs to be done to support the growth of datacenter capex financing. A much more detailed breakdown of both AI IT Capex and AI Datacenter Capex can be found in our AI TCO Model and AI Datacenter Model respectively.\\nExecuting on any AI Compute buildout requires assembling all three legs of what we call the AI Project Trinity – Capital, Offtake, Datacenter :\\nCapital: As of today, lenders require an offtake contract or a backstop from an investment grade hyperscaler before they will provide debt financing.\\nOfftake: To secure an offtake, you first need equity capital to demonstrate you can place deposits for the required IT equipment, but to raise equity – one needs to demonstrate that they have an offtaker and lenders in place!\\nDatacenter: Lastly, an aspiring Neocloud must either have a solid offtaker and lending lined up in order to convince datacenter operators to rent colocation to them, or the Neocloud must build a datacenter themselves.\\nYet this is far from an impossible trinity, and many Neoclouds have been executing on cluster build-outs. Deals have been happening, but they come together thanks to clever structuring of one or more legs of the Trinity, close sponsorship of or matchmaking by capital providers like private equity firms that have been serial providers of capital to Neoclouds and Datacenters, and of course, some good old-fashioned risk taking on the part of everyone involved.\\nOur forecasts for GPU shipments and datacenter capex for the next few years imply that the total outstanding AI debt financing needs will quickly surpass the size of all other US asset backed markets.\\nBut growing this debt market from hundreds of billions in 2024 and 2025 to ~$7.1T by 2029 is no mean feat – there are a number of significant obstacles that must be overcome for the debt market to reach this size and for the compute market to serve more than just hyperscalers and large AI labs:\\nHyperscaler backstops are not infinite: Hyperscaler balance sheets will not be able to backstop trillions of dollars’ worth of compute, yet outside of the four corners of a 5-year hyperscale backstopped compute deal, the appetite to lend drops off almost entirely. If the lending market does not evolve beyond this template, once hyperscalers exhaust their capacity to backstop deals, there will be no further projects to lend to.\\nLenders are still on the learning curve: Private credit and private equity have led the charge on lending to the first batch of large Neoclouds, but as spreads compress over time, and as capital needs increase, a broader set of lenders will need to be tapped, yet most banks still have a nascent understanding of AI Cluster total cost of ownership, the AI compute market as well as tokenomics and end demand and still hide behind the shield of an investment grade offtake or backstop.\\nCapital providers lack tools for pricing and managing risk : There are very few well-constructed price indices for GPU rental outside of our own SemiAnalysis GPU Rental Pricing Index . GPU rental transactions are all on a bilateral basis and not generally publicly available, and there is no active derivatives market to provide a pricing signal or a good reference for GPU residual value.\\nThe current Neocloud market structure has a few other issues. The most pressing problem to solve is broad-based access to compute for renters other than hyperscalers and large AI labs, as well as the limited supply of shorter-term rentals given that most lending is for the 5y backstop template.\\nFor example, VC-backed AI startups and inference providers may be cash rich, but they want large clusters on short term contracts so they can quickly get to their next round of funding and reload on compute. With most Neoclouds thus far preferring to stick with doing large 5y offtakes, these startups are forced to take on larger prepayment or longer contracts than they want, rent fewer GPUs than they need, end up using different GPUs than they would prefer, and often have to settle for start dates far into the future.\\nInference providers in particular are very contracted time sensitive compared to training focused AI Labs. While AI Labs are able to commit for longer periods like 3 years and beyond, inference providers are completely unwilling to sign for longer than 1y and would rather forego access to compute than take the risk of committing for any extended period of time.\\nWhen it comes to shorter-dated rentals for everyone else, it is still a seller’s market. At this point, we are only aware of a few Neoclouds that are still offering 1y rentals, and they are setting aggressive terms for rental – sometimes requiring prepays of up to 100% of the total contract value. Neoclouds have so much demand for their GPUs that they are able to solve for a prepay amount that can entirely fund the cluster capex, meaning a theoretically infinite IRR as they can stand up a cluster with no cash out the door on their part!\\nIn 2025, we wrote extensively about how datacenter capacity was the bottleneck for AI compute growth. By early 2026, the datacenter supply situation improved considerably, but it became clear that chip production was now the limiting constraint. Now – mid-way through the year – it is clear that financing will now be one of the most significant obstacles to ramping large scale compute broadly available to everyone.\\nThis is why Nvidia has stepped in and has started backstopping GPU rental offtakes themselves . In the backstop program, Nvidia provides a take-or-pay commitment to Neoclouds – a minimum revenue guarantee on the underlying GPU capacity. In exchange for this backstop, Nvidia also shares in a portion of the Neocloud’s revenue earned above the backstop level.\\nThe Neocloud is of course free to rent to any other customer they would like for any term length they deem commercially reasonable and indeed the intent is for the Neocloud to never actually have to invoke the backstop.\\nNvidia’s backstop program has a few key objectives:\\nTo broaden compute availability: This objective has two dimensions, to open up the compute market well beyond just a few large hyperscalers and AI labs and to ensure rental contracts of varying contract terms (tenor) are available and not just 5y terms – particularly short terms of less than 1 year,\\nSupport evolution of the GPU financing market: To ease lenders into funding Neoclouds with a varied book of clients and rental contract tenors by buying time for lenders to get up the learning curve and adopt tools to price and manage risk,\\nGrow Neoclouds: Provide early support so Neoclouds can grow rapidly and establish a track record and demonstrate the viability of their business model and client books so that they can become platforms that can be banked on attractive terms as well as take up datacenter obligations with greater ease. This broadens the base of buyers beyond just a few hyperscalers that will pit their own custom silicon solutions against Nvidia’s systems.\\nWith this backstop in hand – the Neocloud can much more easily assemble the AI Project Trinity:\\nCapital: Lenders look to Nvidia’s backstop and its AA/Aa2 investment grade credit rating and are satisfied to lend matching the length of the backstop. With a viable go to market plan and an Nvidia backstop, equity can be raised to fund deposits to secure equipment and the various payments needed to start developing the cluster.\\nOfftake: Having the backstop in hand and with funding for the cluster, Neoclouds can then tap previously unaddressed offtake demand outside of just the typical 5-year AI lab or hyperscale offtake.\\nDatacenter: However, even with the backstop in place, securing the final leg of the Trinity and obtaining datacenter capacity remains challenging absent a creative datacenter rental structure or resorting to a self-built datacenter. Here, Nvidia is diving even deeper, as it has started backstopping datacenter leases.\\nThe incremental revenue from the backstop program will be significant, but Nvidia stands to gain far more from these backstops than this additional revenue. They aim to do nothing less than entirely reshape the structure of the GPU market itself. We have already discussed how the TAM they can sell into will become bottlenecked very soon if a 5y hyperscaler backstopped offtake is the only viable deal structure.\\nIn January 2026 for institutional subscribers, we first discussed how Nvidia was becoming the Central Bank of AI . A central bank exists to supply liquidity when others in the banking system are unwilling to step in, supporting economic activity until others are ready to take over.\\nMost in the neocloud ecosystem are unable to raise enough debt for large GPU buildouts unless they lease to the big hyperscalers directly. Nvidia doesn't want the market to be the same handful of concentrated buyers. The small buyers want GPUs, can pay for them, but they can't offer credit ratings to creditors funding the build out. In mid-2026, Nvidia is clearly showing it stands ready to offer this support as the central bank.\\nNvidia’s backstop program is typically six years in length, and during these six years, Nvidia stands ready to purchase compute at pre-agreed price levels that vary over the time period. Each Neocloud is expected to negotiate the backstop terms individually, and we can expect that different Neoclouds may end up with different revenue share and backstop schedules. The below table is an illustrative example to explain the basic terms of the program – we present a backstop pricing curve that we believe to be on the lower end of the backstop range – in this case at an average of $2.36 over the six-year period, but we expect that most Neoclouds will negotiate higher backstops.\\nLet’s start by exploring Neocloud economics under the backstop program by considering a few different scenarios. In the first scenario, the Neocloud focuses on a customer base that rents at a 1-year or lower tenor, so we model the 1-year rental price for a GB300 starting at $6.75/hr for the first year before decaying over time as the overall market cost of compute declines. As with any business that is operating a “curve trade” – that is investing long and renting out on shorter tenors – we should expect that in theory the average realized rental price over the project lifetime should be slightly higher than the current 6y fixed price of ~$4.00. This is to compensate the Neocloud for taking on future price risk.\\nIn any given year, the Neocloud will earn 100% of the rental price up to the backstop amount, but in respect of the amount which the rental price exceeds the backstop, the Neocloud will share a portion of this revenue with Nvidia.\\nIn the table below, we show workings for this scenario. Let’s walk through an example of how to calculate the Neocloud’s revenue share. In the case where Nvidia earns 40% revenue share, we see that in year 1, the Neocloud will charge its customers $6.75/hr on average. Of this, the Neocloud earns the full amount up to Nvidia’s year 1 backstop of $3.68/hr, but for the difference between $6.75/hr charged to customers and the $3.68/hr backstop amounting to $3.07/hr, Nvidia will earn $1.23/hr and the Neocloud will earn $1.84/hr. In total, the Neocloud will realize $5.52/hr for the first year, which of course is lower than the $6.75/hr that they would have earned without the backstop. Over the six years of this scenario, the Nvidia take rate works out to be about 18% on average, but without the backstop, there would be no cluster!\\nOur second scenario analysis illustrates how the backstop math would work with a 6-year fixed price offtake. This scenario is admittedly an oxymoron – if you can get a 6-year fixed price offtake, then you of course do not need an Nvidia backstop and would thus would certainly not want to pay the revenue share out to Nvidia. Turning around and renting out compute on a 6-year contract would also be against the spirit of Nvidia’s backstop program as this deal would do nothing to further the objective of supplying compute for shorter tenors to a variety of different buyers, especially emerging workloads.\\nIt is important to highlight that while Nvidia does have meaningful internal compute needs, neither Nvidia or the Neoclouds have any intention of using the backstop, and thus it is not surprising that the backstop levels would result in a typical Neocloud earning a zero or slightly negative project IRR.\\nIn the table below, we illustrate the economics of renting to Nvidia under the backstop. This scenario would only materialize if the Neocloud is unable to find sufficient third-party customers at market-clearing rental and must activate the backstop.\\nThat said, this scenario is exactly why the structure is financeable. Though the IRRs earned under this scenario will turn out to be near zero or even slightly negative, the Neocloud will at least still be able to cover debt payments, making lenders comfortable with this structure. Loan underwriting for clusters with an Nvidia backstop will evaluate debt service coverage ratios in the scenario in which the backstop is triggered to determine how much funding can be provided.\\nThe table below summarizes the return profiles under the various scenarios discussed above as well as displaying the economics of a Neocloud renting at the same prices but without any backstop.\\nWe see that the short-term 1y rental book scenario clearly offers the best IRR among the backstopped scenarios at 25.4%, and these IRRs could realize even higher if the rental market turns out to be strong enough such that 1y and shorter rental rates decay by less than we modeled in the curve below. As expected, the IRRs are higher without a backstop – reaching as high as 40.7% when renting for 1y tenors with no backstop. But if Neoclouds activate the backstop and rent to Nvidia at those prices, they can expect to earn zero or negative IRRs.\\nMany lenders are already being sounded out on the Nvidia backstop structure. They use the typical hyperscale backstopped compute deals as a starting point for their credit assessment but instead substitute Nvidia’s credit rating.\\nLet’s briefly review how the GPU financing market works before we explain how this market is evolving to cater to the Nvidia backstop. Pricing for Neocloud financing with a long-term offtake is heavily driven by credit spreads for the hyperscaler or other company that is providing the offtake as opposed to following the Neocloud’s credit spreads. Instead – the Neocloud’s execution risk is what drives the spread above the underlying hyperscaler’s credit spread.\\nReaders may notice the huge gap between CoreWeave 5y unsecured bonds at ~10% and the 5.9% that CoreWeave paid for the fixed-rate tranche of their DDTL 4.0 $8.5B delayed-draw term loan, which was backstopped by Meta. This 5.9% yield works out to about 90 basis points wider than Meta’s 5y bond yield of ~5.0%. This can be interpreted as the market pricing CoreWeave’s execution risk with respect to that particular project at 90bps.\\nWe breakdown the main type of financing we see today in the table below from lowest credit risk to highest credit risk.\\nA natural question to ask is why has financing not evolved outside the four walls of 5-year IG offtake take-or-pay contracts? Neocloud financing has been challenging because it involves financing a complicated and novel stack of infrastructure and IT equipment where end demand for tokens is still new, not well understood by lenders, and lacks a long history from which to draw insights on risk management from. For lenders, it is much easier to focus on the hyperscaler offtake or backstop as there is no need to dive deep into tokenomics or rapidly evolving AI models and delve into the minutia of which scale-up network topologies perform best on the all to all collective – at the end of the day, it is hyperscaler risk.\\nNeoclouds have also had a strong incentive to stick with this recipe. To fund a cluster that they intend to rent on shorter terms with a broad book of customers, a Neocloud would have to fall back on unsecured funding. For a top Neocloud, this would mean paying an additional 4% interest, which would significantly impact the economics of the deal given the 70-80% loan to value ratios used to finance GPUs. In our modelling, we show that increasing funding from an all-in cost of 5.62% to an all-in cost of 10% drops PBT margins from 14.8% down to only 5.4%. Unsecured lending to a top Neocloud would probably be around 10%, but expect even more expensive unsecured financing for smaller Neoclouds with a shorter track record.\\nLastly, let’s discuss how banks will size lending to Neoclouds.\\nThe most important debt ratio that lenders look at is the debt service coverage ratio (DSCR). DSCR is calculated as the ratio of the project’s cash generation divided by total debt interest and principal payments in each period, and it is a measure of how well covered the debt payments are by the cash flow of the business.\\nWhen it comes to the Nvidia backstop, banks look at the DSCR ratios assuming revenue earned under the scenario where the backstop is activated. They are looking for a DSCR of at least 1.3x, particularly for the first few years of GPU financing agreement and size their loans to ensure that this bar is met. This typically corresponds to a 70-80% loan to value (LTV) ratio.\\nGreat Expectations – How the GPU Financing Market will Come of Age\\nGPU financing will have to rapidly evolve if it is to meet the staggering capital needs we outlined earlier in this article, and financing Nvidia backstopped clusters is the vanguard of this huge shift. Lenders in the past were not comfortable financing projects with price risk and have wanted a long-term offtake from a strong credit, a residual value cushion, a parent guarantee, a vendor backstop or some other floor under the asset. However, lenders are now starting to look deeper into Neoclouds’ business models, with our assistance and due diligence consulting services. Even though they have the Nvidia backstop in their back pocket, the highest quality lenders are now utilizing SemiAnalysis to scrutinize Neoclouds’ operational quality, go to market plan, customer book and pricing strategy.\\nThis is a healthy development – and the Nvidia backstop provides the support that lenders need to get up the curve and prepare for the era when they will finance Neoclouds as a standalone platform basis without any external backstops or guarantee, just like they would understand any other business they lend to that invest in the long term yet are exposed to shorter-term price risk – be it petrochemical companies, memory chip manufacturers and the like.\\nWe think that initial lending will be at a wider spread than the current 5y backstopped hyperscale deals which come in at around SOFR + 225bps (roughly equivalent to a Z-spread of 195bps, or about 5.9% total yield) but at a tighter spread than CoreWeave’s 5-year unsecured corporate bond which has a yield of ~10% (roughly equivalent to Z-spread of 600bps).\\nLenders will also need tools to price and manage risk in the new GPU lending world order. They will need a good GPU rental price index, a way to forecast GPU rental prices into the future and a model for estimating and tracking GPU residual value over time. They will need some way to differentiate among operator quality and a framework for understanding the differences in operator capability. They will also need to be able to model end demand, and how token demand maps into GPU demand.\\nLuckily – at SemiAnalysis, we have already launched products that address many of these needs!\\nOur GPU Rental Pricing Index tracks bilateral contract prices across the entire term structure, from on-demand to 5-year deals, across all major GPU SKUs, giving lenders and investors a transparent, transaction-validated benchmark where none previously existed. The H100 1y index is freely available for public use!\\nMeanwhile, our AI TCO Model has been forecasting GPU rental prices since 2023 and provides a complete three statement financial model complete with return metrics like IRR, return on invested capital, debt service coverage ratio and more. In our consulting practice, where we have provided technical and consulting due diligence to clients that have provided tens of billions in capital to Neoclouds, we have used the AI TCO model as a tool to validate many of their commercial assumptions and help underwrite the investment cases. The AI TCO Model also includes a framework for calculating GPU residual values and much more.\\nClusterMAX is the only Neocloud rating system in the industry. Our ClusterMAX rating system evaluates GPU cloud providers across 10 criteria including reliability, networking, and pricing, giving capital providers a standardized quality framework to assess counterparty and collateral risk. We do significant custom diligence as ClusterMAX cannot be used for commercial purposes without our authorization.\\nOur tokenomics practice tracks end token demand and consumption closely across hyperscalers and AI labs with a focus on SKU and model level ARRs for all key labs, model performance, token pricing, and more.\\nLastly, our InferenceX benchmarking product continually measures and tracks real-world GPU inference throughput and token efficiency across inference systems, models and software configurations, enabling lenders and operators to quantify the productive output, and therefore the revenue-generating capacity, of a cluster they are financing. It is an important resource for mapping token demand into GPU demand.\\nNvidia’s backstop program is only getting started, and we see announcements coming faster every quarter. Many Neoclouds are already deep in negotiations with Nvidia, and there are still others that are considering engaging with the backstop program. Let’s briefly discuss the deals that have been announced to date – all of which are in the Asia Pacific region so far.\\nThe first is SharonAI’s 72MW AI factory in Australia. Announced in June 2026, the deal has SharonAI scaling up to as many as 40,000 GB300s under a six-year backstop. Sharon disclosed a total backstop value of $4.88B, which works out to an implied floor of about $2.33/hr/GPU on average over the six year period. Sharon AI’s footprint is now set to grow to 132MW with 102MW contracted, and the company guides to reaching more than 55,000 Nvidia GPUs in total (inclusive of the 40,000 GB300s backstopped) by mid-2027.\\nFirmus’s 360MW AI Cluster in Batam, Indonesia is the next major project announced with an Nvidia backstop. It will likely be housed in a DayOne facility in Kabil Industrial Tech Park (KITP). Announced just recently on June 29 th , 2026, this project shows the scale at which Nvidia is rolling out the backstop program. Firmus’ first clusters were based in Singapore and focused on H100s deployed using immersion cooling. The next key cluster launched was the 18,000 GB300 cluster in Melbourne, Australia housed in a 42MW self-built datacenter. This project was financed using a $10B USD facility led by Blackstone (Tactical Opportunities, Credit and Insurance) and supported by Coatue.\\nThe Nvidia backstop allows Firmus to grow their business by an order of magnitude. As per Nvidia’s key objectives that we outlined above, the focus of this cluster will be to open up compute to a variety of AI Natives, Enterprises and Inference Providers, with many different rental tenors to be offered. Firmus announced that they expect $25B to $30B of customer revenue over the six years. We expect a portion of the revenue above the backstop level will be shared with Nvidia as per the structure we outlined earlier today.\\nBeyond this – Firmus also announced a 600MW firm-energy deal with Gunvor that will also underwrite 1.2GW of new renewables development and 1.5GWh of storage in South Australia by 2032. Firmus will still need to identify a datacenter provider or build their own datacenters before they can deploy GPUs.\\nWe have seen many other neoclouds having backstops, but many are not public yet.\\nNvidia is not the only merchant GPU vendor to utilize backstops. AMD has already been providing backstops starting last year. We wrote in our AMD article from June 2025 that AMD offered AWS, OCI, Digital Ocean, Vultr, Tensorwave, Crusoe and other Neoclouds a backstop deal, whereby in exchange for customers’ willingness to buy more AMD GPUs, AMD will stand ready to rent back a significant chunk of this capacity in the form of long-term contracts for internal AMD software development purposes if the Neocloud isn’t able to full sell their capacity.\\nThere are many more deals in the works and we believe that many of the clusters announced to be built in partnership with Nvidia will later be confirmed to be built atop an Nvidia backstop. In the next section, we will explore how this revenue backstop will affect Nvidia’s balance sheet and income statement.\"}\n{\"article\": 18, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"finance.yahoo.com\", \"title\": \"Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims\", \"text\": \"Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims\\nJim Cramer doubled down on Nvidia on Monday, urging investors to buy the stock as the chipmaker rejected claims that its next-generation AI rack systems face delays until 2028.\\nThe clash pits Nvidia against research firm SemiAnalysis, which alleges manufacturing setbacks have hit the Kyber NVL144 architecture showcased at GTC earlier this year.\\nSemiAnalysis Claims Put Nvidia's Kyber Timeline in Doubt\\nSemiAnalysis claims the high-density rack design built for Rubin Ultra GPUs has slipped by more than 12 months. The firm blamed persistent manufacturing problems with the system's complex PCB midplane.\\nThe firm also claimed Nvidia scrapped its NVL72x2 back-to-back rack after pushback from hyperscaler customers.\\nNvidia's supply chain felt the report within hours. Japan's Ibiden, which counts Nvidia as its largest client, fell as much as 10% on Monday, Bloomberg reported.\\nKingboard Laminates tumbled 18% in Hong Kong, while Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid 11% in Seoul.\\nNvidia rejected the claims, telling media outlets that its roadmap remains intact. The chipmaker, fresh off launching a revenue-sharing compute program for AI startups, has faced this script before.\\nWhen Blackwell delay reports surfaced in August 2024, Nvidia insisted production would ramp on schedule. It then fixed a design flaw and shipped several billion dollars of Blackwell hardware within months.\\nJim Cramer Backs Nvidia Despite the Noise\\nCramer reaffirmed his bullish stance and urged investors to buy Nvidia. He told CNBC that chip stocks are staging a \\\"revenge trade\\\" after last week's \\\"misguided selling.\\\"\\nThe numbers frame his conviction. The Philadelphia Semiconductor Index gained 87.8% in the second quarter, its best quarter since records began in 1994, Axios reported.\\nNvidia missed most of that rally. The stock traded near $196.58 at this writing, up almost 2% over the last 24 hours.\\nLast week tested the sector's nerve. AI chip stocks cracked after Michael Burry's bubble warning, while memory stocks plunged sharply on supply glut fears.\\nCramer, however, sees the pullback as an opportunity. He named his five AI stock picks earlier this month, favoring chip suppliers over Big Tech giants.\\nNvidia's next earnings report will show whether rack-level friction reaches data center revenue. Until then, investors must weigh Cramer's conviction against a laggard chart and SemiAnalysis' supply chain warnings.\\nRead the Original story Jim Cramer Says Buy Nvidia as Chipmaker Rejects 2028 AI Delay Claims by Lockridge Okoth at beincrypto.com\"}\n{\"article\": 19, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"futurumgroup.com\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA DSX Promises More Revenue per Gigawatt. Who Actually Captures It?\", \"text\": \"NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system are engineered to raise the revenue per gigawatt an AI factory can produce by lifting tokens per megawatt. Paired with NVIDIA’s July 1 revenue-sharing model, DSX forms half of a two-part business model: grow the revenue a gigawatt generates, then collect a recurring cut of it. Sharon AI and Firmus are building the first DSX-aligned factories under the arrangement.\\nHow NVIDIA’s DSX reference design and operating system raise revenue per gigawatt by lifting token efficiency\\nThe DSX stack: reference designs, DSX Sim digital twin, the open source DSX OS, and the MaxLPS power-tuning layer\\nHow DSX and the revenue-sharing model form two halves of one business model — grow the revenue a gigawatt produces, then take a recurring cut\\nWhy AI factory economics approaching $100 billion per gigawatt make tokens-per-megawatt efficiency the decisive variable\\nThe bull case of a compounding efficiency-plus-financing flywheel against the bear case of product-market mismatch and an unresolved split of the upside.\\nThe News: On July 1, 2026, NVIDIA introduced a business model that lets AI cloud operators deploy large-scale, multi-tenant AI factories through a combination of revenue sharing and credit support, with the resulting facilities built as DSX AI factories. Participating clouds procure NVIDIA infrastructure and sell NVIDIA-powered services; NVIDIA earns its standard product revenue plus a share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity. Sharon AI, deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs across new capacity in Australia, and Firmus, building a 360-megawatt DSX-aligned AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 GPUs, are among the first partners.\\nAnalyst Take: NVIDIA’s revenue-sharing model answered how the company gets paid twice on the same silicon. The DSX reference design and operating system is NVIDIA’s mechanism for raising the revenue per gigawatt that a fixed block of power can produce, and it is the half of the business model that determines whether the recurring cut NVIDIA now takes on cloud revenue is a rich stream or a rounding error. Introduced as a platform at GTC Taipei and now the design standard for the AI factories that Sharon AI and Firmus are building under the July 1 financing model, DSX reframes the gigawatt from a power budget into a revenue function. NVIDIA is now selling the operating system that decides how much sellable output a gigawatt of compute yields, then taking a share of that output.\\nThe clearest signal of how NVIDIA wants the market to read this sits in the byline. The announcement carries the name of Colette Kress, NVIDIA’s EVP and CFO, in her first company blog post on the topic. A CFO does not headline a product blog unless the company intends the announcement to be read as a financial-reporting event rather than a catalog addition — the debut of a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream that will surface in the financial model, not merely a new offering in the lineup. That authorship is the tell that revenue per gigawatt is the point of the announcement, not a byproduct of it, and it is consistent with the message Kress has carried on recent earnings calls.\\nDSX Reframes the Gigawatt From a Power Budget to a Revenue Function\\nDSX is not a single product but a stack: reference designs for physical blueprint, DSX Sim as an Omniverse digital twin that validates a factory before a rack lands, DSX OS for the open source operations software that runs it, and the MaxLPS efficiency layer that tunes power at the rack. The layer that matters most for revenue per gigawatt is MaxLPS, which combines liquid cooling with software-driven in-rack power tuning to run up to 40% more GPUs at their most efficient operating point inside a fixed power budget, on NVIDIA’s figures. DSX Flex extends the same control to the utility grid, with a multi-megawatt pilot alongside Emerald AI and Silicon Valley Power that adjusts factory consumption programmatically in response to grid signals. DSX reframes AI factory competition from cores per dollar to tokens per megawatt. That reframing is the entire revenue-per-gigawatt case: if power is the binding constraint, the operator that extracts more tokens per watt earns more revenue, and DSX is the tool NVIDIA is selling to do it.\\nThe Business Model Has Two Halves: Grow Revenue per Gigawatt, Then Share It\\nThe connection to the financing model is where the strategy becomes coherent. Under the July 1 revenue-sharing and credit-support model, NVIDIA earns its standard hardware margin plus a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud income the supported capacity generates. That recurring cut is worth precisely as much as the capacity is utilized and efficient, which is exactly what DSX is engineered to maximize. DSX grows the revenue a gigawatt produces; the revenue-share model lets NVIDIA collect a slice of the larger number. Financing places NVIDIA silicon inside capital-constrained AI factories, and DSX ensures the resulting factories run at the token-per-watt efficiency that makes both the operator’s business and NVIDIA’s royalty viable.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the template. Firmus is explicitly building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, co-designing its HyperCube architecture to NVIDIA’s DSX blueprints, and states the integration is meant to improve tokens per watt and reduce cost per token for its customers. That is an operator adopting NVIDIA’s revenue-per-gigawatt engine and NVIDIA’s financing in the same deal.\\nThe $100 Billion Gigawatt Makes Efficiency the Whole Game\\nThe economics explain why NVIDIA is willing to give the operating software away. Huang has said a 1-gigawatt AI factory that once cost $20–30 billion now runs $50–60 billion and will soon reach $80–100 billion per gigawatt, with 100 gigawatts expected online before the end of the decade. When a gigawatt costs as much as $100 billion to build, an idle or inefficient watt is not a technical footnote but destroyed capital. In that environment, revenue per gigawatt is the metric that decides whether a $100 billion facility earns its cost of capital, and tokens per megawatt is the lever. DSX is NVIDIA’s claim on that lever. The implication is that the reference design and operating system are not a giveaway but the highest-leverage point in the entire buildout: the software that governs whether the most expensive industrial assets ever built run at a profit.\\nWho Captures the Revenue per Gigawatt Is the Open Question\\nThe bull case is a flywheel: DSX raises revenue per gigawatt, the financing model spreads NVIDIA silicon into every regional and sovereign operator, and the recurring royalty compounds. The bear case is that the operator builds their own software system and applies further margin pressure on system vendors. NVIDIA has partly funded its own demand through investments in OpenAI, CoreWeave, and Nebius, and the revenue-share model extends that pattern by other means. The competitive counter to this strategy is concentrated among the buyers that have the most to gain from it. The hyperscalers and frontier labs on NVIDIA’s own Vera CPU adopter list are precisely the organizations capable of building alternatives with custom CPUs, open networking, disaggregated inference, or their own operations software. The question this headline poses may gain clarity over the next several quarters: DSX will raise revenue per gigawatt, but the split between the operator that owns the power and the vendor that owns the operating system is the number NVIDIA has not yet disclosed.\\nIndependent confirmation of the 40% revenue claims, which are currently NVIDIA figures.\\nWhether the open source operations software attracts genuine multi-vendor contributions or remains optimized primarily for NVIDIA silicon.\\nWhether the grid-responsive pilots with Emerald AI and Silicon Valley Power expand into utility-scale deployments, turning stranded-watt recovery into a repeatable source of revenue per gigawatt.\\nNVIDIA has not disclosed the revenue share percentage or how usage is measured.\\nWhether the DSX-aligned factories reach the tokens-per-megawatt efficiency and utilization the model assumes, and whether Firmus’s $25–30 billion in committed off-take converts into recognized revenue on schedule.\\nSee the complete announcement on the company blog.\\nCan ADI Hot Swap Controllers De-Risk NVIDIA’s 800 VDC Transition?\\nWill Adobe and NVIDIA’s RTX Spark Partnership Redefine Creative AI Workflows?\\nNVIDIA Cosmos 3 and Open Agent Tools: Is Physical AI About to Leave the Lab?\"}\n{\"article\": 20, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"the-decoder.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\", \"text\": \"Nvidia's Kyber NVL144 reportedly pushed back more than a year, Asian suppliers drop\\nA report from analyst firm SemiAnalysis says Nvidia's next-generation AI server rack has been delayed by more than a year. Suppliers across Japan, Taiwan, South Korea, and Hong Kong saw their stock prices drop sharply in response.\\nAsian tech stocks slid after analyst firm SemiAnalysis reported on X that Nvidia's next AI server rack system, Kyber NVL144, has been pushed back more than twelve months to 2028 because of manufacturing problems.\\nThe issue, according to SemiAnalysis, is the circuit boards. Specifically, the PCB midplane, a central board that connects all the individual components, has proven extremely difficult to produce without defects. Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang had shown off Kyber NVL144 just three months earlier at the company's GTC conference.\\nThe report hit an already jittery investor base. After years of AI-fueled gains, even small setbacks trigger sharp sell-offs. Japanese PCB maker Ibiden, which counts Nvidia as its largest customer, dropped as much as ten percent. Kingboard Laminates fell 18 percent in Hong Kong, Elite Material lost ten percent in Taiwan, and Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid eleven percent in South Korea. The sell-off follows massive run-ups. Samsung Electro-Mechanics had gained more than 600 percent this year, and Kingboard Laminates more than 470 percent.\\nScrapped designs give competitors an opening\\nSemiAnalysis lists more setbacks. A planned alternative design called NVL72x2, which would have placed two Oberon racks back to back, has been scrapped entirely. Cloud providers and large data center operators pushed back against the unusual form factor and the high operational overhead. The more powerful version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra chip with four compute dies has also been canceled. Only the smaller two-die version remains, delivering roughly half the real-world compute performance.\\nA key interconnect technology called CPO-NVSwitch, which links many chips into a single large system, won't arrive until the generation after next, called Feynman. That means Nvidia lacks a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra to very large systems for now. The gap could give competitors like AMD's MI500X or Google's TPUv8i Broadfly room to move in. Nvidia plans to make up for the shortfall by selling more Oberon-Rubin racks in the existing form factor.\\nA Kyber delay doesn't necessarily mean overall AI spending will shrink, said Gary Tan of Allspring Global Investments, according to Bloomberg . It shows that Nvidia's most ambitious system is taking longer than expected. The current stock weakness is mostly driven by profit-taking. Shawn Oh of NH Investment & Securities pointed to growing uncertainty around Nvidia's expansion plans, which gives alternative AI platforms more room to compete.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_4","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if 21 <= x[\\\"article\\\"] <= 30]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"article\": 21, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"datacenterdynamics.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia pushes Kyber release to 2028 following manufacturing concerns – report\", \"text\": \"By Charlotte Trueman\\nNvidia has delayed production of its Kyber rack-scale architecture by more than 12 months, pushing its release date to 2028, SemiAnalysis has reported.\\nThe Kyber server cabinets, which have been designed to house 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs connected via the company’s NVLink 7 Switch, had originally been slated for release in 2027.\\nKyber allows GPUs to be mounted in compute trays that sit vertically rather than horizontally, a design that Nvidia has described as being “like books on a shelf,” and which reportedly boosts density and reduces latency.\\nHowever, the research firm claimed that issues in manufacturing the printed circuit board (PCB) midplane that connects the modules together have caused the setback.\\n“Kyber NVL144 rack architecture has been delayed to 2028 as the PCB midplane remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint,” SemiAnalysis said, adding that Nvidia’s alternative plan, which involved placing two Oberon racks back-to-back in order to achieve a similar power output, has also been scrapped after cloud companies and hyperscalers pushed back against its “odd design and heavy operational burden.”\\nThe manufacturing issues will also impact the planned NVL576 system, which connects 8x Oberon racks over CPO between the NVSwitches, meaning that the system will also likely be delayed or limited to small volumes, the firm noted. All this leaves Nvidia with “no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,” SemiAnalysis said.\\nFirst announced at Nvidia’s GTC conference in 2025, Nvidia has previously said the liquid-cooled Kyber racks will offer a 4x performance improvement over its Blackwell NVL72 system – also known as Oberon – and consume some 600kW.\\nThe news comes a week after SemiAnalysis separately reported that Nvidia had canceled the four-die Rubin Ultra design in favor of the two-chiplet design, also due to “manufacturing execution concerns.” The two-die offering will deliver around half the performance of the four-die Rubin Ultra.\\nIn response to a request for comment from DCD regarding the Kyber claims, a spokesperson for Nvidia said: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\"}\n{\"article\": 22, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"businesstimes.com.sg\", \"title\": \"Nvidia AI server delay report sends Asian tech stocks sliding\", \"text\": \"Nvidia AI server delay report sends Asian tech stocks sliding\\nThe stock declines showed investors are wary of any potential negative developments after big runups in AI-related stocks\\n[SINGAPORE] Asian technology stocks slumped after a report that Nvidia’s next-generation AI server rack system has been delayed by more than a year due to manufacturing difficulties.\\nResearch firm SemiAnalysis said in an X post that Nvidia’s Kyber NVL144 hit setbacks in the construction of printed circuit boards for the platform. Nvidia did not respond to a request for comment outside of regular office hours.\\nThe report landed as AI stock investors are growing increasingly jittery , with even the smallest setback sparking outsized reactions after the yearslong rally. Last week, global tech stocks whipsawed on headlines hinting at potential overcapacity in the AI buildup and growing competition.\\nJapan’s Ibiden, a PCB maker that counts Nvidia as its largest client, dropped as much as 10 per cent. Among related suppliers, Kingboard Laminates Holdings tumbled 18 per cent in Hong Kong, Elite Material fell 10 per cent in Taiwan and Samsung Electro-Mechanics slid 11 per cent in South Korea.\\nThe SemiAnalysis report spurred “weakness across regional tech” on Monday (Jul 6), said Shawn Oh, head of Korea cash equities at NH Investment & Securities in Seoul. The prospect of a Kyber NVL144 delay together with other points in the post are “raising uncertainty around Nvidia’s next-generation scale-out roadmap and creating a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms,” he added.\\nThe stock declines showed investors are wary of any potential negative developments after big runups in AI-related stocks. Samsung Electro-Mechanics has surged more than 600 per cent this year, while Kingboard Laminates is up more than 470 per cent.\\nAn MSCI gauge of sector shares is down 8.5 per cent in the past two weeks, extending its loss on Monday with PCB makers among the leading decliners.\\nA delay to Kyber does not necessarily imply weaker AI capex, but “it more likely suggests that Nvidia’s most ambitious next-gen system architecture may take longer to deploy, which is not entirely surprising”, said Gary Tan, a portfolio manager at Allspring Global Investments.\\n“Today’s weakness is driven more by profit-taking, sector rotation and caution ahead of the US reopening after the Jul 4 holiday,” Tan said. He also cited a possible drag from this week’s mega US listing for Korean memory maker SK Hynix\\nAn earnings report due on Tuesday from Samsung Electronics is the next key point to watch for Asian tech stocks. BLOOMBERG\"}\n{\"article\": 23, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"tomshardware.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\", \"text\": \"Nvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated] | Tom's Hardware\\nNvidia's Kyber rack for Rubin Ultra reportedly delayed to 2028, stopgap solution also axed due to customer pushback — Analyst firm SemiAnalysis says PCB midplane problems led to the delay [Updated]\\nBy Luke James\\nLeaving Nvidia without a proven way to scale Rubin Ultra past its current Oberon rack.\\nUpdate 7/6/2026 4:10pm PT : An Nvidia representative responded with a short statement to Tom's Hardware : \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\" Nvidia provided no further details in response to our follow-up questions. As such, it is unclear if Nvidia's statement refers to its original roadmap, planned delivery schedules, and hardware, or if those particulars had already been changed and the statement refers to a newly updated roadmap.\\nOriginal Story:\\nNvidia reportedly won't ship its Kyber NVL144 rack until 2028, a delay of more than 12 months that pushes the cabinet meant for 2027's Rubin Ultra GPUs into the following year, according to a SemiAnalysis thread on X. The holdup is ostensibly being caused by manufacturing challenges with a PCB midplane that connects eight Oberon racks between the NVSwitches, which Nvidia calls the orthogonal backplane. Nvidia is also understood to have killed NVL72x2, a stopgap rack designed to tide customers over, and that no proven alternative is now available to widen Rubin Ultra's scale-up in 2027.\\nThe orthogonal backplane sits between Kyber's vertically mounted compute trays and the switch trays behind them, replacing the cable harnesses of earlier racks with a rigid board that carries the all-copper NVLink fabric. Kyber runs liquid cooling by default and stacks 144 Rubin Ultra packages, double the 72 packages in a current Oberon NVL72 rack . Every GPU-to-GPU link inside the cabinet runs through that board, and copper traces lose signal integrity as layer counts increase, alongside power delivery and thermal design challenges. Jensen Huang held up the gray backplane on stage at GTC back in March.\\nTrade analyses of the board describe three 26-layer sections laminated into one 78-layer stack close to a square meter in area, with trace spacing at or below 25μm and impedance held within a tolerance of 5% to keep 448 Gb/s-class signaling intact. A cabled version of the same interconnect would need upward of 20,000 discrete cables, which is why Nvidia is moving the wiring onto a single passive board.\\nNVL72x2 would have bolted two Oberon racks back-to-back to reach Kyber-class density over copper NVLink, per SemiAnalysis , which said Nvidia abandoned the stopgap after its largest customers balked at running two linked cabinets as a single unit. NVL576, a separate configuration tying eight racks together through co-packaged optics, is likely to slip too or ship in low volume until that optical technology matures.\\nThese cancellations leave Nvidia with \\\"no proven solution to expand the scale-up world size for Rubin Ultra,\\\" meaning the largest single Rubin Ultra domain in 2027 could match, but not exceed, what Oberon already delivers.\\nNvidia dropped the quad-chiplet Rubin Ultra GPU for a dual-chiplet part last week over manufacturing execution concerns, halving the accelerator's per-package compute. SemiAnalysis has also placed a fully production-ready co-packaged optics NVSwitch no earlier than the Feynman generation that follows Rubin, which leaves copper as the only near-term solution for linking Rubin Ultra at rack scale and thereby puts even more weight on the PCB midplane.\\nThe delay only applies only to the Rubin Ultra phase and its Kyber rack. Nvidia's 2026 Rubin GPUs, which reuse the current Oberon rack, aren't part of the reported delay. We've reached out to Nvidia for comment.\\n2028? Maybe some data centres will be finished by then only to have to entirely replace the racks if they want to use these instead of the dusty old blackwells?\"}\n{\"article\": 24, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"tikr.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Delays Next-Generation Kyber Server Architecture as Manufacturing Hurdles Give Rivals a Rare Opening\", \"text\": \"Price change for Nvidia stock in the last 6 months: 4%\\n$NVDA Share Price as of Jul. 2: $195\\n52-Week High: $237\\n$NVDA Stock Price Target: $302\\nNvidia ( NVDA ) stock barely moved on Monday after news broke that one of the company’s biggest upcoming products is running late. Research firm SemiAnalysis reported that Kyber, Nvidia’s next rack-scale server design, has been pushed back more than a year , from 2027 to 2028.\\nKyber matters because it’s built to pack 144 of Nvidia’s most powerful chips into a single cabinet, letting them work together like one giant computer. That kind of processing power is what AI companies need to train and run their most advanced models.\\nThe design was supposed to launch alongside Vera Rubin Ultra, Nvidia’s next-generation system, in 2027. The holdup comes down to manufacturing problems with a specialized circuit board inside the system, according to SemiAnalysis.\\nA larger version, called NVL576, which links eight racks together via optical connections, is also likely to be delayed or to ship only in small volumes.\\nNvidia had a backup plan: bolting two current-generation racks together to achieve similar power, but that idea was scrapped too.\\nCloud customers reportedly rejected it because the design was awkward and the service was expensive to run. SemiAnalysis says this leaves Nvidia without a proven way to scale up Rubin Ultra for now, which could give rivals like AMD and Google an opening at the high end of the AI chip market. Nvidia has not responded to requests for comment.\\nDespite this news, Nvidia stock isn’t showing much reaction, trading close to flat. That’s likely because the company’s current-generation Rubin systems are already in full production and set to start shipping this fall to eight major cloud partners, including Amazon Web Services, Microsoft Azure, and Google Cloud.\\nSemiAnalysis actually expects Nvidia’s data-center compute revenue in the second half of fiscal 2027 to come in 20% above Wall Street’s current expectations.\\nNvidia’s recent earnings back up why investors aren’t panicking.\\nIn the most recent quarter, the company posted $82 billion in total revenue, up 85% year-over-year, with data center revenue alone hitting $75 billion.\\nFree cash flow came in at a record $49 billion.\\nCEO Jensen Huang has repeatedly said demand for AI infrastructure remains “parabolic,” driven by hyperscalers, AI-native clouds, and now a growing CPU business built around the new Vera chip.\\nSee analysts’ growth forecasts and price targets for Nvidia stock (It’s free) >>>\\nWhat the Market Is Telling Us About Nvidia Stock\\nThe muted stock reaction suggests investors see this delay as a bump in the road rather than a major setback.\\nNvidia stock has been driven mostly by current-generation product strength and record financial results, and that momentum doesn’t disappear because a future product slips a year. Still, the Kyber delay is worth watching.\\nIf manufacturing issues persist, it could open a real window for competitors to gain ground at the top end of the AI chip market.\\nFor now, Nvidia stock seems to be trading on today’s results more than tomorrow’s roadmap.\\nEstimate a company’s fair value instantly (Free with TIKR) >>>\\nHow Much Upside Does Nvidia Stock Have From Here?\\nWith TIKR’s new Valuation Model tool , you can estimate a stock’s potential share price in under a minute.\\nAll it takes is three simple inputs:\\nIf you’re not sure what to enter, TIKR automatically fills in each input using analysts’ consensus estimates, giving you a quick, reliable starting point.\\nFrom there, TIKR calculates the potential share price and total returns under Bull, Base, and Bear scenarios so you can quickly see whether a stock looks undervalued or overvalued .\\nSee a stock’s true value in under 60 seconds (Free with TIKR) >>>\\nTwo lawmakers disclosed buying SpaceX stock after its record IPO, raising ethics questions amid heavy short-seller activity.\\nSeagate dropped 10.38% in a single session on a memory-sector oversupply scare, one day after Bank of America lifted its target to $1,150. The trigger had little to do with what Seagate actually sells. With the order book locked for four to five quarters and fiscal Q4 earnings days away, the question is whether this was a crack or a discount.\\nDell dropped 7.27% in a single session as the market circled back to one worry: AI servers are dragging down margins. The same week, Dell signed a storage deal that speaks directly to that fear. We checked whether the sell-off is a warning or a window.\\nApplied Materials dropped more than 7% in a single session as a sector-wide chip selloff hit and Michael Burry disclosed a fresh short at $729.40. After a 366% five-year run, the question is whether the pullback is a gift or the first crack. The TIKR model gives a measured answer.\\nFigma stock rose 9.49% to close at $21.34, its second straight leg higher off the June lows. But the fuel was a Russell index add and a re-upped analyst call, not new fundamentals. With an August lock-up and an activist overhang still ahead, the question is whether an index-flow bounce can hold.\"}\n{\"article\": 25, \"day\": \"2026-07-06\", \"publisher\": \"uk.finance.yahoo.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia underperforms peers as report sparks rack delay worries\", \"text\": \"Investing.com -- NVIDIA Corporation (NASDAQ:NVDA) shares ticked up just 0.8% on Monday, starkly trailing a 3.2% surge in the Philadelphia Semiconductor Index and lagging rivals Advanced Micro Devices Inc (NASDAQ:AMD), which gained 7.7%, and Broadcom Inc (NASDAQ:AVGO), which jumped 4.4%. The underperformance came as a broad rally for chipmakers collided with a boutique research report alleging significant delays in the AI giant's next-generation server architectures.\\nSupply chain research firm SemiAnalysis claimed in a weekend report that Nvidia's highly anticipated Kyber NVL144 rack architecture will be delayed to 2028 due to manufacturing challenges with its PCB midplane. An Nvidia spokesperson swiftly refuted such claims in a brief statement emailed to Investing.com, stating: \\\"Our roadmap is intact.\\\"\\nThe research firm further alleged that Nvidia cancelled an alternative back-to-back rack design following heavy operational pushback from hyperscale cloud clients. Additionally, the report pointed to a downsized version of the upcoming Rubin Ultra platform, claiming a crucial four-die configuration had been scrapped.\\nThe negative headlines triggered a sharp overnight selloff among Asian tech suppliers, which stumbled on the report as investors locked in profits following massive year-to-date run-ups. Japan's Ibiden Co., a PCB maker that counts Nvidia as its largest client, dropped 8.4% by close, while Hong Kong-listed Kingboard Laminates Holdings Ltd. tumbled 12.6% and South Korea's Samsung Electro-Mechanics Co. slid 8.1%.\\nAccording to analysts cited by Bloomberg, these rumored technical setbacks could severely cap the scale-up capacity of Nvidia's future hardware. This perceived vulnerability may create a wider competitive window for alternative AI platforms, specifically AMD's MI500X and Broadcom-backed custom TPUs.\\nMizuho analyst Jordan Klein dismissed such fears in his daily \\\"Tech Bytes\\\" note, calling the product delay stories \\\"more noise.\\\" \\\"I would not be worried about NVDA nor looking to sell the stock,\\\" Klein said.\\nThe analyst additionally pointed to strong earnings last Friday from Hon Hai Precision Industry Co Ltd (TW:2317), one of Nvidia's largest suppliers. Klein noted that Hon Hai management forecasted a continued acceleration in AI demand, which the analyst said was \\\"a very good sign into the NVDA launch of their new Vera Rubin rack.\\\"\\nThe lackluster session keeps the world's most valuable public company squarely rangebound as investors navigate an increasingly jittery AI market. Just last week, erratic headlines regarding Meta Platforms' AI spending ambitions sent tech stocks on a whipsaw ride, underscoring growing market anxiety in the AI trade.\\nUltimately, the market's outsized reaction to the unconfirmed supply chain friction reveals a fragile consensus among AI investors after a multi-year, historic run-up. With valuation multiples priced for perfection, even the slightest hint of a product roadmap stumble is enough to make traders pause, regardless of Nvidia's official denials.\"}\n{\"article\": 26, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"idnfinancials.com\", \"title\": \"DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\", \"text\": \"DeepSeek develops AI chips, challenging Nvidia and Huawei\\nJAKARTA - Chinese artificial intelligence (AI) startup DeepSeek is developing its own AI chips to reduce its reliance on products from Nvidia and Huawei, which have so far been used in the development of the company's AI models.\\nAccording to Reuters , citing three sources familiar with the plans, DeepSeek's chips are being designed specifically for inference, the stage where trained AI models generate responses for users.\\nUnlike chips used for model training, inference chips generally consume less power and are designed to meet the growing demand for AI computing.\\nThe move marks a major strategic shift for DeepSeek, which has so far been known for focusing on AI model development rather than the semiconductor business.\\nIf successful, the company would join a number of global AI developers that have begun designing their own chips to gain greater control over computing infrastructure while reducing dependence on Nvidia.\\nThe news briefly weighed on Nvidia shares, which fell around 1.6% in US pre-market trading.\\n\\\"Nvidia's position in China is effectively at zero and will remain that way. DeepSeek has almost no chance of selling chips outside China unless it gains access to advanced manufacturing technology,\\\" said analyst Richard Windsor of Radio Free Mobile, adding that the development would not affect the chipmaker.\\nDeepSeek attracted global attention in early 2025 after launching a low-cost, high-performance AI model that disrupted the global technology industry.\\nThe Hangzhou-based company has so far been better known for focusing on AI model innovation rather than commercialising the technology it develops.\\nAmid US restrictions on AI chip exports to China, Huawei has expanded its domestic market share and now controls around half of China's AI chip market, which is valued at approximately US$50 billion.\\nHowever, that dominance is increasingly being challenged as several local technology companies, including Alibaba and Baidu, develop their own AI chips.\\nAccording to Reuters sources, DeepSeek's chip development project remains at an early stage. Over the past year, the company has been communicating with chip design firms, foundries and memory suppliers to support the development of the semiconductors.\\nDeepSeek has also increased recruitment of chip design engineers in recent months. The hiring process has been conducted discreetly, without publicly posting job openings on general recruitment platforms.\\nDespite becoming one of the symbols of China's AI resurgence, DeepSeek has maintained a low profile and has not responded to Reuters' request for confirmation regarding the project.\\nGlobally, DeepSeek's move follows a broader trend among major AI companies developing their own chips. OpenAI introduced its first inference chip, called Jalapeno, last month in collaboration with Broadcom, while Anthropic is also reportedly considering developing its own AI chips.\\nFor DeepSeek, chip development carries greater strategic importance because US export restrictions have prevented Chinese companies from accessing Nvidia's most advanced AI chips.\\nThe Chinese government has also continued to encourage domestic technology companies to build their own semiconductor supply chains.\\nDeepSeek founder Liang Wenfeng acknowledged in a 2024 interview that chip export restrictions posed a challenge for the company.\\nPreviously, DeepSeek used a combination of Nvidia and Huawei chips. The base model supporting DeepSeek R1 was trained using Nvidia's H800 chips, a product specifically designed for the Chinese market before the US government banned its export in late 2023.\\nIn recent months, the company has increasingly relied on Huawei's ecosystem. In April, DeepSeek launched its V4 model, which had been optimised for Huawei Ascend chips, while Huawei revealed that training for the V4-Flash model also used Ascend processors.\\nThe launch also drove a surge in demand for Huawei's Ascend 950 chips among Chinese technology companies.\\nIf successfully developed, DeepSeek's inference chips would target the fastest-growing segment of the AI industry.\\nAs the use of AI applications expands, computing demand is shifting from model training towards inference processes that require more efficient and energy-saving chips.\\nHowever, developing AI chips is not an easy task. The semiconductor industry requires significant investment and years of development to produce competitive products.\\nAnother challenge comes from US restrictions limiting Chinese companies' access to the most advanced chip manufacturing facilities and high-bandwidth memory (HBM), a key component in modern AI chips.\\nAt the same time, DeepSeek has begun opening itself to external funding. Reuters previously reported that the company was preparing its first funding round worth US$7 billion, with a valuation of between US$52 billion and US$59 billion, ending a years-long strategy of rejecting outside investment. (DH/LM)\"}\n{\"article\": 27, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"247wallst.com\", \"title\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\", \"text\": \"A Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia's Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout - 24/7 Wall St.\\nA Circuit Board Problem Just Delayed Nvidia’s Next AI System to 2028 And Chip Stocks Are Already Feeling the Fallout\\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed,…\\nBy Danielle Liverance\\nA 78-layer PCB midplane delays NVIDIA's Kyber NVL144 to 2028, dropping NVDA 1.4%, while AMD gains a clear competitive window with shares up 158% YTD.\\nGoogle Cloud revenue surged 63% with backlog nearly doubling to $460 billion, strengthening its in-house TPU case as NVIDIA's Kyber slip extends to 2028.\\nThe AI bottleneck has shifted from chip design to system assembly, yet NVIDIA still commands 81% market share and trades 45% below GuruFocus fair value.\\nA printed circuit board just pushed NVIDIA’s most powerful AI system into 2028 — at least according to one research firm. Semiconductor research firm SemiAnalysis reported on July 5–6, 2026 that NVIDIA’s Kyber NVL144 rack-scale AI system has been delayed, from its planned 2027 launch to 2028. CNBC cited the SemiAnalysis post. NVIDIA publicly disputed the report on Monday, with a spokesperson telling Bloomberg its “roadmap is intact.”\\nNVIDIA ( NASDAQ:NVDA | NVDA Price Prediction ) shares actually rose more than 1% on Monday — helped by the company’s denial and a Goldman Sachs note calling the valuation compelling — leaving its market cap around $4.7 trillion. Overseas, PCB suppliers to NVIDIA came under pressure, with reports of sharp intraday declines in names such as Japan’s Ibiden and Hong Kong’s Kingboard Laminates Holdings.\\nKyber represents NVIDIA’s shift from selling chips to selling complete AI infrastructure. A single Kyber NVL144 cabinet houses 144 Rubin Ultra GPUs operating as one unified computing platform, targeting hyperscalers like Microsoft, Google, Meta, and Amazon. A larger sibling, the NVL576, designed to link eight racks via optical connections, is also likely delayed or limited to small initial volumes, according to SemiAnalysis.\\nThe reported culprit is a component most retail investors have not heard of: the PCB midplane (which NVIDIA also calls the orthogonal backplane), a printed circuit board with up to 78 layers that connects electronic modules within the rack. SemiAnalysis said it “remains challenging from a manufacturability standpoint.” Stacking 78 layers with signal integrity, thermal tolerance, and yield at volume is a hard engineering problem, and SemiAnalysis frames it as the gating item for NVIDIA’s most ambitious system.\\nThe delay raises a broader question: whether NVIDIA’s annual cadence, built to stay ahead of AMD and custom silicon, is hitting physical manufacturing limits. Data Center revenue reached $75.246 billion (+92% YoY) in Q1 FY27, and CEO Jensen Huang described “the largest infrastructure expansion in human history”. The bottleneck is assembly.\\nAMD ( NASDAQ:AMD ) has the clearest window. Shares are up 157.77% year to date, and Goldman Sachs raised its AMD price target to $640 from $450 on July 6. CEO Lisa Su has said “customer engagement around MI450 Series and Helios is strengthening, with leading customer forecasts exceeding our initial expectations.”\\nBroadcom ( NASDAQ:AVGO ) benefits through custom ASICs. Q2 AI semiconductor revenue was $10.8 billion, +143% YoY, with Q3 guided to $16.0 billion. Bloomberg Intelligence forecasts 27% CAGR for the custom ASIC market through 2033.\\nGoogle ( NASDAQ:GOOGL ) is both a Rubin customer and a TPU competitor. Google Cloud revenue rose 63% with backlog nearly doubling to over $460 billion, and 2026 capex is guided up tp $190 billion. A Kyber slip strengthens the in-house silicon case.\\nNVIDIA has not confirmed the delay. The affected product is a 2028 item; Blackwell and current Rubin systems remain in short supply. NVIDIA still commands approximately 81% of the AI chip market , and GuruFocus analysis flags NVDA as 45% undervalued relative to GF Value at current prices. Hyperscalers cannot swap vendors overnight.\\nI've spent more than 15 years inside enterprise software, working alongside the finance, sales operations, and HR leaders who run the revenue engines at some of the largest tech companies in the country.\\nMy day job is helping enterprise executives make smarter decisions about retention, compensation, and growth. These are the same operational levers that show up in every earnings report investors actually read. That perspective shapes my writing for 24/7 Wall St.\\nThe headline numbers are easy. The interesting stuff is underneath: how companies make money, what executives are worried about, and what any of it means for the person checking their 401(k) on a Sunday afternoon. I write about personal finance and business as someone who has spent her career inside the rooms where these decisions get made.\\nJensen Huang just staked his credibility on a single word to silence delay concerns about Vera Rubin, and Wall Street is already reworking its price assumptions in response.\"}\n{\"article\": 28, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"developer.nvidia.com\", \"title\": \"Maximize Spectral Efficiency with AI-Native RAN and NVIDIA AI Aerial\", \"text\": \"By Cindy Goh\\nNVIDIA AI Aerial enables an algorithms-first approach that shifts RAN design from fitting CPU compute budgets to maximizing spectral efficiency through AI-native Layer 1 and Layer 2 models.\\nGPU acceleration unlocks higher-order spatial multiplexing, with ML-based beamforming delivering up to 1.62x throughput gains over traditional zero-forcing methods in 64T64R massive MIMO scenarios.\\nDeep reinforcement learning link adaptation achieves 1.3x throughput improvement over outer-loop link adaptation at the cell edge while maintaining latency below the 30-microsecond RAN budget.\\nAcademic research validates these gains, including a 20% spectral efficiency improvement from pilotless spatial multiplexing and up to 70% throughput increase for practical DRL link adaptation in high-mobility conditions.\\nThe platform supports integrated sensing and communications workloads and enables operators to monetize off-peak GPU capacity by hosting edge AI inference applications.\\nNVIDIA is collaborating with Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia anyRAN software for AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution.\\nExplore the NVIDIA AI Aerial platform to build, simulate, and deploy AI-native networks from research to commercial deployment.\\nLearn more about commercial Aerial RAN Computer (ARC)-Pro with NVIDIA RTX Pro 4500 Blackwell Server Edition GPU.\\nSee how Nokia is enabling AI-native 5G-Advanced and 6G networks.\\nSpectrum is one of the most valuable assets in wireless communications. Over the last 30 years, telecom operators in the US have spent more than $240B to acquire wireless spectrum . A goal of a radio access network (RAN) system is to extract the maximum spectral efficiency (bits/second/Hertz) possible, which translates into more capacity, stronger network resilience with fewer dropped packets, and better economics per site.\\nAs operators look for ways to extract more value from existing spectrum, Massive MIMO (Multiple-input, multiple-output) has become an important method for increasing capacity and improving how efficiently the network serves users.\\nMassive MIMO promised a revolutionary leap in spectral efficiency. However, in field deployments, the industry is operating below what the technology can theoretically achieve, leaving immense capacity unused. The root causes are fundamentally system-level problems. The network struggles to accurately track user locations, signals overlap and cause interference, and the system fails to pair users efficiently for simultaneous data transmission.\\nThe industry has examined these challenges through a “compute-constrained” lens, treating compute as a scarce resource, forcing compromises to fit complex algorithms within the strict limitations of CPU power and performance budgets.\\nNVIDIA AI Aerial is changing that paradigm. With parallel computing, compute is no longer the bottleneck for operating within existing power budgets. By embracing an AI-native, highly parallelized architecture, we don’t have to ask, “How can I squeeze more into the same compute?” Instead, we ask, “How can we reinvent the algorithm across the full stack to maximize the spectrum?”\\nThis “algorithms-first” approach enables the network to finally run the complex tracking and pairing models required to close the massive MIMO performance gap.\\nThis blog describes the benefits of GPU acceleration in the RAN, and how AI-native RAN helps close the massive MIMO performance gap. It shows how NVIDIA AI Aerial enables a new class of Layer 1 and Layer 2 algorithms designed to unlock greater spectral efficiency in real-world deployments.\\nWhy GPU acceleration benefits spectral efficiency\\nModern RAN pipelines consist of mathematically dense algorithmic tasks, with highly specific compute characteristics. A scheduler that evaluates a wider combinatorial space, a channel estimator that learns from richer observations, or a beamformer that adapts jointly across users can improve throughput and quality of service for user applications. Table 1 is a breakdown of the highest-impact RAN workloads, their compute characteristics, and why GPU acceleration is required to execute them effectively:\\nThe following sections review two workloads—beamforming and link adaptation—and explore how GPUs unlock new spectral efficiency gains.\\nBeamforming quality determines how effectively a network converts channel knowledge into usable throughput. Classical methods are effective but are shaped by computational compromise, relying on simplified models to meet limited budgets. As systems move to higher antenna counts and denser MU-MIMO operation, these simplifications result in lost signal-to-interference-plus-noise ratio (SINR) and lower realized throughput.\\nML-based beamforming weight generation solves this by using richer channel information for weights, though it raises the compute burden sharply. NVIDIA analysis shows that in a 64T64R MU-MIMO scenario with 16 users and 2 layers per user, where users are randomly assigned SNRs in the [0-20] dB range, AI beamforming requires significantly more FLOPs than traditional regularized Zero Forcing (rZF), but this increase in compute produces a spectral efficiency gain of up to 1.62x higher throughput at 32 layers compared to the traditional zero-forcing beamforming algorithm.\\nEven at a lower number of layers, ML-beamforming delivers 1.28x throughput improvement.\\nGPU compute enables higher-quality beam weight generation at the scale and latency required for RAN deployment. This connects directly to field validation. SoftBank and NVIDIA recently reported stable outdoor 16-layer massive MU-MIMO operation on a GPU-based AI-RAN platform. The trial delivered roughly 3x the spectral efficiency of a conventional 4-layer baseline. GPU value is not abstract acceleration; it is the practical ability to sustain higher-order spatial multiplexing in a real system.\\nLink adaptation is a complex MAC-layer control function. The scheduler must repeatedly pick a modulation and coding scheme (MCS) to maximize throughput while keeping block error rates (BLER) near targets for QOS and maximizing spectral efficiency under changing mobility and interference conditions.\\nTraditional link adaptation schemes, such as outer-loop link adaptation (OLLA) implemented in vector engines in CPUs, are lightweight, reacting to feedback through hand-crafted predefined logic. Deep reinforcement learning (DRL) link adaptation changes this by learning the MCS-selection policy directly from observed radio behavior.\\nA DRL agent uses channel quality indicator (CQI) history, ACK/NACK feedback, and short-term channel evolution to adapt to site-specific conditions in real time. Early NVIDIA engineering results show a 1.3x throughput gain over baseline OLLA at the cell edge when combined with channel-orthogonality-based user pairing. Further improvements are expected when DRL-LA is combined with more advanced MU-MIMO pairing algorithms.\\nThe technical turning point is latency scaling. A small, distilled model can run on a CPU equipped with vector engines. The highest spectral efficiency gains require a larger model handling richer MU-MIMO conditions at large batch sizes, while maintaining stable BLER below target.\\nAs shown in Figure 3, the GPU-based architecture stays below the ~30 μs typical reference budget across the tested user range, even for a 396K-parameter model. In contrast, a single-core CPU implementation exceeds the latency budget starting with the first scheduled user. CPU inference can support only a low-complexity model, while GPU inference enables higher-capacity models that deliver greater performance gains. Link adaptation becomes a true spectral efficiency lever only when policy quality and inference latency improve together.\\nRecent academic studies also find the same result. ML-based channel estimation and equalization, and link adaptation outperform classical baselines under difficult radio conditions. Below are the summaries from three separate research papers:\\n20% spectral efficiency improvement\\n67% BLER reduction compared to OLLA in the URLLC scenario\\nUp to 70% throughput increase over classical OLLA in a high-mobility scenario\\nNVIDIA AI Aerial does more than accelerate RAN workloads; it enables new network capabilities. The following five capabilities—including improved spectral efficiency—highlight how the platform advances next-generation wireless networks.\\n1. Algorithm-first efficiency\\nInstead of relying on simplified heuristics to fit narrow CPU budgets, AI Aerial enables operators to run mathematically dense, AI-native Layer 1 and Layer 2 models. Aerial shifts the design target from “what fits on the CPU?” to “what maximizes radio performance?” by evaluating massive combinatorial spaces in real time.\\n2. Model growth without redesign\\nAs AI moves closer to the physical waveform, models for channel estimation, equalization, and adaptation will grow. Rigid ASICs freeze algorithms in silicon, but the programmable tensor compute headroom of a GPU enables AI to live at the heart of the RAN. AI Aerial absorbs the growth in software rather than forcing a new hardware partition every time the algorithm changes.\\n3. Scale and coordination\\nNext-generation networks require dense, overlapping coverage, creating a massive computational burden for cross-cell interference tracking. Traditional CPU schedulers struggle with the latency scaling required for this level of coordination. AI Aerial handles this through massive memory bandwidth and shared data spaces between cuPHY and cuMAC, executing complex multi-cell math instantly to support higher-order MU-MIMO implementations\\n4. Integrated sensing and communication\\nIntegrated Sensing and Communications (ISAC) essentially turns a radio network into a ubiquitous radar system. This introduces a workload that requires processing standard communications while simultaneously executing deep-learning classification models. AI Aerial provides a parallel architecture that blends high-throughput communications with sensing.\\n5. Monetize AI infrastructure\\nTelecom networks are typically provisioned for peak traffic, leaving infrastructure severely underutilized during off-peak hours. AI Aerial changes the return on assets by supporting the dynamic allocation of 5G/6G and AI workloads on the same GPU. Operators can reallocate spare GPU compute to host monetizable edge inference applications, transforming dedicated telecom equipment into revenue-generating infrastructure for the broader AI economy.\\nThe shift to AI-native RAN is the path to closing the massive MIMO performance gap, giving the network the intelligence and parallel compute to turn theoretical spectral efficiency into real-world gains.\\nNVIDIA AI Aerial was built for this transition. As a software-defined, accelerated computing platform for AI-native RAN, it makes advanced Layer 1 and Layer 2 intelligence practical at scale. The result is a more capable RAN that helps operators unlock more value from every Hertz and opens the door to new AI-driven revenue opportunities.\\nNVIDIA is working with industry leaders like Nokia to deliver AI-RAN platforms integrated with Nokia’s anyRAN software, enabling operators to deploy AI-native 5G-Advanced networks ready for 6G evolution. With platforms like NVIDIA ARC-Pro enhancing performance, efficiency, and programmability, operators can accelerate their path to fully intelligent, software-defined radio networks.\\nLearn more:\\nExplore the NVIDIA AI Aerial platform to build, simulate, and deploy AI-native networks, from research to commercial deployment.\\nCindy leads Product Marketing for AI-RAN and 6G at NVIDIA, driving the strategy for the next generation of intelligent wireless networks. With a deep foundation in semiconductor innovation, she previously led Technical Marketing and IP Product Management at Intel and Altera. Cindy holds both an M.S. and B.S. in Electrical Engineering from the University of Southern California.\"}\n{\"article\": 29, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"developer.nvidia.com\", \"title\": \"Develop Humanoid Robot Policies End-to-End with NVIDIA Isaac GR00T | NVIDIA Technical Blog\", \"text\": \"By Edith Llontop and Brandon Neel\\nThe NVIDIA Isaac GR00T Development Platform unifies every stage of the humanoid robot pipeline, from data collection and model training to large-scale evaluation and deployment readiness.\\nGR00T 1.7 is the first open, commercially usable vision-language-action model for generalized humanoid skills, released under the Apache 2.0 license with a 3-billion-parameter base checkpoint.\\nThe model introduces a Cosmos-Reason2-2B (Qwen3-VL architecture) backbone, robust pretraining on ~32K hours of real and ~8K hours of simulated data, and full ONNX and TensorRT export support.\\nBenchmarks show consistent improvements over N1.6, including DROID-F0 (+10%), DROID-F6 (+61%), SimplerEnv Bridge (+5%), and Fractal (+2%).\\nAn end-to-end simulation workflow demonstrates environment setup with Isaac Lab-Arena, demonstration collection via Isaac Teleop and CloudXR, LeRobot format conversion, post-training with frozen language model, and closed-loop policy evaluation.\\nThe platform is being adopted by humanoid makers including 1X, Agility, ANYBotics, and Skild AI, research institutions such as Stanford and ETH Zurich, and XR device makers including HTC Vive and PICO.\\nAs more teams move from humanoid robot bring-up to task-specific skill development, the need for repeatable development workflows is growing. Building humanoids remains complex, and today’s development pipelines are still highly fragmented. As a result, developers spend significant time configuring robotics infrastructure before they can focus on building robot capabilities.\\nWhile the robotics community has developed capable tools across individual stages of the pipeline, connecting them into a seamless workflow remains a challenge due to siloed software ecosystems, incompatible data formats, and manual integrations between tools. To address this, we introduce the NVIDIA Isaac GR00T Development Platform : an open source humanoid robot platform that unifies every stage of the pipeline for faster development.\\nThis post covers the end-to-end GR00T platform, from data collection and model training to large-scale evaluation and deployment readiness, showcasing how a fully integrated platform can streamline humanoid development.\\nAccelerate end-to-end humanoid development\\nThe Isaac GR00T platform helps robotics developers scale humanoid data collection and simulation-based training to develop, validate, and deploy policies on real robots. Its end-to-end platform is fully open and modular, enabling teams to use individual components or the complete pipeline, integrate their own tooling, and build using the validated NVIDIA software stack.\\nCreate a simulation environment for data generation, policy training, and testing.\\nCapture high-quality robot demonstration data for policy training and development.\\nNVIDIA Isaac GR00T 1.7 + Training Scripts\\nTrain robot policies for humanoid reasoning and multitask behavior using sim and real demonstration data.\\nTest and evaluate robot policies in simulation before real-world deployment.\\nExport the model into a deployable LEAPP bundle and deploy the post-trained policies to robots for real-time, on-device inference and control.\\nA key component of the GR00T workflow is the open Isaac GR00T 1.7 vision-language-action (VLA) model, which streamlines development through a strong pretrained foundation. Trained on diverse data, it can efficiently generalize to new tasks and environments while producing more natural, human-like motion.\\nGet started with GR00T 1.7\\nGR00T 1.7 is the first open, commercially usable VLA model for generalized humanoid robot skills, released under the permissive Apache 2.0 license. It’s a cross-embodiment model that takes multimodal input, including language and images, and produces the actions needed to perform tasks across diverse environments. Instead of training a policy from scratch, developers can start from a model that already encodes broad manipulation priors and adapt it to specific robots, tasks, and environments through post-training.\\nWhat’s new in GR00T 1.7\\nRobust Human Video Pretraining: 1.7 is pretrained on ~32K hours of real demonstration and human ego-centric data as well as ~8K hours of simulated rollouts and demonstrations from BEHAVIOR, RoboCasa, and Simulated GR-1 for more human-like action.\\nNew VLM backbone: Cosmos-Reason2-2B (Qwen3-VL architecture) replaces the Eagle backbone used in GR00T N1.6. This helps support flexible resolution and encodes images in their native aspect ratio without padding.\\nExpanded deployment support: Adds full pipeline export to ONNX and TensorRT, with improved export reliability and higher-frequency updates.\\nEnhanced performance: Improved long-horizon task reasoning through task- and subtask-level decomposition, increasing reliability, motion quality, and cross-embodiment generalization.\\nImproved benchmarks: Consistent improvements across DROID and SimplerEnv compared to N1.6, including DROID-F0 (+10%) and DROID-F6 (+61%), as well as SimplerEnv Bridge (+5%) and Fractal (+2%), demonstrating stronger generalization.\\nGR00T 1.7 is accessible via GitHub and Hugging Face , with model weights publicly available and a base checkpoint of 3 billion parameters.\\nUsing the GR00T 1.7 model in the end-to-end GR00T workflow streamlines post-training and policy evaluation, simplifying the transition from foundation model to deployed robot behavior. The following section details the steps for using GR00T 1.7 in the simulation workflow.\\nUsing GR00T platform and 1.7 model for a dexterous manipulation task\\nThis section walks through a simulation workflow for building a simple pick-and-place task using the GR00T platform and the GR00T 1.7 model, from environment setup to deployment readiness. This is an excerpt from our end-to-end GR00T development guide , which covers the full process in more detail.\\nThe walkthrough includes:\\nPost-training GR00T 1.7 with the converted dataset\\nAlthough this section focuses on simulation, the same steps can be applied to physical robots and adapted across different embodiments and tasks.\\nBefore collecting any training data, you must first set up the environment. Compose the scene with object and robot assets, define the task, and choose the teleoperation device.\\nIn this example (see Figure 3, above) , the robot stands in front of a shelf and uses its arms to move an apple onto a plate on the same shelf. A Whole Body Controller (WBC) keeps the robot balanced; since the robot doesn’t walk during this task, use AGILE WBC, a single end-to-end policy that fits a static task better than a stand/walk controller.\\nChoose the WBC deliberately up front: during teleoperation, the AGILE WBC and PinkIK produce the joint-space targets that become the policy’s training signal, so a different controller yields a different training distribution for the same motion.\\nAt a high level the implementation looks like the following code snippet. These snippets use the Isaac Lab-Arena APIs to compose the scene, register assets, and wire up the teleoperation device. These are just snippets; for the entire environment set-up, go to GitLab Pages .\\nbackground = self.asset_registry.get_asset_by_name(\\\"galileo_locomanip\\\")()\\npick_up_object = self.asset_registry.get_asset_by_name(args_cli.object)()\\ndestination = self.asset_registry.get_asset_by_name(args_cli.destination)()\\nembodiment = self.asset_registry.get_asset_by_name(args_cli.embodiment)(\\nself.device_registry.get_device_by_name(args_cli.teleop_device)()\\nif args_cli.teleop_device is not None else None\\nscene = Scene(assets=[background, pick_up_object, destination])\\nOnce you’ve set up your environment, you can use Isaac Teleop to capture demonstration data, with AGILE WBC handling whole-body control during collection. Demonstrations are collected using a supported VR headset over a CloudXR client. After starting the CloudXR runtime and sourcing the CloudXR environment, record demonstrations with:\\n--dataset_file $DATASET_DIR/arena_g1_static_apple_dataset_recorded.hdf5 \\\\\\n--num_demos 400 \\\\\\n--num_success_steps 10 \\\\\\ngalileo_g1_static_pick_and_place \\\\\\n--object apple_01_objaverse_robolab \\\\\\n--destination clay_plates_hot3d_robolab \\\\\\nQuality matters as much as quantity here. Aim for clean demonstrations with smooth motions, stable grasps, and diverse approach directions, since noisy or inconsistent data limits how well the policy can learn. For this task, we collected 400 trajectories not all at once but stitched together through multiple sessions of data collection. You can definitely start with a smaller batch of data to understand how the pieces fit together before committing to a full set of demonstrations.\\nSuccessful demonstrations are saved to an HDF5 file, which becomes the raw input for the next stage, where it’s converted to the LeRobot format that GR00T 1.7 consumes for post-training.\\nGR00T 1.7 consumes datasets in LeRobot format, so the recorded HDF5 is converted inside the Arena container before training. The conversion is driven by a config file, g1_static_apple_config.yaml , which maps the recorded fields to what GR00T expects:\\ndata_root: /datasets/isaaclab_arena/static_apple_tutorial\\nhdf5_name: \\\"arena_g1_static_apple_dataset_recorded.hdf5\\\"\\nlanguage_instruction: \\\"move the apple to the plate\\\"\\ntask_index: 3\\nstate_name_sim: \\\"robot_joint_pos\\\"\\naction_name_sim: \\\"processed_actions\\\"\\npov_cam_name_sim: \\\"robot_head_cam_rgb\\\"\\nfps: 50\\nchunks_size: 1000\\nRun the conversion with:\\npython isaaclab_arena_gr00t/lerobot/convert_hdf5_to_lerobot.py \\\\\\n--yaml_file isaaclab_arena_gr00t/lerobot/config/g1_static_apple_config.yaml\\nThis produces a lerobot folder containing parquet files with states and actions, MP4 camera recordings, and dataset metadata. Now with a converted dataset you can post-train GR00T 1.7 model.\\nPost-train GR00T 1.7\\nPost-training runs outside the Arena container, in a standalone checkout of the Isaac-GR00T repo. The fine-tuning command tunes the visual backbone, projector, and diffusion model while keeping the language model frozen:\\nuv run python -m torch.distributed.run --nproc_per_node=1 --standalone \\\\\\ngr00t/experiment/launch_finetune.py \\\\\\n--base-model-path nvidia/GR00T-N1.7-3B \\\\\\n--dataset-path $DATASET_DIR/arena_g1_static_apple_dataset_recorded/lerobot \\\\\\n--output-dir $MODELS_DIR/static_apple_n17_finetune \\\\\\n--modality-config-path /path/to/IsaacLab-Arena/isaaclab_arena_gr00t/embodiments/g1/g1_sim_wbc_data_gr00t_n_1_7_config.py \\\\\\n--global-batch-size 12 \\\\\\n--max-steps 20000 \\\\\\n--num-gpus 1 \\\\\\n--save-steps 5000 \\\\\\n--save-total-limit 5 \\\\\\n--dataloader-num-workers 8 \\\\\\n--color-jitter-params brightness 0.3 contrast 0.4 saturation 0.5 hue 0.08\\nAfter post-training, evaluation is just two steps: load your checkpoint into a GR00T server, then run the policy in a closed loop. Arena uses a server-client setup, where the server hosts your fine-tuned model and the Arena client runs the simulation and queries the model from the server.\\nRun the policy by launching the client from the Arena container:\\n--policy_type isaaclab_arena_gr00t.policy.gr00t_remote_closedloop_policy.Gr00tRemoteClosedloopPolicy \\\\\\n--policy_config_yaml_path isaaclab_arena_gr00t/policy/config/g1_static_apple_gr00t_closedloop_config.yaml \\\\\\n--remote_host <SERVER_HOST> --remote_port 5555 \\\\\\n--num_steps 600 \\\\\\n--embodiment g1_wbc_agile_joint\\nMetrics print to the console as evaluation is in progress until you get a final output that tells you the performance of your post-trained policy.\\n[Rank 0/1] Metrics: {'success_rate': 1.0, 'object_moved_rate': 1.0, 'num_episodes': 1}\\nTo change how much you evaluate, adjust a few flags. --num_steps sets the rollout length; 600 is a quick smoke test corresponding to about one episode for this task. For a representative success rate, evaluate complete episodes instead with --num_episodes 100 (or 1000 for a stronger estimate), and add --num_envs 5 to run environments in parallel for more randomization across trials. Everything else stays the same; you’re only changing how many rollouts you average over.\\nThat completes a full pass through the Isaac GR00T Development Platform. Each stage produces a clean artifact that feeds directly into the next for a fully integrated, streamlined development process. While this walkthrough uses a specific embodiment as an example, the same process can be adapted to your own robot setup, define a task for your use case, collect demonstrations on your system, and reuse the same data conversion, post-training, and evaluation workflow.\\nA growing GR00T ecosystem\\nThe GR00T platform and reference workflow are already being adopted across a growing ecosystem of humanoid robotics partners.\\nHumanoid makers and AI providers including 1X, Agility, ANYBotics, Bellboy Robotics, FieldAI, Lightwheel AI, NEURA Robotics, Nexuni, Noble Machines, Schaeffler, Skild AI, and Techman Robot are integrating components of the GR00T platform such as Isaac Teleop, Sim, Lab, and ROS to accelerate their development pipeline and bring AI-enabled robots into real industrial use faster.\\nResearch institutions including Stanford, CMU, UCSD, ETH Zurich, and AI2 will be experimenting with the unified GR00T end-to-end workflow to reduce integration complexity and move faster from robot bring-up to skill development and real-world validation.\\nLeading wearable and XR device makers including Haptikos, Manus, Noitom, OpenGraph Labs, PICO, Sensing, HTC Vive, Wuji, and Xsens offer devices with native support for NVIDIA Isaac Teleop, making it easier to capture high-quality demonstrations.\\nThe GR00T platform and GR00T 1.7 model are available today for any developer to use. Whether starting in simulation or on a physical robot, the workflow remains the same. Select an entry point, define a bounded task, and build your first end-to-end policy.\\nGetting started can be challenging, so we have built a complete end-to-end reference workflow, which is provided in the learning content How to Develop and Deploy Humanoid Robots End-to-End with NVIDIA Isaac GR00T , along with the GR00T Platform Introduction Video .\\nAdditional resources:\\nNVIDIA Isaac GR00T Development Platform\\nEdith Llontop is a robotics solution architect at NVIDIA. She has a degree in electrical engineering and computer science from the University of California, Berkeley, and has experience doing robotics research at Berkeley’s Artificial Intelligence Research Lab. She now works on supporting customers with Isaac, the NVIDIA robotics platform.\\nBrandon Neel is a senior product marketing manager at NVIDIA, where he focuses on NVIDIA Isaac technologies for humanoid robot development. With experience across digital twin development, open models, and sim-to-real workflows, he is driven to make AI more accessible to developers across the robotics ecosystem.\"}\n{\"article\": 30, \"day\": \"2026-07-07\", \"publisher\": \"cnbc.com\", \"title\": \"Chinese lidar maker with Nvidia ties accused of being cyber risk for U.S.\", \"text\": \"Hesai Technology, a Shanghai-based manufacturer of the sensors that allow robots and self-driving vehicles to see, was blacklisted as a national security threat in 2024 by the U.S. Department of Defense, which designated Hesai as a Chinese military entity.\\nIts presence on the blacklist does not prevent U.S. companies from using Hesai's technology, and the company has been expanding its reach in the U.S., including via a partnership with Nvidia.\\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO, says his company does not work for the Chinese military and its products are safe.\\nRobots on the factory floor. Self-driving vehicles on the Las Vegas strip. Even a substitute for man's best friend — the robotic dog.\\nThey are all part of the physical artificial intelligence buildout that depends on high-tech, low-cost lidar, the critical sensors that allow these technologies to see their surroundings.\\nAnd at the heart of this buildout is Hesai Technology , a Shanghai-based lidar manufacturer blacklisted as a national security threat in 2024 by the U.S. Department of Defense, which designated Hesai as a Chinese military entity . While the blacklist prevents Hesai and the 187 other companies and subsidiaries on the list from securing Pentagon contracts, there is nothing illegal about using these products in nonmilitary applications. Hesai's presence on the blacklist does not prevent U.S. companies from using Hesai's technology.\\nGovernment officials and security experts say the use of Chinese lidar could open this new, critical infrastructure to cyberthreats with potentially serious consequences and become a backdoor for Beijing to access sensitive data collected by the lidar technology.\\nDavid Li , Hesai's co-founder and CEO, says the narrative that his company poses a threat is fiction.\\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO.\\n\\\"In the DOD case, I don't feel there is sufficient evidence, and it's not logical,\\\" Li said. \\\"We are frustrated by that.\\\"\\nIn his first extended interview about the blacklist designation, Li defended the company against allegations that its technology poses national security risks or could be used by the Chinese government to collect data.\\nDavid Li, Hesai's co-founder and CEO, speaks to CNBC's Melissa Lee in his first extended interview about his company's blacklist designation by the U.S. Department of Defense.\\nDespite the federal blacklist designation, Hesai's reach is growing. Under an expanded partnership between Hesai and Nvidia , Hesai sensors will be one of the options automakers can choose to integrate into Nvidia's autonomous vehicle platforms, which the chipmaker hopes will power the self-driving vehicle revolution.\\n\\\"Our vision is that some day, every single car, every single truck will be autonomous. And we have been working towards that future,\\\" Nvidia CEO Jensen Huang said in his keynote address at the Consumer Electronics Show in January, when the expanded partnership was announced.\\nHuang has said that robotics, which includes self-driving cars, is the company's second most important growth category after artificial intelligence. In its latest annual filing, Nvidia reported automotive revenue for fiscal year 2026 was up 39% from a year prior, driven by the adoption of its self-driving platforms.\\nHesai is one of the dominant suppliers in the global autonomous technology ecosystem. Its sensors are integrated into several autonomous systems in addition to Nvidia's, including those of Amazon's robotaxi company, Zoox; autonomous trucking companies such as Waabi and Kodiak; autonomous vehicle technology company Nuro; and agricultural automation firm Agtonomy. The sensors can also be found at New York's John F. Kennedy International Airport, where they monitor passenger and traffic flow at security checkpoints and gate entrances, and even in autonomous lawn mowers.\\nNot everyone is convinced that these data-collecting sensors should be integrated into U.S. autonomous systems.\\nCraig Singleton is a senior director for the China Program at the Foundation for Defense of Democracies, a conservative Washington-based think tank known for being critical of the Chinese government. His research has concluded that there are security risks in Chinese-made sensors operating in U.S. systems, including that lidar sensors could enable Beijing \\\"to access sensitive U.S. data or disrupt critical operations.\\\"\\nLidar — which stands for \\\"light detection and ranging\\\" — works by firing laser pulses and measuring how long it takes for them to bounce off an object and back to the sensors. The sensors then combine thousands of these measurements to create a \\\"point cloud,\\\" or three-dimensional map, which allows autonomous machines to see and navigate their surroundings.\\nSingleton told CNBC that as Chinese lidar sensors become more prolific across the U.S., they will move closer to defense nodes, utility grids and airports.\\n\\\"That data is so sensitive and it's so precise that it could be weaponized by a hostile foreign power if they ever wanted to target our infrastructure,\\\" Singleton said.\\nQuestions about how and where Chinese-made lidar sensors are being used come alongside broader concerns about Chinese government oversight and the potential for that government to access data collected by Chinese companies.\\nThe U.S. Securities and Exchange Commission requires all China-based companies to disclose \\\"the risk of Chinese government intervention or control.\\\" In its SEC filings, Hesai has disclosed that the Chinese government has \\\"significant oversight in regulating our operations and may influence or intervene in our operations at any time.\\\"\\nSingleton said that in part means Hesai can be compelled to share data collected by its lidar sensors with the Chinese government.\\n\\\"Whether they want to transmit that information or not isn't a question, it's mandated by law,\\\" Singleton said.\\nLi said the company's sensors hold no data because they lack the memory capacity to do so. He said Hesai's partners are responsible for securing the data the sensors collect and that Hesai has no control over that.\\nLi also rejected concerns that the Chinese government could access data through the company.\\nBut Singleton said the rapid deployment of autonomous systems is outpacing scrutiny over potential security risks.\\n\\\"It's a tale as old as time with Chinese tech,\\\" he said.\\nCompanies in the U.S. have previously embraced low-cost Chinese technology, even from entities that had been blacklisted, only to later spend billions replacing it after the products raised national security concerns.\\nChinese telecommunications giant Huawei, for example, was placed on the Defense Department's blacklist in 2021, but that did not stop U.S. companies from using Huawei products. The Federal Communications Commission, which had designated Huawei a \\\" national security threat \\\" in 2020, forced U.S. companies to \\\" rip and replace \\\" Huawei products from their networks beginning in July 2021. Huawei challenged both the decision to bar it from securing federal contracts and FCC's designation of the company as a national security threat in court, but lost both cases.\\nLike Huawei, other Chinese companies have been found by the Defense Department to be national security risks only after their products made their way into U.S. homes and businesses.\\nDJI, the world's largest drone maker, and Wi-Fi router maker TP-Link Technologies, both based in Shenzhen, China, sold popular consumer goods. DJI was blacklisted in 2022 by the Pentagon for its ties to the Chinese government, and TP-Link Technologies was added to the list in June 2026.\\nDJI sued the Defense Department in 2024 to be removed from the blacklist, but a federal judge ruled there was \\\"substantial evidence\\\" that the company contributes to China's defense industrial base. DJI is appealing the decision.\\nUnlike Hesai, which remains free to sell lidar sensors commercially in the United States, a separate FCC ruling in December banned DJI from selling new products to U.S. consumers due to national security risks, though existing models remain legal to use.\\nTP-Link Technologies was also barred from selling new models after the FCC in March determined foreign-made routers pose \\\"an unacceptable risk to the national security of the United States.\\\"\\nNational security concerns surrounding foreign-made lidar sensors have also spurred academic research on the potential risks posed by these devices.\\nMiroslav Pajic, a professor at Duke University who studies vulnerabilities in lidar sensors, told CNBC it's \\\"easy to physically spoof lidar.\\\"\\nMiroslav Pajic, a professor at Duke University who studies vulnerabilities in lidar sensors.\\nPajic said any lidar sensor can be compromised with malware inserted at the factory during production or through firmware updates. Malware can be difficult to detect, since automakers and other manufacturers usually cannot access a lidar maker's proprietary source code and malware can remain dormant until triggered.\\nInside his lab at Duke, Pajic demonstrated one such attack.\\nOn a computer monitor displaying a lidar sensor's point cloud image, the room the sensor was capturing appeared exactly as expected. The sensor mapped its surroundings in real time, creating a 3-D picture of the space.\\nBut after Pajic activated malware embedded in the lidar unit, a person appeared in the sensor's point cloud. In reality, nobody had entered the room.\\nThe system generated a phantom person — a false object created entirely through manipulated sensor data.\\nPajic's lab has also conducted demonstrations that have the opposite effect: manipulating lidar data to remove real objects from a sensor's view. In that scenario, an autonomous system could fail to detect a pedestrian, vehicle or obstacle that is physically present.\\nPajic said similar attacks could theoretically be used against autonomous vehicle fleets operating in cities, causing them to malfunction.\\nWhen asked about the simulation, Li said a system can be designed to be vulnerable in a lab setting.\\nBut the risk of malfunction isn't just theoretical.\\nMichael Robbins, CEO of the Association for Uncrewed Vehicle Systems International — a trade organization that represents companies in the industry, including U.S. lidar competitors — said that in 2024 Hesai pushed a firmware update to all its lidar sensors. The firmware didn't take into account that 2024 was a leap year, so on Feb. 29, all of Hesai's lidar sensors stopped working.\\nMichael Robbins, CEO of the Association for Uncrewed Vehicle Systems International.\\n\\\"In that case it was by error, but that could also be done intentionally, where every lidar in use in the United States could be turned off, or it could be used against us in a nefarious way,\\\" Robbins said.\\nWhile the potential for a software error across autonomous vehicles is widespread, an error in lidar systems is grave considering its implementation in cars across America.\\nLi said that in the case of the leap year incident there was an overlooked coding bug in the firmware — not malware. He added that Hesai publishes all its firmware as open source data, so it can be publicly analyzed. In a statement to CNBC, Hesai said the issue was fixed within 24 hours.\\n\\\"We are making ourselves transparent on what exactly this is able to do,\\\" he said.\\nLi also said autonomous systems are designed with other sensors such as cameras and radar systems that can compensate for lidar failures.\\n\\\"If any of them stop, the cars will have to reevaluate the situation to know whether it's safe enough to continue the course or we're gonna have to pull over,\\\" he said.\\nHesai's sensors have also met the standards set by Tüv Rheinland and Dekra, which are independent third-party companies that specialize in product testing, safety validation and cybersecurity assessments.\\nHesai, which is publicly listed on both the Nasdaq and the Hong Kong Stock Exchange, is one of the world's biggest lidar manufacturers. It has one-third of the global automotive lidar market, the company told CNBC in a statement.\\nAnd autonomous driving is projected to become a massive global market. McKinsey & Company estimates the market potential for autonomous driving will be roughly $300 billion to $400 billion by 2035.\\nHesai's footprint inside the U.S. autonomous ecosystem has continued to grow.\\nCNBC reached out to Hesai's U.S. partners about their relationship with the company.\\nCNBC asked Nvidia more than a dozen questions, including whether it was aware that Hesai had been blacklisted by the Pentagon and what safeguards are in place to protect the data the sensors collect.\\nNvidia did not respond to CNBC's specific questions and instead provided a statement:\\n\\\"Automakers worldwide demand an open, vendor-agnostic reference architecture, so they can select components that are best for the markets they serve to build the safest cars. Our NVIDIA DRIVE Hyperion architecture provides that flexibility, ensuring that American industry competes worldwide, consistent with all regulatory and commercial requirements.\\\"\\nIn a statement, Kodiak wrote that its technology is designed \\\"so that Hesai does not have access to the data from their sensors or any data produced by Kodiak's autonomous system.\\\"\\nA spokesperson for Waabi wrote that its \\\"autonomous trucks utilize an array of sensors\\\" and that it does not \\\"comment on or disclose specific hardware being tested or used in our autonomous vehicles.\\\" The spokesperson added, \\\"We have rigorous data security protocols in place and thoroughly vet all third-party hardware to ensure the absolute integrity and safety of our systems as well as compliance with all applicable laws and regulations.\\\"\\nAgtonomy, Nuro and Zoox did not respond to CNBC's request for comment.\\nHesai's expansion has been driven in part by pricing.\\nHesai told CNBC it has reduced the cost of its lidar units from more than $10,000 each to less than $200. By comparison, U.S. lidar manufacturer Aeva told CNBC its automotive sensors cost \\\"in the few hundreds of dollars\\\" per unit.\\nIndustry analysts say that the pricing advantage is reshaping the market. A 2025 automotive lidar report by the Yole Group, a global advisory and market analysis firm, said Chinese firms such as Hesai are \\\"dominating due to cost, scale and government support\\\" while Western players \\\"face higher costs and slower adoption.\\\"\\nIn a statement to CNBC, Hesai said it's among the first in the industry to mass produce its lidar systems due to its \\\"innovation\\\" and \\\"automotive manufacturing capability.\\\"\\nCritics say those lower prices are only possible because of Chinese government assistance.\\n\\\"Chinese lidar companies have benefited from massive unfair state subsidies that have allowed them to scale production and control the market,\\\" said Singleton, of the Foundation for Defense of Democracies.\\nLi denied his company receives support from the Chinese government.\\n\\\"That's an accusation with no evidence,\\\" he said. \\\"When you see a player being able to build sensors at a much more affordable level, you just assume that they get help.\\\"\\nAccording to Hesai's 2025 annual filing with the U.S. SEC, the company received Chinese government subsidies, preferential tax rates of 15% versus the standard 25%, preferential borrowing rates below benchmark, and a tax break that lets it deduct 200% of its research and development costs.\\nIn a statement to CNBC, Hesai said those programs are not unusual, that \\\"governments worldwide commonly offer tax incentives to technology enterprises as a standard measure to stimulate innovation\\\" and that these incentives are \\\"broadly available to all qualifying companies in China, both domestic and foreign.\\\"\\nHesai also wrote that \\\"no government organization, including the Chinese government, holds any equity stake in Hesai.\\\"\\nAt the center of Hesai's legal battle with the Pentagon is whether the company contributes to China's military-civil fusion strategy, a national initiative aimed at integrating civilian and military technological development. The Pentagon says Hesai is part of that ecosystem.\\nAfter the Defense Department blacklisted Hesai in January 2024, the company sued the department in federal court over the designation, in May 2024.\\nIn court filings, the Defense Department cited several factors in support of the designation. Among them is that Hesai's Chinese headquarters are in Shanghai's Jiading district, an area associated with military-civil fusion initiatives.\\nLi rejected that argument. He said a military-civil fusion zone has not been clearly defined and that several of the companies in the area are American companies.\\n\\\"For lack of a better analogy, just because the Pentagon is in Virginia, you think that Virginia is a place full of military,\\\" Li said. \\\"Anybody operating in the state of Virginia becomes military.\\\"\\nThe Pentagon also pointed to supplier relationships between Hesai and China Electronics Technology Group Corporation, or CETC, a state-owned defense conglomerate tied to the People's Liberation Army. In a prospectus Hesai submitted for possible listing on the Shanghai Stock Market, CETC is listed as its third-largest supplier.\\nLi said none of the parts Hesai purchased from CETC have a military application.\\nIn court, Hesai also said its sensors are \\\"solely for commercial and civilian uses.\\\"\\nLi also told CNBC that Hesai's commercial agreement strictly prohibits customers from using its devices in military applications. However, he also said that, like any piece of hardware, once sensors leave the factory, it is impossible to physically control where they end up.\\nHesai lost its lawsuit against the Defense Department in 2025 and remains on the blacklist. The company is appealing the decision.\\nBeyond the courtroom, the company is also facing scrutiny from international policy groups.\\nIn July 2025, the Prague Security Studies Institute — a nonprofit, nongovernmental public policy organization — published a white paper on Hesai. Among the findings: Hesai's lidar sensor appeared on a military vehicle during a 2023 Chinese military television program . The program is called \\\"Land Unmanned Systems Challenge,\\\" a Chinese unmanned systems competition hosted by China's Army Equipment Department and organized by a military research institute.\\nChinese defense materials describing the event say its goal is to advance China's military-civil fusion strategy. Participants included military and civilian universities, state-owned enterprises, private companies and research institutes testing unmanned vehicles.\\nLi confirmed that the sensor that appeared in the footage was Hesai lidar technology but said the company did not supply the sensor for the military competition.\\n\\\"We shipped probably hundreds of thousands of lidars like this,\\\" Li said. \\\"So, there is a possibility that they end up somewhere that we are completely unaware of in the aftermarket.\\\"\\nLi also said Hesai has no way to communicate with or trace the sensors once they are shipped out, that the sensors send the data to the company using them, not to Hesai.\\nOn Capitol Hill, lawmakers are raising alarms about the use of Chinese lidar technology in the United States, warning the sensors could create both cybersecurity and national security risks.\\n\\\"These sensors and the ability to transmit information is a huge concern,\\\" said U.S. Rep. John Moolenaar, R-Mich., chairman of the House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party. The committee's website says that it is \\\"committed to working on a bipartisan basis to build consensus on the threat posed by the Chinese Communist Party.\\\"\\nU.S. Rep. John Moolenaar, R-Mich., chairman of the House Select Committee on the Chinese Communist Party.\\nSome of the committee's work has focused on protecting U.S. automakers from Chinese competition.\\nThe committee has proposed legislation that would phase out Chinese-made lidar technology in the United States, arguing that the Chinese Communist Party could otherwise exploit a fast-growing and strategically important industry.\\nIn May, Moolenaar introduced legislation that would ban Chinese vehicles from U.S. roads.\\nLi said his company's sensors are not capable of storing data and therefore could not transmit information to China. But Moolenaar said he is skeptical of those assurances, arguing that Chinese technology has been known to have back doors.\\n\\\"We've seen back doors in robots that would transmit information back to these Chinese Communist interests,\\\" he said.\\nNo proposed legislation or existing laws currently prohibit blacklisted Chinese companies from being listed on U.S. stock exchanges or prevent American investors from buying their shares.\\nMoolenaar said companies focused on growth and shareholder returns may not prioritize national security concerns in the same way policymakers do.\\n\\\"We need to consider what implications there are when we merge these Chinese technologies with American technologies,\\\" he said. \\\"Often legislation takes a while to catch up.\\\"\\nClarification: This article was updated to clarify that TP-Link Technologies, based in Shenzhen, China, was added to the Defense Department's blacklist in June 2026.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_5","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'import json; d=json.load(open(\\\"docs/research/2026-11-14-use-acceptance/packet-A1.json\\\")); [print(json.dumps(x,ensure_ascii=False)) for x in d if 1 <= x[\\\"article\\\"] <= 10]'\"","aggregated_output":"{\"article\": 1, \"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"publisher\": \"moomoo.com\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA is about to start taking a share of cloud providers' revenue.\", \"text\": \"It has been reported that, in an effort to break the monopoly of tech giants, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees for emerging cloud providers by offering GPU computing capacity lease-back arrangements to address their financing challenges. In exchange, NVIDIA will take a revenue share from these cloud providers, directly participating in profit distribution in the downstream computing market and extending its control further down the industrial chain.\\nNVIDIA is leveraging its strong balance sheet to gain market leverage, quietly evolving from a chip vendor into the 'central bank' of the AI computing ecosystem by offering financial backing to emerging cloud service providers in exchange for a share of their revenue.\\nOn July 1, according to the technology news outlet The Information, NVIDIA is providing financial guarantees to young cloud service providers that lease or rent its GPUs—if these companies fail to secure enough AI developers to rent their computing capacity, NVIDIA will repurchase their unsold GPU capacity at a pre-agreed price.\\nIn return, NVIDIA will receive a percentage of these cloud providers’ revenue, with the revenue-sharing rate gradually declining over the term of the contract. GPU cloud providers Firmus and Sharon AI have already joined the program, and three executives with existing business ties to NVIDIA confirmed the arrangement.\\nOn July 1, NVIDIA published a statement on its official website announcing a new business model that combines revenue sharing with credit support, enabling AI cloud providers to procure NVIDIA infrastructure without bearing the full upfront capital expenditure, and allowing them to offer computing services to downstream AI-native enterprises, model developers, and corporate clients.\\nAccording to the report, the initiative is internally referred to by some NVIDIA personnel as the 'AI Compute Partnership.' A NVIDIA spokesperson also confirmed the existence of the program. This move marks a significant strategic shift for NVIDIA:\\nOn one hand, it lowers financing barriers for emerging cloud service providers to expand its customer base; on the other, it directly participates in profit distribution within the downstream computing market through revenue sharing, thereby extending its control further down the AI value chain.\\nBusiness Model Shift: From Selling Chips to Sharing Cloud Revenue\\nAccording to NVIDIA’s official press release, the company will earn not only standard product revenue but also an additional share of cloud service revenue, creating a usage-based, recurring revenue stream. The core objective of this model is to overcome the long-standing financing barrier that has constrained startups from accessing large-scale computing power.\\nNVIDIA positions this framework as the 'DSX AI Factory' model, targeting AI service scenarios requiring continuous cross-regional operations, high utilization rates, and multi-tenant accelerated computing.\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first cloud providers participating in this model. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 NVIDIA Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, while Firmus is constructing a DSX AI Factory campus in Batam, Indonesia, expected to scale up to 360 megawatts and accommodate up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs. These deployments directly reflect NVIDIA’s latest progress in transforming computing demand into deployable, financeable infrastructure.\\nNVIDIA noted that emerging AI companies have historically faced significant constraints in accessing capital-intensive infrastructure—even long-term contractual commitments have often been insufficient to secure financing for compute capacity procurement. This means that numerous AI-native firms, model developers, and inference service providers face lengthy delays when scaling their computing capabilities: site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware commissioning—each step can take several months or even longer.\\nThe promise of this new model is to realign economic structures so these groups can rapidly access full-stack accelerated computing capabilities without waiting for the traditional infrastructure build-out cycle to complete.\\nUnderlying Rationale: Addressing the Core Challenge of GPU Financing\\nReportedly, GPUs typically represent the most expensive component in AI data centers. For chip buyers with lower credit ratings, securing sufficient loans is itself a major hurdle.\\nA data center executive commented on such NVIDIA deals as “killing two birds with one stone.” He explained that if NVIDIA merely provided a guarantee for a data center facility lease, “you’d still face the question of ‘how to finance the GPUs’”; but if NVIDIA commits to purchasing any unsold compute capacity within the facility, “the GPU financing issue is resolved—and so is the data center’s financing problem.”\\nIn other words, NVIDIA’s backstop commitment effectively serves as a credit enhancement tool, enabling emerging cloud service providers—who would otherwise struggle to obtain bank loans—to mobilize significantly larger amounts of capital and accelerate data center construction.\\nStrategic Intent: Breaking the Dominance of Large Customers\\nNVIDIA’s launch of these initiatives is grounded in a clear strategic context. Currently, a small number of large cloud service providers—including Amazon, Microsoft, SpaceX, Oracle, Meta, and Google—purchase the majority of NVIDIA’s chip output. However, several of these companies are developing their own competitive AI chips, posing a potential threat to NVIDIA.\\nTo reduce its reliance on these giant clients, NVIDIA has spent the past few years actively supporting a cohort of emerging GPU cloud service providers, such as CoreWeave. The newly announced 'AI Compute Partnership Program' represents a continuation and deepening of this strategy.\\nAccording to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA has also recently been negotiating to provide financial guarantees for OpenAI’s lease of a large data center in Ohio—an asset that, if fully built out at current prices for chips, labor, power, and other materials, could cost as much as $500 billion.\\nCapital Commitments: From Equity Investments to Capacity Guarantees\\nNVIDIA’s capital commitments in this direction have already become substantial.\\nTo date, NVIDIA has invested several billion dollars in equity stakes in multiple emerging cloud service providers and, in certain cases, agreed to lease back chips from these companies—including CoreWeave and Lambda—in transactions totaling several billion dollars. According to prior reporting by The Information, NVIDIA’s own researchers have also used GPU servers leased back from Lambda.\\nOn the capacity guarantee front, NVIDIA began advancing related deals in the autumn of last year. In September 2024, NVIDIA committed to purchasing all of CoreWeave’s unsold capacity through 2032 if CoreWeave failed to secure tenants, under a contract then valued at $6.3 billion. This move effectively alleviated investor concerns about CoreWeave’s highly leveraged business model and drove its stock price up nearly 30% within the following week.\\nAccording to a regulatory filing submitted by NVIDIA in May of this year (covering the quarter ended in April), NVIDIA subsequently added another $3.5 billion to guarantee customer data center leases in exchange for rights to purchase their shares.\\nTaken together, NVIDIA is constructing a multi-layered mechanism to align interests: equity investments, capacity leasebacks, lease guarantees, and now revenue-sharing arrangements. Each layer deepens the financial ties between NVIDIA and downstream cloud service providers, enabling NVIDIA to directly share in the incremental returns from the commercialization of AI computing power beyond chip sales alone.\"}\n{\"article\": 2, \"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"publisher\": \"tradingkey.com\", \"title\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\", \"text\": \"Intel Price Forecast: Nvidia Picked Xeon 6, Invested $5B, Yet Analysts Still Trail INTC\\nIntel Corporation is demonstrating significant momentum, trading at $140.05 amid robust AI-driven demand. Core catalysts include NVIDIA’s $5 billion equity investment and the selection of Intel’s Xeon 6 for DGX Rubin AI systems, alongside multi-year, volume-locked agreements with Google and Microsoft. Q1 2026 data shows strong revenue growth in Data Center/AI and Foundry segments, with AI-linked revenue reaching 60% of the mix. While currently trading above consensus analyst targets, the valuation is supported by evolving commercial fundamentals. Technically, the stock remains in a neutral-bullish trend, with a potential move toward $159.90 if it clears $150.40 resistance.\\nTradingKey - Intel Corporation (NASDAQ: INTC ) sits at $140.05, holding firm on the ascending trendline within the 2H timeframe. The RSI indicator is currently reading 55.21, positioning it as neutral-bullish, while green candles continue to soak up sell orders in close proximity to the trendline-resistance alignment. This rally translates into a ~465% gain for the company's stock over the prior 12 months, climbing from a $18.97 low, a climb so massive that the 12-month average analyst price target of $93.93 now sits 33% behind the actual price of shares, creating an enduring wedge between where analysts have modelled the stock to end and where they find it today.\\nIntel’s July investment thesis does not hinge on the Apple deal or Trump’s social media post, topics I’ve written about in the past few issues; rather, it turns on the fact that NVIDIA invested $5B into Intel as well as opted for Intel Xeon 6 as the host CPU in their DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems. Put another way, it’s the most important GPU company of our day endorsing Intel’s CPU.\\nJensen Huang Picked Intel — What the Xeon 6 Selection for DGX Rubin Means\\nNVIDIA could have gone with any CPU to accompany their Rubin GPU, but they went with Intel Xeon 6 to power the DGX Rubin NVL8, the AI system that will power the data centre for the next two to three years. CEO Lip-Bu Tan recently said Xeon 6 now plays the role of “the orchestration layer and critical control plane for the entire AI stack” and highlighted that the CPU:GPU ratio for inference is beginning to shift from 1-to-8 to 1:1, meaning the ratio of CPUs needed to support the GPU is actually increasing, not decreasing (the prior narrative was that GPUs would ultimately take over the CPU role).\\nJensen Huang’s decision to build the DGX Rubin NVL8 on Xeon 6 coupled with their $5B equity investment in Intel represents the strongest signal yet from a third party that Intel’s CPU is competitive. This is not an Apple rumor or an announcement on social media: This is a CPU architecture decision from the world’s largest infrastructure company in AI.\\nQ1 2026 showed the beginning of the revenue impact of this AI CPU positioning, with Data Center and AI reaching $5.1B (up 22% year-over-year), and non-GAAP EPS reaching $0.29 while GAAP was negative $0.73 (this was after heavy foundry investment charges). Meanwhile, Intel Foundry saw revenue of $5.4B (up 16% year-over-year), which translates to a Foundry segment that is now over $20B in annualised revenue (still negative earnings but not quite the rounding error it was). CFO David Zinsner characterized the quarter as having “unprecedented demand for silicon” and Q2 guidance is $13.8 to $14.8B, indicating continued sequential revenue strength. About 60% of this quarter's revenue mix now comes from AI-linked sources, compared with a little less than 25% a year ago.\\nThe Google Deal — Multiyear, Locked Pricing, Custom ASIC Co-Development\\nGoogle's Q1 2026 announcement is the second major confirmed hyperscaler commitment that brings contractual revenue certainty to the Intel thesis. Google inked a multiyear deal for continued use of Intel Xeon processors in Google's workload-optimized instances, including the newest Xeon 6 processors that power C4 and N4 instances. The companies also are partnering on custom-designed ASIC Infrastructure Processing Unit (IPU) chips that will help boost utilization, lower system complexity, and scale more efficiently for AI tasks.\\nCEO Tan said the agreements span three to five years, with locked volume and pricing. That represents a rare and tangible commitment in an industry to provide revenue visibility for the term. The structure directly addresses the main criticism of Intel's revenue run-rate so far this year, which is that demand is not real but order-driven (front-loaded).\\nAdd to the list of commitments NVIDIA's $5B equity commitment plus Xeon 6 product selection, Google's multi-year Xeon and custom ASIC deal, and Microsoft's previously confirmed 18A wafer deal, plus Apple foundry negotiations that are ongoing.\\nThis deal stack is where the current price stands relative to the average price target of $93.93 on the stock from analyst coverage. This gap represents how fast the new Intel story is unfolding versus the rate at which sell-side analysts are updating their models: Cantor Fitzgerald raised its price target to $150 from $90 in early July 1 and still held a Neutral rating noting its price target remains a mere 13.9% above current levels.\\nCurrent valuation checks (per Simply Wall St) show Intel trades at a price-to-sales multiple of 13.1x vs. a suggested fair price multiple of 15.1x given its combination of foundry + AI exposure, and scores 2 of 6 on its broader valuation checks. While INTC is not cheap on an absolute basis, it's not obviously expensive when compared to the group of peers and the confirmed customer stack.\\nINTC Technical Setup — Trendline Defense at $140, RSI 55.21, Target $159.90\\nOn the 2H chart, INTC is holding the ascending multi-touch black trendline after a series of rejections of the descending red trendlines from the $169 plus highs and is holding above the EMAs200 at $106.07 with higher lows. RSI at 55.21 is neutral-bullish with room to rise above before becoming overbought and does not have any divergence, as buyer absorption of sell orders (seen on green candles) near the trendline-resistance confluence.\\nResistance overhead sits at $150.43 to $159.97. If INTC holds the trendline, and confirms a move above $150.40, the next price target is $159.90. If it breaks down below $126.20, the next price target is $117.07.\\nEntry: Long above $150.40 — trendline and resistance cleared\\nTarget: $159.90 — trendline bounce extension\\nStop Loss: Close below $126.20 — ascending trendline fails\\nNVIDIA selection: Xeon 6 chosen as host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8; $5B equity investment\\nGoogle deal: Multiyear, locked pricing 3–5 years, custom ASIC IPU co-development\\nNext earnings: July 23, 2026\\nWhy Did NVIDIA Invest $5 Billion in Intel and Select Xeon 6 for Its AI Systems?\\nNVIDIA allocated $5B of equity to Intel and chose Intel's Xeon 6 as the host CPU for its DGX Rubin NVL8 AI systems, the AI product to set the benchmark for data centre AI systems for the next product cycle? Here's the explanation from Lip-Bu Tan: \\\"The CPU has become the orchestration layer, the control plane of the entire AI infrastructure. In inference workloads, we're starting to shift from 1 CPU to 8 GPUs towards 1 CPU to 1 GPU.\\\" Jensen Huang's choice of Xeon 6 is a product architecture decision, by the company that's the benchmark for defining AI infrastructure, not an investment, or a promotion, from NVIDIA: Jensen's selection of Xeon 6 is the best possible validation of Intel's CPU competitive position.\\nWhat Did Intel Report in Q1 2026 and What Is Q2 Guidance?\\nIn Q1 2026, Data Center and AI (DC&A) was $5.1 billion (+22% YoY) and Foundry was $5.4 billion (+16% YoY). Non-GAAP earnings per share was $0.29 per share, compared with $0.73 (GAAP) because of foundry capital investment charges for 18A. Intel expects Q2 to continue expanding sequentially with revenues of $13.8 billion to $14.8 billion. \\\"AI-linked revenue is now approximately 60% of the quarterly mix, as compared with a year ago when it was under 25%,\\\" said CFO David Zinsner. The quarter was defined by unprecedented demand for silicon and Intel's determination to use the entire factory network as a means to maximize output. \\\"Our long-term deals span three to five years, with locked volumes and pricing,\\\" said CFO Zinsner.\\nWhy Is the Average Analyst Target at $93.93 When Intel Trades at $140?\\nThat's a 33% difference, which shows how rapidly the Intel story has evolved relative to the time required for sell-side equity research analysts to update their models. Cantor Fitzgerald increased its 12-month price target to $150 on July 1 while keeping a Neutral rating, stating that the new price target was 13.9% above the current stock price. The majority of Wall Street firms haven't yet updated their financial models to account for the NVIDIA $5B equity investment, the Google multiyear deal, and a full risk production announcement for 18A. The Simple Wall St analysis places \\\"fair value\\\" Price/Sales of 15.1 vs. 13.1, which suggests that Intel is not significantly overvalued vs. peer group P/S at current trading levels.\\nIntel is up 465% over the past year and now trades 33% above the consensus analyst 12-month price target of $93.93. That gap exists because the story, now anchored around NVIDIA's investment and product choice, the Google and Microsoft signed deals, has moved a long distance relative to the ability of sell-side research analysts to update models. Those commercial agreements are no longer a rumour.\\nThe Intel story now includes a $5 billion equity investment from NVIDIA for Xeon 6 as the host CPU for DGX Rubin NVL8, a Google multiyear agreement with locked volume and pricing (3-to-5-year deployment and co-development of Intel custom AI accelerators (ASIC)), and Microsoft has signed a deal for wafers using 18A technology.\\nQ1 2026 was Data Center and AI $5.1 billion (+22%), with an average of 60% AI-linked of the mix. Technicals are a defense at $140, with the average ascending trendline, plus RSI at 55.21, neutral-bullish. Confirmation above $150.40 will send prices to a $159.90 price target. A stop at $126.20 would protect against a failure. Q2 2026 guidance to be issued at July 23 earnings is the next checkpoint, where we'll hopefully see a large component of the stack materialize in guidance.\"}\n{\"article\": 3, \"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"publisher\": \"sg.finance.yahoo.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia offers revenue sharing model for aspiring AI start-ups\", \"text\": \"Nvidia offers revenue sharing model for aspiring AI start-ups\\nThe aim of its new revenue-sharing model is to build business among researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources.\\n(July 2): Nvidia Corp is looking to expand access to its leading AI hardware by granting token credits to developers in exchange for a share of their future sales.\\nThe company at the heart of the AI boom will help connect AI data centre operators with cloud service providers, simplifying the process for each side and establishing a new recurring \\\"usagelinked earnings stream\\\" for itself, chief financial officer Colette Kress wrote in a blog post on Wednesday. The aim of its new revenue-sharing model is to build business among researchers and nascent companies without the capital to access large-scale AI resources.\\nUnder the company's branding of DSX AI factories, an aspiring AI start-up can access the cloud compute resources of a provider like Firmus Technologies with greater speed and flexibility than would otherwise be the case, Kress said.\\n\\\"For model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up,\\\" she wrote.\"}\n{\"article\": 4, \"day\": \"2026-07-01\", \"publisher\": \"NVIDIA Corporation\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unlocks AI Compute at Scale, Inviting Partners to Power the AI Infrastructure Buildout | NVIDIA Blog\", \"text\": \"NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories, aligning economics through a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\nAs AI moves from model development to production inference, compute demand is accelerating and shifting toward continuously operating AI factories that generate tokens at scale. This shift requires access to large‑scale, multi‑tenant accelerated computing that can come online quickly, stay highly utilized and support the economics of token‑scale AI services.\\nEmerging AI companies historically have had limited access to capital-intensive infrastructure, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing for compute.\\nTo address this, NVIDIA is introducing a new business model that opens up compute access to the fast‑growing AI ecosystem of startups, model builders, enterprises, research organizations and regional AI players.\\nThis new model enables AI clouds to procure NVIDIA infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model. Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell NVIDIA-powered cloud services, with NVIDIA earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of NVIDIA platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides NVIDIA with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\nFor model builders, inference providers, agent platforms and enterprises scaling AI, it can mean faster access to full-stack accelerated computing without waiting through site selection, power procurement, construction and hardware bring-up.\\nThe initiative is already taking shape, with AI cloud companies building DSX AI factories designed to serve customers and workloads across regions.\\n“This strategic collaboration with NVIDIA marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI’s mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,” said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI.\\nFirmus is building a DSX AI factory campus in Batam, Indonesia. The campus is expected to scale to 360 megawatts and up to 170,000 NVIDIA GPUs.\\n“AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,” said Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies. “Firmus AI cloud is building a NVIDIA DSX-aligned AI factory, which will enable our cloud to help more customers access the compute they need to build and scale AI.”\\nAI natives such as Baseten, Fireworks AI and Together AI show where compute demand is headed: they need immediate access to AI cloud capacity to run model training, post-training, fine-tuning and high-volume agentic inference for developers, digital natives and enterprises building with AI.\\nTheir customers need reliable access to large-scale NVIDIA accelerated computing as usage grows, but they also need commercial flexibility as products move from pilot to production.\"}\n{\"article\": 5, \"day\": \"2026-07-02\", \"publisher\": \"english.cw.com.tw\", \"title\": \"Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe｜Daily News Digest｜2026-07-02｜web only\", \"text\": \"Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe\\nJuly 2, 2026 -- Today’s top stories: Singapore Seizes $42M Mansion in Nvidia Chip Smuggling Probe, Rubio Would Step Aside for Vance in 2028 White House Race, and Google Ordered To Pay Klarna $1.97b in Antitrust Damages.\\nBy CommonWealth Magazine\\nSingapore seizes $42m home in Nvidia chip smuggling case\\nSingapore authorities have seized a multimillion-dollar mansion and charged people with money laundering as part of a case of suspected cross-border smuggling of hardware containing Nvidia chips.\\nA prohibition of disposal order was issued against the luxury home, valued at about SGD 55M ($42M), freezing the asset and preventing its purchase or sale. Funds in bank accounts under investigation, valued at about SGD 1M, were also seized.\\nLim Jenny and Woon Guo Jie Aaron, two people suspected of involvement in the alleged smuggling, face money laundering charges. Four firms allegedly linked to the export of computer servers that might contain Nvidia chips to Malaysia are facing charges for false representation.\\nThe companies are Aperia International, A-Speed Infotech, and Aperia Cloud Services (II) — collectively known as the Aperia group — and Luxuriate Your Life.\\nThis comes after a US investigation into whether Chinese start-up Deepseek had circumvented US restrictions on advanced Nvidia chips by buying them from third parties in other countries, including Singapore.\\nLuxuriate Your Life was charged on July 1, while the other three firms will be charged on July 6.\\nThree individuals were also hauled to court on July 1 and charged with various offences, including fraud and money laundering.\\nRubio would step aside for Vance in 2028 White House race\\nUS Secretary of State Marco Rubio will not run for the Republican nomination for US president in the 2028 election if Vice President JD Vance announces such an intention, according to the Associated Press.\\nRubio has repeatedly mentioned in conversations with his advisers and aides his intention to make way for Vance.\\nIn recent months, Rubio, while serving as Secretary of State and assistant to the President for national security affairs, has significantly strengthened his influence in the administration by appointing his supporters to key positions on the White House National Security Council.\\nThe outlet also notes that Rubio and Vance are developing different approaches to implementing US President Donald Trump's foreign policy and defense agenda.\\nusnews - Vance and Rubio Take Different Approaches as Iran Tests Their 2028 Prospects\\napnews - Vance and Rubio take different approaches as Iran tests their 2028 prospects\\nnewsukraine - Rubio would step aside for Vance in 2028 White House race\\nGoogle ordered to pay Klarna $1.5bn antitrust damages\\nGoogle has been ordered by a Swedish court to pay $1.97Bn in damages to Klarna after the buy now, pay later company won an antitrust case alleging that the search giant engaged in anticompetitive practices.\\nKlarna’s price-comparison unit, Pricerunner, sued Google in 2022 in a Swedish court.\\nPricerunner alleged that Google was giving preferential treatment to its own price-comparison service in its search results. The case relates to Google’s actions in Pricerunner markets in the UK, Sweden, and Denmark between 2008 and 2023.\\nThe antitrust damages mark a partial win for Klarna, which had sought “ significantly higher ” compensation of close to $8Bn, the court said on Wednesday.\\n“ Pricerunner is considered to have suffered damage as a result of Google having illegally favoured its price comparison service for many years, ” the Stockholm Patent and Market Court said in a statement. The case relates to Google’s actions in Pricerunner markets in the UK, Sweden, and Denmark between 2008 and 2023.\\nSouth Korea to Invest Nearly $1.2 Trillion in Chips and AI Data Centers\"}\n{\"article\": 6, \"day\": \"2026-07-02\", \"publisher\": \"tomshardware.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\", \"text\": \"Nvidia offers to take a cut of AI cloud revenue on top of hardware sales in new optional financing vehicle — trades tokens for revenue cut\\nBy Luke James\\nSharon AI and Firmus are the first partners, with a combined total of up to 210,000 GPUs.\\nNvidia has announced a new business model under which it’ll be able to double-dip for revenue on the same silicon: once when its partner AI clouds use its hardware, and again as an ongoing percentage of the revenue that hardware generates. In a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress, the company pitched the “revenue-sharing and credit-support model” as a way to open compute access to startups that can’t finance it themselves. In practice, cash-poor AI companies trade a slice of whatever they eventually earn for tokens today, while a supplier already running roughly 75% gross margins reaches into its customers’ income statements for a second helping of cash. Australia's Sharon AI and Singapore-based Firmus Technologies are the first named partners.\\nUnder the structure, participating AI clouds procure Nvidia infrastructure and sell Nvidia-powered cloud services to end customers. Nvidia collects its usual product revenue on the hardware plus a percentage of the cloud income earned on that capacity, which the blog post describes as a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream. Per Bloomberg , developers receive token credits in exchange for a slice of their future sales, but neither Nvidia nor its partners has disclosed the revenue-split percentages.\\nIt’s no secret that the credit-support side of this model will help to address a financing gap that Nvidia itself has identified. Even signed, long-term customer commitments have failed to convince lenders to fund large-scale deployments, leaving smaller clouds unable to borrow against the demand they had already generated.\\nIn an 8-K filing dated June 12th, Sharon AI disclosed that the agreement runs for six years and covers 72 MW of new Australian data center capacity built to Nvidia's DSX AI factory design, scaling to as many as 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. The Nasdaq-listed neocloud separately holds a revenue-share facility of up to $200 million with investor Digital Alpha, disclosed in its CY25 results, meaning portions of its income are now pledged in two directions. Meanwhile, Firmus is building a DSX-aligned campus in Batam, Indonesia, that’s expected to scale to 360 MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs.\\nNvidia has spent much of the last year funnelling cash directly to its customers, including a $30 billion participation in OpenAI's $110 billion funding round and backing for xAI's $20 billion Colossus 2 financing , arrangements that drew repeated circular financing criticism. The new model inverts that: rather than investing capital that returns as GPU orders, Nvidia extends credit support and collects a royalty on its partners’ sales for years afterward.\\nThat royalty also ties a slice of Nvidia's income to utilization instead of hardware sales. If partner clouds can’t keep racks rented, the usage-linked stream shrinks, a live concern given the depreciation pressure already building on operators paying off hardware that Nvidia refreshes pretty much every year.\\nRevenue? All these startups are or will be horribly unprofitable, the lengths one takes to ensure they can keep buying leather jackets.\"}\n{\"article\": 7, \"day\": \"2026-07-02\", \"publisher\": \"mitrade.com\", \"title\": \"NVIDIA Unveils New AI Compute Model, But Michael Burry is Shorting Its Stock\", \"text\": \"NVIDIA is expanding its AI infrastructure business with a new model designed to accelerate the deployment of computing capacity across global cloud providers.\\nThe move arrives as Michael Burry increases bearish positions against NVIDIA, creating a sharp debate over AI growth prospects.\\nNVIDIA (NVDA) Price Performance. Source: TradingView\\nNVIDIA’s new AI compute framework allows cloud providers to deploy advanced hardware using revenue-sharing and credit-support agreements. The goal is to reduce infrastructure barriers for startups, enterprises, model developers, and regional AI operators.\\nThe company earns revenue from hardware sales and from cloud usage generated by supported capacity. This approach aims to accelerate the construction of large-scale AI factories capable of serving inference workloads and token-intensive applications.\\nAI is shifting from model training to always-on token production, and that shift demands a new business model.NVIDIA is partnering with AI clouds to deploy large‑scale, multi‑tenant AI factories through revenue-sharing and credit-support. This opens up compute access to the… pic.twitter.com/PSQdTQJTga\\n— NVIDIA (@nvidia) July 2, 2026\\nThe strategy addresses one of the industry’s biggest challenges: the enormous capital required to build AI infrastructure. By helping partners expand capacity faster, NVIDIA hopes to increase utilization rates while making advanced computing resources more accessible.\\nEarly participants illustrate the scale of the initiative. Sharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. Meanwhile, Firmus is developing a major campus in Indonesia that could support approximately 170,000 GPUs and 360 megawatts of power capacity.\\nWhy is Michael Burry Betting Against NVDA\\nDespite NVIDIA’s continued momentum, some investors remain skeptical about how long current AI-driven valuations can be sustained. Among the most prominent bears is Michael Burry , the investor known for predicting the 2008 housing market collapse.\\nBurry’s latest move goes beyond a general warning about the sector. He disclosed a direct short position on NVIDIA at approximately $198.09 per share, while also establishing bearish positions against Tesla , Applied Materials, Caterpillar, and the iShares Semiconductor ETF (SOXX).\\n🔥NEW: 'BIG SHORT' MICHAEL BURRY BETS AGAINST NVIDIA, TESLA AND CATERPILLARBurry disclosed new shorts against $NVDA , $TSLA , $CAT and $AMAT , calling the semiconductor index \\\"a pure form of overvaluation\\\" that is \\\"rarely seen,\\\" per Business Insider.He shorted Caterpillar for… pic.twitter.com/qNJXMQRdV4\\n— Coin Bureau (@coinbureau) July 2, 2026\\nHis thesis is centered on what he views as excessive enthusiasm surrounding artificial intelligence. Burry argues that massive investments in data centers, chips and AI infrastructure may be creating conditions similar to previous technology bubbles.\\nHe has specifically raised concerns about rapid hardware obsolescence, aggressive capital spending by hyperscalers, and the possibility that demand growth could eventually slow.\\nSupporters of NVIDIA see the situation differently. They point to strong demand for AI inference, which is accelerating enterprise adoption and strengthening the company’s dominant position in advanced computing. NVDA traded near $195 at the time of writing, giving the chipmaker a market value of roughly $4.77 trillion.\\n$NVDA Simplistically what this means for neoclouds,in my view, is it gives them certainty but takes away some of the upside. I think that’s a good deal for them.Also reduces $NVDA reliance on hyper scalers. So $META news yesterday almost irrelevant now too https://t.co/Xpz1WfCHhx\\n— SixSigmaCapital (@SixSigmaCapital) July 2, 2026\\nThe result is a growing divide on Wall Street. NVIDIA’s bulls believe the company remains at the center of a multi-year AI expansion cycle, while Burry is positioning for a scenario in which expectations have outpaced economic reality. The coming quarters could determine which view gains the upper hand.\\nThe coming quarters may provide important answers for both bulls and bears. NVIDIA’s success depends on executing its AI factory vision and maintaining strong demand for current and future platforms, including Blackwell and Rubin-based systems.\\nInvestors will closely monitor earnings results, cloud partnership expansion, and progress on infrastructure deployment. If adoption continues accelerating, NVIDIA could further strengthen its position at the center of the global AI ecosystem.\\nHowever, valuation concerns are unlikely to disappear. Any slowdown in spending, infrastructure utilization, or enterprise demand could increase volatility.\\nThe result is a high-stakes contest between technological transformation and concerns that expectations may have outpaced economic reality.\\nDisclaimer: The content presented above, whether from a third party or not, is considered as general advice only. CFD trading involves significant risk of loss. Past performance does not guarantee future results. This article serves informational purposes only and does not constitute financial advice. Consider your risk tolerance before trading.\\nWeekly Market Wrap: Meta’s $145 billion AI bill hit chips — as Brent surged past $116 Meta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116. Explore this week’s key market moves, investor concerns and what Australian traders should watch next.\\nMeta’s US$145 billion AI spending plan puts chip stocks in focus as Brent crude surges past US$116. Explore this week’s key market moves, investor concerns and what Australian traders should watch next.\"}\n{\"article\": 8, \"day\": \"2026-07-02\", \"publisher\": \"datacenterdynamics.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia acts as backstop for customer GPUs in return for cut of cloud revenue\", \"text\": \"By Georgia Butler\\nNvidia is supporting neocloud customers in purchasing its AI chips.\\nThe chip designer is acting as a financial backstop for companies in exchange for a share in their revenue.\\nThe backstop sees Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate. Firmus and Sharon AI are among the first companies to have adopted the offer.\\nFirmus is deploying 170,000 GPUs in Batam, Indonesia, while Sharon AI is deploying 40,000 GB300 GPUs.\\nIn a post detailing the new offering, Nvidia said: \\\"This new model enables AI clouds to procure Nvidia infrastructure for AI-native, enterprise, and ISV customers through economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model.\\n\\\"Through the partnership, AI clouds will sell Nvidia-powered cloud services, with Nvidia earning both standard product revenue and a share of the cloud revenue on the supported capacity. This structure accelerates adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector, and provides Nvidia with a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream.\\\"\\nThis is not the first time Nvidia has signed deals like this, having previously done so with the likes of CoreWeave and Lambda in 2025.\\nThe agreement with CoreWeave was signed in September 2025 and had an initial value of $6.3bn , while the deal with Lambda was valued at $1.5bn .\\nContracts like these ensure that Nvidia maintains a diversified customer base and also expands its internal compute capacity for researchers when GPUs are left idle.\\nThe newer contracts have the added benefit of generating income for Nvidia based on the neoclouds' success, effectively creating a win-win situation.\"}\n{\"article\": 9, \"day\": \"2026-07-02\", \"publisher\": \"qz.com\", \"title\": \"Nvidia launches revenue-sharing model for AI startups\", \"text\": \"The chipmaker will earn a share of cloud revenue from partners, with two data center operators named as its first participants\\nNvidia $NVDA announced a revenue-sharing program on Wednesday designed to give AI startups, model builders, and enterprises access to its computing infrastructure without requiring them to finance hardware buildouts themselves.\\nRevenue from both product sales and cloud operations flows back to Nvidia under the arrangement, as AI cloud companies provide Nvidia -powered services to their end customers. Kress, writing in a blog post, called the resulting income a \\\"recurring, usage-linked earnings stream\\\" for Nvidia ; on the customer side, she said the model removes the need to navigate site selection, power procurement, construction, and hardware bring-up before accessing large-scale compute.\\nToken credits are also part of the offering, giving startups a way to access compute resources without upfront capital outlays.\\nNvidia named two initial partners. Sharon AI will deploy up to 40,000 Nvidia Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs. A campus being developed by Firmus Technologies in Batam, Indonesia is planned at a capacity of up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs and 360 megawatts of power. The company said AI-native firms including Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI represent the kind of customers the program is designed to serve.\\n\\\"This strategic collaboration with Nvidia marks a pivotal moment in Sharon AI's mission to deliver sovereign, large-scale AI compute infrastructure,\\\" said James Manning, cofounder and CEO of Sharon AI, in a statement.\\n\\\"AI-native companies need access to scalable, energy- and cost-efficient compute infrastructure to compete globally,\\\" Tim Rosenfield, co-CEO of Firmus Technologies, said in a statement.\\nNvidia framed the program as a response to a structural shift in AI demand, from model development toward production inference workloads that require continuously operating infrastructure at scale. The company said emerging AI companies have historically had limited access to capital-intensive compute resources, with even long-term commitments insufficient to unlock financing.\\nThe move comes as Nvidia contends with growing competition from custom AI chips developed by large cloud providers. Companies including Alphabet $GOOGL and Amazon $AMZN have been offering access to their own processors, putting pressure on Nvidia to build a broader customer base beyond its largest buyers. Nvidia stock was down 0.5% in premarket trading Thursday.\\nThe new model represents a deeper level of engagement than the equity stakes Nvidia has taken in neocloud operators like CoreWeave and Nebius.\"}\n{\"article\": 10, \"day\": \"2026-07-03\", \"publisher\": \"mlq.ai\", \"title\": \"Nvidia Launches GPU Backstop Financing Model, Takes Cut of Cloud Revenue From Neocloud Partners\", \"text\": \"Nvidia will act as a financial backstop for neocloud GPU deployments, agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate in exchange for a share of cloud revenue generated on that capacity [1]\\nSharon AI plans to deploy up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs in Australia under a six-year agreement; Firmus Technologies targets up to 170,000 GPUs in a 360MW DSX-aligned facility in Batam, Indonesia [1] [3]\\nThe revenue-share percentage was not disclosed; Nvidia declined to provide details beyond its blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [3]\\nThe structure gives Nvidia both standard hardware revenue and a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream — a shift from pure equipment sales toward platform-level economics [1]\\nSHAZ shares fell 14.2% to $67.91 on July 3; NVDA dipped 1.4% to $194.83 on a $4.7 trillion market cap [5]\\nNvidia on July 1 unveiled a new financing vehicle it calls the \\\"AI Compute Partnership,\\\" under which the chipmaker acts as a financial backstop for neocloud customers' GPU deployments and, in return, takes a recurring share of the cloud revenue those GPUs generate [1] . The program's first two adopters are Sharon AI (NASDAQ: SHAZ), an Australian sovereign-cloud provider planning up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs, and Firmus Technologies, which is building a 360MW campus in Batam, Indonesia, targeting up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [2] .\\nThe arrangement creates a dual revenue stream for Nvidia: the company collects standard product revenue from the GPU sale, then earns an additional usage-linked cut of the cloud services those chips power. Nvidia described the model as providing \\\"economic alignment with a revenue-sharing and credit-support model\\\" in a blog post co-authored by CFO Colette Kress [1] [3] . The specific revenue-share percentage was not disclosed, and Nvidia declined to offer further details beyond the blog post [3] .\\nThe backstop mechanism works by Nvidia agreeing to rent back unused GPUs at a fixed rate, effectively de-risking lender exposure and making it easier for smaller cloud operators to secure capital for large GPU deployments [1] [3] . The structure echoes — but formalizes — earlier arrangements in which Nvidia provided demand guarantees to help neoclouds like CoreWeave and Lambda raise billions in debt financing [1] .\\nUnder the AI Compute Partnership, Nvidia guarantees a floor utilization rate on deployed GPUs. If a neocloud partner's customer demand falls short, Nvidia commits to renting the idle capacity at a predetermined rate, absorbing the downside risk that would otherwise sit with lenders or equity investors [1] [3] . In exchange, Nvidia earns both its standard product revenue from the hardware sale and a recurring, usage-linked share of the cloud revenue generated on the supported capacity [1] .\\nThe model addresses a persistent bottleneck in the neocloud sector: smaller operators often have customer demand in hand but cannot secure financing quickly enough to build infrastructure, because lenders view GPU residual values as uncertain [4] . By backstopping idle capacity, Nvidia effectively underwrites the asset, making GPU clusters more bankable. The company framed the program as accelerating 'adoption of Nvidia platforms among the high-growth, high-conviction AI native sector' [1] .\\nSharon AI, which listed on the Nasdaq in February 2026 via a $125 million IPO, is deploying up to 40,000 Grace Blackwell GB300 GPUs under a six-year agreement with Nvidia [2] [6] . The company raised an additional $1.6 billion in private placement in June 2026 to fund its Nvidia-based AI factory and has partnered with VAST Data for 600PB of storage infrastructure supporting roughly 100,000 GPUs across its sovereign cloud operations in Australia and the Asia-Pacific region [6] .\\nFirmus Technologies, a private company, is constructing a campus in Batam, Indonesia, built to Nvidia's DGX SuperPOD (DSX) reference architecture specifications. The facility is expected to scale to 360MW and house up to 170,000 Nvidia GPUs [1] [3] . Both operators plan to serve AI-native companies including Baseten, Fireworks AI, and Together AI, as well as enterprise inference workloads [4] .\\nThe AI Compute Partnership represents a structural evolution in Nvidia's business model. Prior to this formalization, Nvidia had provided demand guarantees and customer referrals to help neoclouds raise debt — CoreWeave secured $6.3 billion and Lambda $1.5 billion through arrangements in which Nvidia played a supporting role [1] . The new model makes Nvidia's financial participation explicit, with the company now directly sharing in its customers' cloud economics.\\nThe shift creates a recurring revenue layer on top of Nvidia's hardware sales, potentially smoothing earnings volatility tied to GPU refresh cycles. Analysts have drawn comparisons to GE Capital's equipment financing model, noting both the upside of recurring income and the counterparty risk if neocloud operators face financial stress [4] . Nvidia's blog post positioned the arrangement as providing 'a recurring, usage-linked earnings stream' that diversifies its revenue beyond one-time chip sales [1] .\\nNvidia shares traded at $194.83 on July 3, down 1.4% on the session, on a market capitalization of approximately $4.7 trillion. The stock is up 4.5% year-to-date and 22.3% over the past twelve months [5] . The muted reaction suggests investors are still parsing the margin implications of revenue-sharing arrangements versus the benefits of demand-side de-risking.\\nSharon AI shares fell sharply, dropping 14.2% to $67.91 on volume of 2.6 million shares. The stock, which IPO'd at roughly $16.55 in February, had rallied to a high of $97.48 in June before the pullback [5] . Firmus Technologies is privately held and does not have a public ticker.\\nThe financing model keeps a broader ecosystem of independent GPU cloud providers alive rather than concentrating AI compute inside the hyperscalers — a strategic imperative for Nvidia, which benefits from having multiple distribution channels for its hardware [3] [4] . By making GPU clusters more financeable, Nvidia may accelerate buildout timelines for operators that would otherwise face 12- to 18-month fundraising cycles.\\nThe structure also gives Nvidia optionality: in a demand downturn, the company gains access to GPU capacity it can deploy for its own inference services or sublease, effectively building a buffer inventory at customer expense. Critics have flagged this as a form of 'double-dipping' — Nvidia profits from the sale and then again from the utilization [3] . Supporters counter that it aligns incentives, since Nvidia now has a direct financial stake in its customers' commercial success.\"}\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True))'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_6","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"python3 -c 'from bs4 import BeautifulSoup; s=BeautifulSoup(open(\\\"docs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\\\").read(),\\\"html.parser\\\"); print(s.get_text(\\\"\\\\n\\\",strip=True))'\"","aggregated_output":"Hivemind 抽取与开题标准\n目录\n✓\n已拍的 12 条\n1\n用词\n2\n账本记什么\n3\n问题的五类\n4\n开题标准\n5\n证据标准\n6\n分量\n7\n研究观点\n8\n金标\n9\n改提示词、测量、迭代\n10\nAlex 拍过的\n11\n冲突怎么摆平\n12\n校验和出处\nHivemind · 抽取这一步的 PRD · 第 5 版（第 3 版已签；D248 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订；D250 证据的四条界线和计数单位）\n抽取与开题标准\n已签\n生效。\n2026-10-29 Alex 拍了 12 条（D243）；2026-10-30 他逐个选了颗粒度的七条（D248，见\"已拍\"一节），改了第 2、3、4 节，升为第 4 版；2026-10-31 他按我给的倾向定了证据的四条界线和计数单位（D250，见\"已拍\"一节），改了第 5、8、9 节，升为第 5 版。这一页取代第 1 版（\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md\n，D229），是抽取这一步唯一的尺子：提示词、金标、回归测量都从它推出。改它要先在\ndocs/DECISIONS.md\n记下 Alex 的原话，再改这一页，再做一次只读的漂移检查。\n2026-10-29 · D241、D242、D243\n分支 fix/extraction-quality-2026-10-28\n起草过程和校验：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/\n读法：每条规则后面的灰色小字是它的出处。标了出处的例子是判例或原文里的例子；没标的是按那条规则举的，帮助理解，不是判例。没有出处的只有\"我定的记录约定\"。\n证据\n一个在追这家公司的分析员读到会记进本周笔记的陈述，就留；留下以后另判分量\n第 1 版 §2、§4\n。整体倾向谨慎：杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索；但新信息带来的增量要敏感，不能漏\nD243\n。预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都可以是证据，不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留\n第 1 版 §2\n。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也照留，分量可以低一点\nD240\n。泛泛的主张和背景是常识，没有数字和具体的叙事就不收\nD243\n。\n开题\n一个问题值得单独追，当它的答案可能改变仓位、能在一个日期用公开材料核对、而且库里没有问题在问同一件事\n第 1 版 §6\n。\n研究观点\n一个观点是关于业务和世界的、有论证的看法，它的立场来自论证和市场已经定价的东西相比\nAGENTS §8\n。问题当前落在哪一档，由判断这一步按 PRD 06 和 D198 读证据推出。\n一张图：同一篇文章里每句话的去向\n一句话\n在第 5.3 节的不留清单上\n→ 先看那一行的\"边界\"一列：写着\"不能用这一行删掉\"或\"按 5.1 判\"的，按第 5.1 节判，留下的走下面；写着\"要留\"或\"按本来分量\"的照办；写着\"可选\"的归可选；其余不留\n分析员会记\n→ 留为证据\n第 5 节\n记来源层：事实、当事人陈述、第三方观点、传闻\n进账本 · 第 5.5 节\n分量：核心、辅助、可选\n只在评估时判，不进账本 · 第 6 节\n归档：挂到最窄的那个已开问题\nD207\n；没有问题接，记 no home 扔掉，评估时逐条读\nD209\n这篇文章还提出了值得追的新问题吗？\n第 4 节\n过了测试，属于五类之一\n→ 候选问题；和库里已有问题是不是重复，由匹配判\nD211、D190\n没过\n→ 不开，它只是证据\n已拍\nAlex 拍板的 12 条\n2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243）。第 5 条他定得比我的倾向更严；其余 11 条按倾向：\"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"。下表每条写定下的是什么、落在哪一节。\n第 5 条，Alex 原话：\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"\n整体方向，Alex 原话：\"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\"\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n分量按信息量定档：核心要碰到关键判断，并带一个量、一个确定状态或一个带期间的预测；别家公司自己的经营数字、对公司是小事的，算辅助；预测离现在越远，越可能只是辅助，由判断定。缺金额、缺日期只影响分到哪一档，不是不留的理由\n第 6 节\n2\n\"共同量\"按 D191 落实：任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格（合计支出、合约价、利率）都可以开题，开题理由叫\nshared_quantity\n；期间按自然年。提示词和代码要跟着改\n第 3 节 D 类\n3\n生态数字（论文数、获奖、下载量、开发者人数、大会人数）可选\n第 6 节\n4\n在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选；带推导的条件估值仍是辅助\n第 5.3、6 节\n5\n典型的主张和背景是常识，没有数字和具体的叙事不收；不是常识的新信息，照第 5.1 节的规则判\n第 5.1、5.3 节\n6\n旧事重提：重提的过去实际数可选；重提的季度指引和仍然有效的预测、计划按本来分量；文字或日期看不出是旧事的不算重提\n第 5.3、6 节\n7\n收购算约束性义务（写明规模、尚未完成）\n第 3 节 C 类\n8\n改提示词时的回归判法：全部文章各发一次和上一版比，\"别的版本都留住、只有这一版丢了\"的核心或辅助才算回归信号；它是诊断，验收仍是三次发送\n第 9.3 节\n9\n来源层：一句话符合两行时取表里上面那行；混合的一条按主要说法定层，次要部分写进摘要\n第 5.5 节\n10\n只有形容的引语不收\n第 5.3 节\n11\n金标冻结后发现错：另记一份带理由的更正，写明改前改后，不覆盖原文件；测量用更正后的版本并注明\n第 8 节\n12\n达标线里\"垃圾\"一项（每篇留下的无关陈述）用定稿金标重算，Luna 基线也重算\n第 9.2 节\nD248：颗粒度的七条（2026-10-30）\nAlex 逐个听了例子、逐个选的，七个都选 A；改了第 2、3、4 节，标准升为第 4 版，金标要按第 4 版重推：开题部分逐条重判，证据部分逐条核对（D244）。\n原则，Alex 原话：\"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则\"\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n同一个计划的几种说法，最后核对同一个数的，是一题；各自会变、会分别盯的数才分开；属于已按金额开题的计划的一笔交易并进去\n第 4 节\n2\n同一件事的不同截止日：早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上证据，不等新文章\n第 4 节\n3\n同一代产品的几个里程碑：一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\n第 4 节\n4\n公司自己的量只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据\n第 4 节\n5\n事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并\n第 4 节\n6\n单笔交易、产品、项目，规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开题；够不上的不开题，它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判，留下的是证据\n第 3 节 C 类、第 4 节\n7\n原始说话人、原始日期、数字都相同的几条证据，代码认作一条，判断时只给看一次、写明几家报道，每条照存；不知道的说话人或日期不算相同\n第 2 节\nD250：证据的四条界线和计数单位（2026-10-31）\nv63 留下、金标判不收的 117 条交给没写金标也没写提示词的读者盲判，混着它分不出的对照：对照上读者和金标一致 93–95%，这 117 条上只有 70%。模型偏偏在标准没说清的地方留东西。五处各给了倾向，Alex 原话：\"五处都按你的倾向定，计数单位也改; 你要修正尺子；你已经多次因为尺子标准问题陷入假的测量\"。\n#\n定下的\n在哪一节\n1\n只描述一个现成安排（合作、客户在用、已有部署）、没有新动作的，是背景，不收，文中没写日期也一样；仍然有效的指引、预测、计划照旧按本来分量\n5.3\n2\n别家公司的事，说不出它通过需求、供给、竞争、定价这类路径动了在研公司哪个问题的，不收；只因同一篇文章、同一场活动或同一个生态挨着的，不收\n5.3\n3\n公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比是产品细节，不收；只有对手拿来和本公司产品比的按 5.1 判（判例 4 不变）；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判\n5.3\n4\n具名分析师对一件已记下的事的解读，没有新事实或推算的，不收；有新事实或推算的按 5.1 判；条件估值不变\n5.3\n5\n计数单位：一条不收的金标条目，只有带着它的那条记录本身不是金标判为核心、辅助或可选的同一件事时，才算留下；引文里顺带带出的不收半句，是那件事的同一条记录\n9.2\n尺子跟着修：金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推、另存冻结，打分程序遇到证据部分版本对不上就拒绝打分；开题部分重推之前不报开题的分（第 8 节）。\n我定的记录约定\n只定怎么记，不改变留不留、开不开。\n页面杂项（广告、相关文章预告、推荐列表、别的新闻标题）一律归第 1 版的\"模板\"一行，不另起名字。\n标题里的说法和正文是同一条证据，不单记\n第 1 版 §2 同一事实的复述\n。\n分四类时看\"谁在说谁\"（第 5.1 节）。四类只是金标里的标签，不进账本，不影响留不留。\n金标里，不在不留清单上、按第 5.1 节判掉的句子标出来并写理由，供复核（第 5.3 节、第 8 节）。\n1\n这一页用的词：一词一义\n词\n意思\n不要和它混\n陈述\n原文里某一方说的一句话或一件事\n证据\n被留下的陈述，带引文、摘要、说话人、原始出处、来源层\n作者的观点（它本身可以是一条证据）\n问题（观点）\n账本里追踪的一件事：一个问题句、一组结果档、一个到期日，写下后不改\nPRD 06\n候选问题\n抽取这一步提议新开的问题\n标的问题、主题问题\n标的：答案是一家可交易公司（或币、商品）自己的经营结果。主题：答案是世界上的一件事，影响很多公司\nPRD 06\n开题理由\n候选问题值得开的几类理由之一，答案里写在\nopens_as\nD184；英文 admission reason\n结果档\n一个问题事先固定、互不重叠、覆盖全部结果的结局，五档或是非两档\nPRD 06；英文 rung\n分量\n来源层\n事实、当事人陈述、第三方观点、传闻\nPRD 06；英文 evidence tier\n分量\n分量\n一条证据对判断有多重要：核心、辅助、可选、不收\n第 6 节；D243\n。Alex 说的\"档位低一点\"指它\n结果档、来源层\n研究用途\n判断这一步引用一条证据去支持一个具体结论时，它撑得住多强：直接可用、有条件可用、线索、不可用\nD221，第 7 节\n分量\n七类驱动\n一条陈述为什么对公司业务重要的七条路径\nD208\n。需求：客户要买多少，如订单、客户支出、贷款需求、客流。供给与投入：能做出多少、成本多少，如产能、原料和零部件价格、燃料、矿石品位。竞争与份额：对手的产品、价格、开店、赢单。定价与装机：公司能收多少价、存量多大，如售价、费率、利差、装机量、存量客户。产品周期：发布、出货、试验、获批、延期。政策：法规、许可、关税、利率、监管决定。资本与义务：融资、担保、承诺、投资、收购\n问题类型\n市场预期与估值\n市场已经定价了什么：估值倍数、市值、目标价、股价反应。观点的立场要和它比\nAGENTS §8\n。对一个业务量的一致预期（收入、EPS）是那个业务量的证据，不归这里\n七类驱动\n研究组\n几个问题共同在追的一件正在进行的事\nD165\n问题\n2\n账本里记三种东西\n证据\n一条记录一个陈述，一件事用几句话说仍是一条\n第 1 版 §2；Alex 判 11\n。记逐字引文、能单独读懂的一句话摘要、说话人、原始说话人和原始日期（不知道就写不知道，不填报道日\nD220\n）、来源层。同一条证据可以挂到几个问题上，推力写在挂接上\nPRD 06\n。每一条进来的证据都单独存下；和已有证据的关系（同一说法、修订）由匹配标出，作为意见，两条都留着\nD202、D204\n。原始说话人、原始日期、数字都相同的几条（说话人或日期不知道的不算相同），由代码认作同一条：判断时只给看一次，写明几家报道，每条仍照存\nD248 第 7 条；改 D204 的\"两条都给判断看\"\n。这条在打开之前要先在同一说法的金标上量两件事（金标按这条规则实际会合的组取，对着原文读）：它会不会藏掉不同或更丰富的说法（比如多一句限定），它依赖的原始说话人、原始日期两个字段准不准；量完之前，照 D204 两条都给判断看。\n问题\n问题句、结果档、到期日写下不改；当前判断随证据更新，到期定格\nPRD 06\n。问题分标的和主题，按开题理由分五类（第 3 节）。问题从三处来：文章里的候选问题；用户的备忘录\nPRD 06 State\n：\"A thesis from the user's memo is not yet judged until evidence first links to it\"；分析员开始覆盖一家公司时种下的驱动问题\nD208，设计未建\n。\n研究组\n一件正在进行的事（一个产品换代及其部署、一笔融资或收购、一个政策过程、一轮投资或需求周期），把几个问题组织在一起，常常跨公司。它不是问题，也不代替开题判断。公司自己的报表行（收入、毛利率、费用、税率、资本开支）不带研究组，除非这一行本身就在追这样一件事；它也可以加入别的问题建的组\nD165\n。\n不进账本的：\n归档找不到家的证据，记\nno home\n扔掉\nD209\n。评估时逐条读被扔的，分四种：抽取的垃圾；库里缺这个问题（覆盖缺口）；问题在却没挂上（归档错）；该开没开（开题错）。各归各的环节\n第 1 版 §7\n。存储的表结构归 PRD 06 管。\n3\n问题的五类：账本的几大类型\n开题理由是 D184 定的四类，D236 把主驱动量扩到产品节点，所以写成五类。判两件事，互不顶替：这是谁的问题（标的还是主题）；为什么值得开（开题理由）。\n类\n开题理由\n标的或主题\n问什么\n量还是事件\n期间和到期\n核对材料\nsettled_by\n出处\nA\n主驱动量\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那个年度量，不管公司指不指引：撑起业务的分部收入、毛利率、资本开支；跨行业如银行的净利息收入、零售商的同店销售、公用事业的费率基数、航空公司的燃油成本（当它是争论焦点时）。不是每一行报表数\n量，五档\n公司财年的今年或明年；到期日是这个数的发布日\n公布这个数的公开材料，通常是年报\nD184：\nsettled_by\n是 \"the public material that resolves it\"；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\nB\n主产品节点\nmain_driver\n标的\n承载公司价值的那一代产品哪天走到能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；发布、送样是它的证据，只有会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n事件，五档，或是非两档\nPRD 06 梯子第 1 条\n能判出结果的那天\n公司公告、发货确认\nD236\nC\n约束性义务\nbinding_obligation\n标的\n公司以自己名义承担的：为别人的债务、租约或收入担保或提供信用支持；借钱给别人；承诺购买（向别人买产能或服务也算）；为客户的产能或收入兜底；或写明规模、尚未完成的入股或收购\nD236；收购按 D243 第 7 条\n。规模要大到能改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险才开；够不上的单笔入股、收购、租约、授信不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条\n。不算：自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。D239 选定的 role-v29 原文：\"Its own borrowing, its own leases and a completed or unsized investment are not obligations\"；D238 的记录写作 \"the company's own borrowing and leases and a completed or unsized stake do not\"\n还没签：是非两档。已经承担（一笔或一个计划）：金额，五档\n检查日最多在文章后 18 个月\nD185\n；已承担的按下一份年报\n签约公告、年报承诺附注\nD184、D185、D210、D236、D238、D243\nD\n共同量\nshared_quantity\nD191、D243 第 2 条\n；现行提示词和代码还叫\nindustry_aggregate\n，按这一页改\n主题\n任何一家公司之外、好几家都会受它影响的量或价格：几家公司的合计（云厂商资本开支）、价格（内存合约价、油价）、利率。同一总量的另一种人群、另一种说法是这个量的证据\n量，五档\n自然年的今年或明年。同一个量、同一年只有一个问题，由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判\nD197\n；量的类别是\nother\n的不算\n几家公司年报、统计发布、官方决定\nD184、D191、D197、D243；PRD 06\nE\n政策阶段\npolicy_stage\n问的是一家公司的收入、利润、份额或产品进度，算标的，即使由政策驱动；问的是世界上的事（许可制度、利率、合计支出），算主题。D184 采纳的 role-v12 原文：\"A question about a company's revenue, profit, share or product progress is an asset thesis; put its ticker in object, even when policy or macroeconomics drives it\"\n一个政策或监管过程到了哪一步，写明谁说了算\n事件，五档或两档\n能判出那一步的那天。文章没写日期的待决监管事项，用本财年末（role-v12 原文：\"A pending regulatory decision may lack a date; use the current fiscal year-end as its due date\"）；这个日期是研究员定的检查日，要标明\n评审 §1 第 1 条\n官方决定、公报\nD184\n例子（跨行业）：\nA：\nNVIDIA FY2027 数据中心收入；一家地区银行 FY2026 净利息收入；一家公用事业公司 2027 年费率基数。\nB：\n下一代机架系统能否在 2027 年底前开始出货；一家车企的新平台能否在 2026 年内开始交付。\nC：\nNVIDIA 为云客户产能兜底的义务在 FY2027 年报达到多少；一家保险公司是否在 2027 年中前为合资公司 5 亿美元债务签署担保。\nD：\n五家最大云厂商 2026 年合计资本开支；2027 年 DRAM 合约价相对 2026 年的变化、美联储 2026 年最后一次会议后的利率上限。\nE：\n中国是否在 2027-01-31 前正式批准本国公司进口 H200；美国是否在某日前对某类芯片实行出口许可。这两题问的是许可这件世界上的事，所以是主题，role-v12 原文的例子就是 \"licensing\"。一个监管决定对一家公司收入或产品进度的影响，问成那家公司的量或产品节点时，是标的问题（同一句原文）。\n五类之外的东西去哪\n七类驱动不是问题类型。\n它们是一条陈述为什么重要的理由，用来判分量和归档。D208 设计的\"每家公司种下的驱动问题\"由分析员开始覆盖时写好，不从文章里开；还没建。\n别家公司自己的量\n（对手的份额、客户的资本开支）：D187 定了\"任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算\"，但还没实现，要先在覆盖名单里加私营公司这一类。D155 定了\"公司清单可以作为初始参考，但清单外不能忽略\"。现行代码在匹配时跳过名单外的对象\nPRD 06 Step 4\n。所以抽取照常可以提这样的问题，它要过第 4 节的测试；名单外的现在会被匹配跳过，这不算抽取错。\n宏观量\n（利率、通胀）：按 D 类共同量开题\nD243 第 2 条\n。只来自一篇纯宏观文章时，只有主题在名单上才开\nD191 第 3 条\n。宏观对每家公司的正负，在判断那家公司时读主题块决定\nD191 第 1 条、D195\n。\n开题理由是封闭的\nD184\n：一件事属不属于五类之一，按上表逐条看，比如一个监管决定看它是不是 E 类。哪一类都不属于的不开；它是不是证据、挂到哪，照第 5 节和归档规则。\n问题怎么写：PRD 06 的梯子规则，不重写\n一个问题只问一个量的一个期间，或一件事；能各自朝相反方向走、而且分析员会分别下注的部分要分开\nPRD 06 梯子第 3 条；\"分别下注\"是 D248 加的，PRD 06 签字后更新\n，分开的每一题都要过第 4 节的测试。\n写成问句，不写成断言\nPRD 06 Rationale\n。\n结果档可核对、不重叠、不留缝，到期恰好一档成立；中间一档写明和什么比；\"小幅\"\"部分\"这种不能核对的说法不用，部分状态写成单独一档\nPRD 06 梯子第 2 条\n。标的问题的档描述业务结果；主题问题的档只写结局，不写好坏\nPRD 06、D186\n。\n到期日：量是它的发布日；事件是能判出结果的那天。公司的量用公司财年，别的用自然年，只开今年和明年\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；约束性义务例外，D185\n。\n4\n开题标准\n一个问题值得单独追，要同时满足三件事：它的答案可能改变仓位；能在一个日期用公开材料判出落在哪一档；库里没有问题在问同一件事。\n开多少个是判断的结果，不是目标\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1\n。\n颗粒度按一个高端对冲基金分析员：只有会单独下注的大事开题，鸡毛蒜皮不开题（它的陈述留不留照第 5 节判）；同一个赌注的几种说法是一题，截止日、里程碑、财年和日历年各按本节\"同一题、续期，还是另一题\"里的那一条判\nD248\n。\n另有一条前提：新问题要有独立的投资判断；一个新产品、一个新数字、一个更窄的范围，单凭这些不开\nD201\n。\n四个测试\n第 1 版 §6\n值得追\n写得出\"这个结果会改变哪项收入、利润、现金或信用风险、竞争位置\"吗？写不出就留作证据，不开题。\"数字很大\"不算理由。单笔交易、产品、项目，规模够不上改变公司的收入、利润、现金或信用风险的，不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD248 第 6 条：问的就是小交易、小产品\n；这一测试的其余理由（包括竞争位置）不变。\n独立意义\n能写出两个情景吗：其他问题的答案不变，只有这一题相反，而对业务的判断确实不同？写不出就优先作为证据\n评审 §1 第 3、8 条\n。评审里的一个判例：NVIDIA FY2027 总收入题判为数据中心收入题的证据，理由是\"该段明确说主要增长来自数据中心，未提出独立的其他业务争论\"。这是对那一段材料的判断，别的材料要逐个判。\n能核对\n有一个日期和一份公开材料能把答案放进事先固定的档吗？没有就重写问题。到期没有材料记\"未能判定\"，不算错。\n不是重复\n和已有问题比，只是预测值、措辞、来源、方向或同一总量的另一种口径不同，最终核对的是同一个结果吗？是就是同一题。抽取看不到库\nD211\n，这一条在抽取时只对同一篇文章里提出的几个问题判；和库里的比由匹配做。\n另外三条写法检查\n评审 §1 第 1、5、6 条\n。没过的问题不能照原样开，要重写；重写不了就不开，材料作证据。\n原文支持：\n主体、量或事件、时间依据都能在原文找到。原文没给日期、而规则允许研究员定检查日的，用那个日期并标明：没写日期的待决监管事项用本财年末（第 3 节 E 类）；还没签的约束性义务用文章后 18 个月以内能核对签约的那天\nD185、D210\n。不把\"拟议\"\"预计\"写成\"已签\"\"已完成\"。\n口径完整：\n算谁、不算谁、算什么写清楚；支出、融资、担保、订单、收入不混。\n时间正确：\n原文支持这个期间，不把几年的平均写成今年。\n不开的\n第 1 版 §6 的清单\n不开\n出处\n它变成什么\n季度数字和季度指引\n第 1 版 §6；PRD 06 梯子第 6 条\n证据\n同一段指引里的每一行（\"费用率、税率是模型输入，不是争论\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n没日期的\"护城河\"\"战略\"\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n高管任免、股东投票、回购授权；章程\n第 1 版 §6；章程出自 D239 选定的 role-v29 原文（\"executive appointments, shareholder votes or bylaws\"）\n证据或不留\n例行诉讼程序\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n客户的部署计划（\"它是供应商需求和产品问题的证据，除非改变供应商自己的现金或信用风险\"）\n第 1 版 §6\n证据；改变公司自己的现金或信用风险时按 C 类判\n以约束性义务为理由开：公司自己借钱、自己的租约、已完成或没写规模的投资。它们不是约束性义务；对别的问题仍可以是证据，或按别的开题理由判\nD236、D238\n证据\n把远期年度预测改装成事件\n第 1 版 §6\n证据\n后年及以后的年度量（约束性义务除外）\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D185\n证据\n同一个量的另一个估计、另一种口径、另一个来源\n第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n那个已开问题的证据，由匹配并入\n同一题、续期，还是另一题：匹配这一步的事，抽取不判库里的\n总规则：\n同一个结果能判掉两题，就是同一题；答案能分开走、而且分析员会分别下注的，才是另一题\nD248\n。\n同一题：\n同一个结果能同时判两个问题。另一个估计、来源、措辞、方向\n第 1 版 §6 测试 4\n；同一总量的另一种人群或代用口径（D190 碰撞裁决提示 collision-v1：\"another population or proxy of one aggregate\"；D197 引的 role-v12 原文：\"another population or another wording of the same aggregate is evidence to the shown aggregate thesis\"）。候选并进已开问题，用它的结果档和到期日\nPRD 06\n。只有以共同量理由开题、带量的候选由代码自动判，其余是匹配和碰撞裁决的判断。\n同一个计划的几种说法\n（担保、融资承诺、信用支持……），最后核对的是同一个数，是一题\nD248 第 1 条\n。\n同一件事的不同截止日：\n早的那题开着时，晚的截止日是它的证据，不另开；早的到期没有结果、事情还在追，系统开下一个截止日的续期题，带上并进来的证据，不等新文章\nD248 第 2 条\n。\n同一代产品的几个里程碑：\n一题，问能带来收入的那一步（通常是开始出货）；送样、发布作证据；会分别下注的里程碑（新药的三期数据和获批）各一题\nD248 第 3 条\n。\n公司自己的量：\n只按财年开题；按日历年的估计是财年那题的证据，两种年份差得多时分量低一点\nD248 第 4 条；PRD 06 梯子第 5 条\n。\n身份：\n事件和政策题里模型写的期间起点，不拿来挡合并；对象、事件、截止日照旧核对\nD248 第 5 条\n。\n续期：\n同一个量或同一串事件的下一个期间\nPRD 06 Succession、D190\n。\n另一题：\n同一件事里答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的：有自己驱动的组成部分；同一笔交易或同一份附注里两个各自会变、分别盯着的数；另一件事\nD190 碰撞裁决的四种；D248 第 1 条收窄了\"担保和融资\"\n。\n约束性义务：\n\"一笔或一个计划\"一题\nD210\n；一笔交易是不是某个已按金额开题的计划的一部分，由匹配判，是就并入\nD248 第 1 条\n。还没签的单笔交易是\"签不签\"的是非题，照旧可以开，不并进计划的金额题\nD210；PRD 06；D248 未改\n。\n共同量：\n以共同量理由开题、带量的候选（量的类别是\nother\n的不算），由代码按\"主题＋量＋期间\"判同一题；政策阶段和别的事件保留按事件判的身份；不带这个开题理由的主题量由模型判\nD197\n。\n谁判，谁查\n内容\n谁\n测试 1–3、同一篇文章里的测试 4、属于哪一类、问题怎么写\n抽取的模型（判断）\n结果档形状、期间是真日期、到期在文章之后、季度数、年限、约束性义务 18 个月、\nsettled_by\n不为空\n代码：只查形状，不判重要性\nD154、D155、D184\n和库里的同一题、续期、另一题\n匹配（Jev）和碰撞裁决\nD190\n；共同量由代码\nD197\n年限按公司财年数：2026 年 7 月时 NVIDIA 在 FY2027（2027 年 1 月结束），所以 FY2028 是明年。代码原来按自然年算，把 FY2028 当成后年拒掉；在 v29 答案的 40 个带年份的候选里拒错了 2 个。已修，测试是\ntests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\nD243\n。\n5\n证据标准\n5.1 留的规则和四类\n一个在追这家公司的分析员，读到会在本周笔记里写下来，因为它让某个问题的答案更可能或更不可能。\n问题不限于库里已有的，也不限于本公司：客户、供应商、竞争者、监管、宏观都算\n第 1 版 §2；D208 七类驱动\n。\n整体倾向谨慎，对增量敏感。\n杂音很大，不是什么都要抽，不是什么都算线索。判一句话之前先问：这是新信息吗？它让哪个问题的答案变了？一件新发生的事、一个新数字、一个新的具体判断，是增量，不能漏；重复常识、描述长期状况、泛泛的看法，是杂音\nD243\n。增量和杂音的具体界线按 5.3 各行和边界一起判\nD250\n。\n从第一段读到最后一段，分析员会记的（按本节和 5.3 各行边界一起判）都留。一页有八条就是八条，不挑\nD199\n。它们通常是下面四类之一；四类描述的是会记的陈述长什么样，不是\"属于四类就一定留\"，留不留由上面的规则判；具体不留的行和边界见 5.3。\n公司自己的数字、指引或决定\n第 1 版 §2\n\"同店销售本季增长 4.2%\"；\"管理层把全年收入指引定在 120–130 亿美元\"；\"董事会批准 100 亿美元回购\"；\"公司取消软件订阅费，生产使用免费\"\nAlex 判 8，Omniverse\n。\n客户、供应商、竞争者、伙伴的有规模、有日期或有名字的动作，以及它们公布的数字\n第 1 版 §2：三者有一个就够\n第 1 版的例子\"Google Cloud 季度收入 248 亿美元，增长 82%，积压超过 5000 亿美元\"\nAlex 判 11\n；\"最大客户说明年订单砍五分之一\"；\"主要供应商说 2027 年产能翻倍\"；\"对手在欧洲降价 15%\"；\"一家银行选用竞争对手的系统\"；\"一家软件公司把本公司的模型集成进政府系统\"。后两条有名字、没金额，也留\nD240\n。\n具名一方的主张或估计\n第 1 版 §2\n\"CFO 说十年前建的工厂仍满负荷\"；\"分析师把明年 EPS 预期上调到 4.10 美元\"；\"做空机构指控公司虚增应收 20 亿美元\"；\"内存厂说 2027 年短缺最严重，持续到 2030\"。\n政策或监管的决定或步骤\n第 1 版 §2\n\"监管机构批准该药第二个适应症\"；\"政府要求该产品出口须申请许可\"；\"25 家公司联名反对过早限制开放权重模型\"；\"财长说将调查某国公司是否窃取知识产权\"。\n典型的主张和背景\nD243 第 5 条\n：Alex 的原话是\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"。常识，就是跟这家公司的分析员读这篇之前就已经知道的：谁领先、谁在竞争、需求强、供给紧、融资难这类说法，和对已知状况的重述。这类陈述没有数字和具体的叙事就不收。不是常识的，是新信息，比如一份新报告的判断、一个新的市场数据、刚发生的事的细节，照上面的规则判，分析员会记就留。\n常识，不收：\n\"博通 CEO 说能从英伟达抢份额\"；\"Blackwell 和 Rubin 仍然供不应求\"；\"融资会是扩大算力最大的障碍\"；\"内存厂避开了以往的产能过剩\"；\"博通预计明年随着新客户推出定制芯片大幅增长\"。\n新信息，收：\n一位科技公司高管对媒体说\"中国国产 AI 芯片厂商已经全部卖光，客户在考虑性能更低的芯片\"；\"旧型号 GPU 的租金在涨\"；研究机构说\"这让 Rubin Ultra 暂时没有成熟的扩展方案，给对手留出了空间\"；\"华为现在占中国 AI 芯片市场约一半，这个市场约 500 亿美元\"。\n有数字也要过上面的规则：\n作者说\"英特尔多年领先，占 CPU 市场 59%\"，和英伟达的问题关系不大，分析员不会记。\n5.2 不完整也是证据\n预测、传闻、反证、远期、条件情景都是证据；它们的层级和确定性另记。不因为\"还没发生\"\"不是官方\"\"离现在远\"\"和我们方向相反\"就不留；一个真实的反例可能比十个顺风数字更重要\n第 1 版 §2\n。有名有姓的动作缺金额、缺日期也是证据，分量可以低一点\nD240\n。不留清单只按 5.3 各行和边界删，不能用来删掉标准要留的陈述：\"a reject rule never removes what the standard keeps\"\nD240\n。\n5.3 不留的\n第 1 版 §2 的清单，加 D238、D243 的行\n\"按同一行补的例子\"一列是我按同一行举的例子。\n不留\n第 1 版的例子\n按同一行补的例子\n边界\n没有业务规模的产品、游戏、驱动、跑分细节\n\"这张卡 415W\"；\"Geekbench 跑分\"\n接口、功率、旧型号的支持周期\n对手拿它和本公司产品比的说法不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（Alex 判 4：AMD 2.4 倍是辅助）；伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不是产品细节（5.1 第 2 类）；公司自己、伙伴或第三方发布的对比（\"比闭源模型便宜 10 倍\"、\"同样功率多放 40% GPU\"、第三方测试）是产品细节，不收；同一句里伙伴、客户采用或集成的动作不因这一条删，照旧按 5.1 判\nD250 第 3 条\n股价涨跌、投资人仓位、技术面\n\"本月跌 13%\"；\"Burry 做空\"\n期权成交、基金买卖\n股价反应背后的那条新闻不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判；\"财报后股价反应\"可选\n第 1 版 §10.2\n；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选\nD243 第 4 条\n；别的股价涨跌照旧不留\n作者的一般性风险清单和没有推导的看法\n\"如果 AI 退潮会受伤\"\n\"这只股票该持有\"；\"芯片指数是纯粹的高估\"\n有假设、有推导、有时点的条件估值要留\n第 1 版 §10.1\n；具名分析师对一件已记下的事的解读（\"推迟会给对手留出窗口\"），没有新事实或推算的，不收，有新事实或推算的按 5.1 判\nD250 第 4 条\n记者的背景和框架\n\"A100 2020 年上市\"；\"海湾国家花了几十亿但仍依赖它\"\n\"公司成立于 1998 年\"；通用的技术讲解\n没有数字和具体叙事的主张和背景不收；怎么分见 5.1\nD243 第 5 条\n；只描述一个现成安排（合作、客户在用、已有部署）、没有新动作的，是背景，不收，文中没写日期也一样\nD250 第 1 条\n没有数字也没有决定的政治、战略表态\n\"分散供应商不能减少政治依赖\"\n大使馆说\"立场一贯\"\n官员宣布的政策步骤不能用这一行删掉，按 5.1 判（第 4 类）\n同一事实的复述\n一篇稿子用五句话说一个部署是一条；三家媒体转同一个独家，原始出处写同一个\n标题和正文说同一件事\n已知事实的重述\n两个月后再说一遍 Q1 收入、指引、已公布的回购\n抽取看不到库，只能从文字和日期看。重提的过去实际数可选；重提的季度指引和仍然有效的预测、计划按本来分量；看不出是旧事的不算重提\nD243 第 6 条\n；只描述现成安排、没有新动作的，按背景判（见\"记者的背景和框架\"一行），其余仍按这一行判\nD250 第 1 条\n模板\n署名、作者简介、经纪商页脚、前瞻性声明、广告\n页面上和本文无关的内容（相关文章预告、推荐列表、别的新闻标题）\n只有形容的引语\n\"a pivotal moment\"；\"heterogeneous inference\"（role-v18 原文的例子）\nD243 第 10 条\n新加坡转运案件的概况和被告个人资产\n可选，收不收都不扣分\nD231\n另外一条来自后来的决定：\n和公司所有市场都无关的别家公司的估值、股价、交易\n不留（D238：\"another company's valuation, share price or deals that touch none of the company's markets\"）。别家公司的动作和经营数字不能用这一行删掉，它们留不留按 5.1 的规则判：要说得出它通过需求、供给、竞争、定价这类路径动了在研公司的哪个问题；只因同一篇文章、同一场活动或同一个生态挨着、说不出这条路径的，不收\nD250 第 2 条\n。\n不在清单上的句子，用 5.1 的规则逐句判（分析员会不会记）；金标里把这类判断标出来供复核。\n5.4 一条记录必须保住的意思\n第 1 版 §3，原样\n六格里只填这一条用得上的\nD220\n。\n格\n要保住\n丢了就错的例子\n谁\n原始判断者；是本人估计还是别人实测\nAMD 自己的模拟说成独立实测\n什么\n业务、指标或动作\n\"收入增长 82%\"丢了\"收入\"\n多少\n金额、比例、数量\n四家 7450 亿写成五家\n哪个期间\n预测或事实覆盖的期间；\"今年\"按公司财年或自然年写都算对\nD233\n，余下时间不改成全年\n\"今年余下时间\"改成 FY2027 全年\n什么条件\n推导的假设；计划、上限、\"最多\"\n\"最多 4 万颗\"写成已部署 4 万颗\n预测还是已发生\n指引、预计、商谈、传闻不等于已实现、已签约\n\"商谈 6000 亿\"写成\"已承诺\"\n原始发言日不知道就写不知道，不能填报道日\nD220\n。一条记录单独读要能懂，条件不能只放在旁边另一条里\n第 1 版 §3，引主分支 D251；两个分支的决定编号各自独立，D227\n。不必要的：转述机构、普通发言场合、付费方案全名；漏了不扣原意分，\n编造了仍算记录错误\n第 1 版 §3\n。没有来源证实的结论，可以作为分析员的推断写出，但不能写成来源说的事实\nD218\n。\n边界\n第 1 版 §2\n：Sharon 合同是供需的辅助证据，不是担保金额的证据；NVL72x2 取消归产品进度\nAlex 判 10\n。\n5.5 来源层\nPRD 06 的四层；D238 采纳的 role-v29 原文\nPRD 06 的定义：事实＝正式动作、公布的数字、完成的交易；当事人陈述＝公司指引、管理层表态、官员讲话；第三方观点＝卖方分析师、媒体分析、专家；传闻＝匿名来源的说法和没有出处的说法。层级跟着\"是谁确认的\"走，没有固定分数；二十家转载一个独家是一个来源。\nrole-v29 提示词的原文（D238；Alex 在 D239 选它作为抽取请求；D245 换成的 role-v42、D246 换成的 role-v51、D247 换成的 role-v53 这一句都未改）：\"fact for official actions, published actual figures or completed transactions; party_statement for company guidance, management statements or officials' remarks; third_party_view for analyst, media or expert opinions; rumour for claims the item credits to unnamed sources or calls unconfirmed; an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source.\"\n这两处列了四层，下表把它们排成一张。一句话同时符合两行时，取表里上面那行\nD243 第 9 条\n。\n说的是什么\n层\n1. 文章说来自没具名的人，或文章自己说未经证实（即使说的是一项公告或一个数）\n传闻\n2. 正式动作、公布的实际数字、完成的交易；文章报道的公告或已完成的交易（即使记者没写出处）\n事实\n3. 公司的指引、管理层的表态、官员的讲话\n当事人陈述\n4. 分析师、媒体、专家的看法和估计\n第三方观点\n5. 以上都不是、又没有出处的说法（PRD 06：\"unattributed statements\"）\n传闻\n一条记录里既有已发生的部分、又有计划或传闻的细节，按主要说法定层，次要部分的性质写进摘要；不拆成两条，除非是两件事\nD243 第 9 条\n。层级之间的关系：后来的确认和先前的传闻都留在账本里；匹配判断两条的关系（同一说法、修订或新的），修订的条件过了才记修订，没过就作为新的一条存下\nD202、D204\n；同一说法在判断时怎么显示，见第 2 节\nD248 第 7 条\n；判断时只有事实能确定到期落在哪一档\nPRD 06\n。\n6\n分量\n分量问\"这条对判断多重要\"，分四级。按投资后果判它碰到的是什么；核心和辅助的界线按信息量定\n第 1 版 §4；D243 第 1 条\n。缺金额、缺日期只影响分到哪一档，不是不留的理由\nD240\n。分量不由数字多不多、是不是官方、库里有没有页、和我们方向是否相同、文章篇幅决定\n第 1 版 §4\n；离现在远不是不留的理由\nD216\n，它落在核心还是辅助由判断定。\n等级\n什么样\n例子\n核心\n碰到关键判断，并且带着能定位答案的信息。关键判断：收入、利润、融资或信用风险、需求、关键产品进度、关键投入的供给和价格；相对在研公司判，包括它的约束性义务和它承载价值的那一代产品及其配置、过渡方案。能定位答案的信息：一个量（金额、比例、数量；在谈的金额也算，\"在谈\"写进意思里）、一个确定状态，或一个带期间的预测。对公司不是小事，也不是别家公司自己的经营数字\n分析师预计今年余下收入增长 82%、2027 年 41%；云巨头 2026 年合计支出 6500 亿美元；内存厂说短缺持续到 2030；为客户 1050 亿美元租约担保；公司宣布为云客户产能兜底的新计划（确定状态，没给金额）；银行指引全年净利息收入降 3–5%；监管机构批准主力药或定下裁决日；主要供应商宣布 9 月起涨价 8%；交易对手披露公司 34 亿美元的采购承诺；据报道在谈最多 6000 亿美元的支持\n辅助\n支持或反驳上面的判断，或给出可检验的估值假设；碰到关键判断但没有能定位答案的信息；别家公司自己的经营数字（收入、利润率、积压订单）；对公司规模来说是小事的；离现在越远、判断下来只是辅助的预测\n对手自己模拟的 2.4 倍性能比较；公司取消软件订阅费；三条开放模型政策表态；Sharon 六年 72 MW 合同；FY2028 EPS 一致预期；12.76 × 25 的条件估值；一家银行选用对手系统（没写金额）；伙伴把公司模型集成进政府系统（没写金额）；内存厂公布的季度收入和利润率；客户公布的订单积压；客户园区\"规划最多 17 万颗 GPU\"\n可选\n帮助理解市场预期，省略不损害论证；留不留都不扣分\n财报后股价反应；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价；生态数字；重提的过去实际数；新加坡案件\nD231、D243 第 3、4、6 条\n不收\n第 5.3 节的各行；与判断无关\n模板；产品参数；个人仓位；空泛风险；没有数字和具体叙事的主张\n判法\n先判留不留\n按 5.1 判分析员会不会记，不会就不收。在 5.3 的不留清单上的，先看那一行的\"边界\"一列：写着\"不能用这一行删掉\"或\"按 5.1 判\"的（对手比较、伙伴或客户的采用或集成、股价反应背后的新闻、官员宣布的政策步骤、带来新事实或推算的解读）按 5.1 判，留下的按下面两步定分量；写着\"要留\"或\"按本来分量\"的（条件估值、重提的指引和仍然有效的预测、计划）照办；写着可选的归可选；其余不收。\n是可选的那几样吗？\n在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价，财报后股价反应，生态数字，重提的过去实际数，新加坡案件，归可选。带推导的条件估值例外，是辅助\n第 1 版 §10.1\n。\n核心还是辅助\n碰到关键判断、带着能定位答案的信息、对公司不是小事、不是别家公司自己的经营数字，四条都满足是核心；其余是辅助。别家公司直接说出关键投入或需求的状况（短缺到哪年、明年花多少），按关键判断算。预测离现在越远，越可能只是辅助，这由判断定：FY2028 EPS 一致预期是辅助，Alex 说\"太久远了\"；内存厂说短缺 2027 年最严重、持续到 2030 是核心，它覆盖今明两年。\n\"确定状态\"\n：已经签了、宣布了、开始了、批准了、取消了、推迟到某个日期了、定下了裁决日；没有量的\"在谈\"\"在考虑\"\"可能\"\"在探索\"\"打算\"不算。确定状态以陈述当天为时间，不要求生效日。\n\"带期间的预测\"\n：分析师预计今年余下收入增长 82%；内存厂说短缺持续到 2030 年。确定不确定看说法本身的内容；靠不靠得住另记在来源层。\nAlex 拍过的分量\nD215；gold-check.md，D229\n：收入增速核心；内存短缺核心；AMD 跑分辅助；三条政策辅助；云厂商支出核心；Omniverse 辅助；Sharon \"辅助属于供给需求\"；商谈最多 6000 亿支持 OpenAI 核心（在谈的金额）；投资 SB Energy 15 亿核心；Broadcom AI 半导体收入 +143%、Google Cloud 三个数辅助（别家公司的经营数字）；FY2028 EPS 辅助（远期）；逐案审核、有拒绝辅助（没有量和确定结果）。\n分量只在评估时判。\n分量只在金标和测量里判，不进账本，不进抽取的输出\nD240\n；账本只存来源层。抽取的模型决定留不留、怎么写，以及提哪些候选问题\n第 1 版 §1\n。\n仿真里剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助这条线上：\"对公司规模来说是不是小事\"从一句话里常常看不出来（第 12 节，第三轮）。所以核心和辅助分开报，评估时允许读者间合理的不同判\nD226、D228\n。\n7\n研究观点标准\n判断这一步归 PRD 06、D198、D201 管；这里只列和抽取共用的。\n观点是什么：\n一个关于业务和世界的、有论证的看法；立场来自论证和市场已经定价的东西相比；报表行是它的证据，不是它的定义\nAGENTS §8\n。\n判断读什么：\n一个问题的判断看到的，是它仍然有效的证据里的两部分：新到的（在它倒数第五次判断之后挂上的；判断不满五次时全部显示）和依据（最近十次判断里被引用过的）。其余仍在账本里有效，请求里写明还有多少条没显示；答案只能引用和推动显示了的\nD198；从 judging-v5 起，v4 的配置照旧读全部有效证据\n。依赖问题的结论和理由、标的问题的主题块也读\nPRD 06、D195\n。主题结论变了，同一轮里排队重判符合条件的标的问题；主题的结论不作为证据引用\nD195\n。\n研究的交付物是论证：\n保留不可改的来源记录，和一份有版本的研究论证，写明支持和反对的来源、是否独立、变化和局限\nD201\n。\n研究用途：\n评估判断时，读者按四级看它引用的每条证据对那个具体结论撑得住多强：直接可用、有条件可用、线索、不可用；同一条证据对不同结论可以不同\nD221\n。这是评估的读法，不是判断这一步要输出或存下的字段（D221：\"not a numerical confidence score or a new storage layer\"）。用途和分量不同：分量问\"这条多重要\"，用途问\"拿它证明这个结论够不够\"。\n事实和推断分开：\n来源没说的结论可以作为分析员推断写出，但要标明\nD218\n。只有事实能确定落在哪一档\nPRD 06\n。\n重复不是新证据，模型意见一致不是互相印证\nAGENTS §8\n。\n8\n金标：改提示词的测试标准\n下面这份第 3 版金标是历史记录。第 4 版（D248）改了开题的规则，没有改动证据条目（逐条核对见\nresearch/2026-10-30-noise/gold-v4-evidence-check.md\n）；第 5 版（D250）改了证据的四条界线。金标的证据部分按第 5 版重推，两位互不知情的读者逐条判，分歧对着原文定，另存成新文件冻结，每条改动写改前、改后和理由，第 3 版文件不动（D243 第 11 条）。开题部分要按第 4 版逐条重判，重判之前打分程序不报开题的分；证据部分版本对不上，打分程序拒绝打分（D244、D250）。\n第 5 版金标（2026-10-31 冻结）：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v5/golden-v5-dev.json\n（sha256\n6f9476bb6a81ea590af273a71565e7c3c0f49908d26274b8482d52cfdee31a21\n）和\ngolden-v5-test.json\n（sha256\naf2145cdc60c34ec1ded0bebcb809800e187d616992ea325919c3ad771da61e3\n）。重判了 537 条（全部要收、可收的证据，引 D238、别家公司、5.1 判断或新加坡案件的不收句，和盲读认为该留的 35 句）：Codex 和 Claude 两位读者各自盲读，96 条进裁定，62 处改动逐条写改前、改后、两位读者的判法和理由（\ngolden-v5/corrections-v5.json\n）。开发集：核心 86（不变）、辅助 151（原 181）、可选 72；不收 770、可选 31。测试集：核心 9、辅助 37（原 42）、可选 1。两篇以中东冲突主题为对象的文章，读者被告知按在研公司读，是说明写错，那几条不改。下面的数字是第 3 版的历史记录。\n按这一页从原文逐篇写的参照，用来量提示词改动。Alex 的要求是\"以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"\nD241\n。定稿文件：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/golden-v3/golden-v3-signed.json\n，sha256\n78844bc329fc85000e18ddcab9ea59feb57abe6407cf11bbead4ba8fe4c3c0d3\nD243\n。\n118\n篇测试文章：那一周 98 篇，留出 20 篇\n348\n条要收或可以收的证据，一件事一条\n738\n句不收，各写理由；另有 24 句可选\n1110\n段原文片段，代码核过都在原文里\n28 · 42\n个必须开、可以开的问题；240 个不该开\n每一条记什么\n证据：\n原文逐字片段；属于 5.1 哪一类；定稿分量\nweight\n（核心、辅助、可选）；来源层；谁；必须保住的意思（5.4 用得上的格）；判它的那一句标准；签字前的两套分量和它依赖哪几条，留作记录。\n不收的句子：\n属于 5.3 哪一行，或按 5.1 的理由；\nweight\n是不收或可选。可选的留不留都不扣分。\n问题：\n必须开的（类、开题理由、标的或主题、要点、\nsettled_by\n）；可以开的（另一种合理写法）；不该开的（第 4 节哪一行或哪个测试）。\n没判清的地方：\n两种读法都写出来。\n分量的分布（定稿）\n那一组\n核心\n辅助\n可选\n要收或可收\n不收\n原列不收、现为可选\n那一周 98 篇\n80\n152\n63\n295\n621\n22\n留出 20 篇\n6\n29\n18\n53\n117\n2\n参考答案第 2 版那一周是核心 47、辅助 69、可选 48。辅助多出来的，主要是有名有姓、没写规模的动作（伙伴集成、客户采用、合作）：第 2 版标成\"什么都不留\"，现在按 D240 收为辅助。\n怎么写成、怎么核的\n六个起草者分批写\nClaude opus 各写 20 篇左右，只读标准和原文；参考答案第 2 版只当起点，每条重判。\n合并时统一写法\nmerge.py\n统一各批的写法，每处写理由（\nmerge-log.txt\n）：旧事重提 49 条逐条判；估值倍数和别家公司股价按现行规则处理；中国是否批准 H200 进口改为主题问题。\n逐条读改动\nClaude 读了每批相对参考答案第 2 版新增、删掉、改档的条目：新增约 124 条，改档约 37 条；旧条目没对上的 10 条逐条查过，都在，只是引文片段不同或按旧事重提处理。\n两轮独立盲读\nCodex 读者只读这份标准和原文，不看金标。读者多留的逐条对原文判：金标漏了的补上，其余写明不补的理由。补进来的 49 条，\"谁\"和\"意思\"对着原文补齐（\nadditions-fields.json\n）。\n按签字定稿\nfinalize.py\n按 D243 给每一条定分量，39 处改动逐条写理由（\nfinalize-log.txt\n）：32 条移出要收的一栏，其中 24 条是常识性的主张和背景（第 5 条），1 条是只有形容的引语（第 10 条），7 条和起草者写了理由的不收句是同一句；另有 7 句不收句和要收的条目重复，删掉。这 19 处\"同一句话既留又不留\"，是盲读后补条目时没和不收的句子对照造成的。此外，不收的句子里 24 句按第 3、4、6 条改为可选。\n盲读\n金标条目\n读者也留了\n分量相同\n读者多留\n补进金标\n第一轮：随机 15 篇，补之前\n34\n33\n26\n19\n8\n第一轮：补之后\n42\n41\n32\n11\n第二轮：其余 103 篇，补之前\n229\n194\n162\n144\n40\n第二轮：补之后\n262\n227\n193\n结论：起草稿大约漏了两成，集中在有名有姓、没写规模的采用和合作，和一些带期间的判断。补完以后，独立读者能找到的金标条目约九成。读者多留的 163 条里，48 条是金标漏了、已补；其余 115 条按标准不补：同一件事金标已有、新加坡案件、产品细节、泛泛看法、旧事重提、合作方自己的事、和公司市场无关、只描述长期状况。剩下的不一致集中在核心和辅助的界线（第 1 条）和\"背景还是主张\"（第 5 条），两条都已由 Alex 拍定。记录在\ngolden-v3/spotcheck.md\n、\npass2-decisions.json\n。\n用它之前要知道的\n它是模型写的。\nClaude 起草和裁定、Codex 盲查；按 AGENTS §7 这样标明。Alex 抽查之前，它不是 Alex 的判断。\n留出集要换。\n之前的提示词循环发过这 20 篇，答案读过一部分。按第 1 版 §11，\"看过模型答案的文章只做开发集\"，所以第一次改提示词之前，要另取一批没人看过答案的文章，从原文写参照，同样盲查、定稿、记 sha256。\n发现错了怎么办。\n不改这个文件；另记一份更正，每条写理由和改前改后，测量用更正后的版本并注明\nD243 第 11 条\n。\n按金标打分的程序还没写。\n代码只核结构和计数；意思由没写这次改动的读者判（第 9.2 节）。\n9\n改提示词、测量、迭代\n签字以后的第一件事：把现行提示词（role-v29）和这一页逐句对一遍，列出不一致的地方，再按 9.3 一类一类改。一般的规则全文在\nAGENTS.md\n§6（写提示词）和 §7（测量和迭代），这里只写到这一步怎么用，出处标在后面。\n9.1 改提示词\n每一句都对得上这一页。\n提示词里每一句关于留、不留、分量、开题的话，在这一页找得到出处；找不到的，是给 Alex 的提议，不是改动\nAGENTS §6\n。\n写\"不留\"之前先核它删不掉要留的。\n分析员会记的陈述，缺金额、缺日期本身不是不留的理由；分量另判，可能低一点\nD240\n。Alex 的原话：\"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？……你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\"。\n通用的对冲基金大脑。\n每一句、每个例子对银行、药企、零售、芯片读起来一样；例子跨行业，不点名覆盖的公司，不取自测试文章\nD237；AGENTS §6\n。\n不靠加长。\n读整个请求，就像模型读到的那样（系统提示、schema、内容）；列出矛盾、为别的情况写的规则、逼出缺陷的说法；删、并、拆。缺一句才加，别处删掉同样多；报改前改后的字数和 token\nAGENTS §6\n。\n分类写成封闭集合。\n每类两三个跨行业的例子，加走法（从第一段读到最后一段）和停止点（\"一页有八条就是八条\"）\nAGENTS §6\n。例子就是指令：和 schema、读答案的代码不一致的例子删掉。\n发之前先免费核一遍。\n写一份照提示词作答的假答案，过一遍全部代码检查；契约句逐条对金标该收和该拒的例子读一遍；确认发出去的字节和生产一样；\nscripts/preflight.py\n把存下的答案重放一遍\nAGENTS §5、§6\n。\n9.2 测量\n一把尺子。\n这一页 → 定稿金标 → 下面的达标线。另一把尺子在用，比如旧参考答案、评审打分表、提示词自己的类别，就是缺陷，先修掉再测\nD241；AGENTS §7\n。\n计数单位。\n一条\"不收\"的金标条目，只有带着它的那条记录本身不是金标判为核心、辅助或可选的同一件事时，才算留下；引文里顺带带出的不收半句，是那件事的同一条记录，不算误收。是不是同一件事，代码的计数辅助先算，有出入的由读者对着原文判\nD250 第 5 条；第 2 节\"一件事用几句话说仍是一条\"\n。\n不在还在改的尺子上给自己打分。\n金标先给 Alex 看、冻结，再开始改提示词；意思由没写这次改动的读者按金标判；代码只核结构和计数\nAGENTS §7\n。\n被扔的逐条读。\n按四种归因计数（第 2 节），从最大的一类修起\nD209\n。\n总数只看三次发送。\n一次发送只能说明机制；直接读（每篇发一次，每个答案和原文并排读，D238）用来找机制，它的数是一次发送的数，要这样标明。回归先报，每个数都报\nAGENTS §7\n。\n达标线是第 1 版 §11，逐字照抄在下面的引线内：\n单位\n：一篇原文里的一个研究事项（可由多条证据表达）。参照在任何发送之前写好并冻结（sha256），从原文写，不看模型答案；看过模型答案的文章只做开发集，留出集另取没看过的文章。\n每个参照项记四件事\n（gold v5 §二）：选了没有（kept / omitted，拆几条合几条只算一次）；原意（correct / partial / wrong / absent）；可核查（引文和上下文够不够）；记录问题（原始日期、期间、归属、确定性、错来源、重复、推断未标明，各标 material / record_only）。\n分开报，不合成总分\n：核心 N 项里 correct / partial / wrong / absent 各多少；辅助同样；误收率 = 留下的\"不收\"项 ÷ 评过的\"不收\"项；多留的垃圾按条数（我读，代码核结构）；每个 material 问题单列。分母为零报 not_exercised。三次独立发送逐次报，不投票。\n达标线（提案）\n：\n核心：每一次发送都 kept 且 correct，不带 material 记录问题（v18 验收用的就是\"六篇五发每条都在\"）。\n辅助：三次里至少两次 kept，correct 或 partial。\n不收：模板 0/3 留下（新加坡案件按 D231 改为可选）。\n垃圾：每篇留下的无关陈述不多于 Luna 对照组的中位数（基线，不是目标）。\n候选：§6 例子里该开的一个问题在每次发送都提出，且 settled_by 写得出；不该开的（部署计划、季度指引、同段每行）0 次。\n不达标就读原始答案找机制，再改一处；不在读过的文章上调。\n结构由代码查\n（免费）：schema 通过、引文逐字在原文里、每条一个陈述、候选的 due 在发布日之后。结构错不算内容错，分开报。\n引文检查接受的写法：只在标点、引号、大小写上和原文不同，或在一句之内省略一段，存原文\nD236\n。\n之后的决定对它的影响：参考答案第 2 版里\"整篇什么都不留\"的标注不再当目标\nD240\n；\"垃圾\"一项用定稿金标重算，Luna 基线也重算\nD243 第 12 条\n；第 1 版说的\"§6 例子里该开的一个问题\"是 AI Compute Partnership 的义务金额题；合理的不同判和不同措辞可以接受，可选细节和标签不同不变成硬性的过或不过\nD226、D228\n。\n9.3 迭代：一类一类改\nAlex：\"你一类一类改，快速迭代然后测regression\"。每一轮是下面六步：\n挑最大的一类\n把漏收、误收和被扔的按机制归类计数，从最大的一类开始；报这一类占多少\nAGENTS §1\n。\n找到是哪一句\n读这一类的原始答案，和原文并排（改之前一共至少读过十个原始答案）；对模型做出的选择，接着那次对话问它\"哪一句让你这样判\"；先看请求里有没有它需要的、schema 写不写得出\nAGENTS §1、§6\n。\n只改一处\n一次改一句或一组说同一件事的话，别处删掉同样多；新的话对得上这一页\nAGENTS §6\n。\n全部文章各发一次，和上一版比\n改一句会改变每一次请求，所以要发的是全部文章。\"别的版本都留住、只有这一版丢了\"的核心或辅助才算回归信号；单次发送本身每版有 0–4 条这样的意外丢失\nD243 第 8 条；surprise.py\n。每个信号读原始答案、问模型自己的理由，追到是不是这次改的那一句造成的。这是诊断。出错的那几篇只用来找机制；接不接受，看全部文章三次发送、和新对照一起报的结果\nAGENTS §7\n。\n记一行\n假设、改动、数字（回归先报）、留不留；没用的改动和自己的错也记\nAGENTS §7\n。\n一批改完再验收\n在最终配置上三次发送，按 9.2 由不是作者的读者评，结果给 Alex\n第 1 版 §11\n。\n尺子有疑问就停。\n改的时候发现金标和这一页说法不同、两份标准打架、或读者对\"什么是对的\"有分歧，就停下来先和 Alex 定尺子，不在有疑问的尺子上接着调\nD241；AGENTS §7\n。\n10\nAlex 已经拍过的判例\n第 1 版 §8 的全部，加之后的。\n日期\n判例\n原话或答案\n10-22\n讲话方式\n\"explain and answer like I'm 16 and in human understandable language\"\nAGENTS §8\n观点是什么\nA thesis is an argued view about the business and the world; its stance comes from the argument weighed against what is priced. Financial-statement lines are its evidence, never its definition.\n10-26 D191\n共同量、主题\n决定文字：\"\nindustry_aggregate\nis renamed\nshared_quantity\n: a quantity or price outside any one company that several move on\"，Alex：\"好\"；纯宏观文章只在主题在名单上时开，Alex：\"是\"；主题对公司一对全部，Alex：\"我觉得这个 1 to all mapping 比较 make sense\"\n10-26 D187\n别家公司的量\n选 C（\"可以\"）：任何公司自己的量是那家公司的问题，私营公司也算；记录，未实现\n10-27\n这一步的标准\n\"做一个 hedge fund analyst 的专业视角去看怎么做这一步的标准\"；\"什么叫重要陈述，你也没给例子\"\n10-28 D208\n驱动层\n\"可以，但是不能因为有了这层我们的抽取就是非常的模版化缺少了hedge fund analyst 的灵魂\"；宏观\"算是个global factor to all, 但影响正反面不一定\"\n10-28 D209\n没家的陈述\n\"if it doesn't matter, just fucking throw it away … if there are false positives that got thrown away … you got to pick it up. Again, that's the way to do proper evaluation.\"\n10-28\n不许前视\n\"不是你这么粗暴的把这些都先写好那岂不是lookahead了\"\n10-28\n逐条读\n\"有用的，你得一条一条看是不是真的值得抽才知道你这些改动的抽取效果\"\n10-29 D215\n十一题\n1 A（收入增速 核心）；2 C→D216 \"2028 EPS 预测可以留下来啊，我的意思是因为太久远了，现在才2026\"；3 A（内存短缺 核心）；4 B（AMD 跑分 辅助）；5 C（新加坡案件 不留，D231 改为可选）；6 B（三条政策 辅助）；7 A（云厂商支出 核心）；8 B（Omniverse 辅助）；9 \"辅助属于供给需求\"（Sharon）；10 \"属于产品进度问题\"（NVL72x2）；11 \"一条吧都属于财务\"（Google Cloud 三个数一条）\n10-29 D218\n推断\n\"是推断，你不要再问我了，你要自己尝试 figure out 开始跑\"\n10-29 D220\n谁定标准\n\"你来修正对齐，你是 hedge fund analyst 你来做好标准\"\n10-29 D217\n目标模型\n\"现在开始以haiku 为模型取代luna … 每一轮都根据尺子去迭代 … 小心 overfit … 尺子其实不对 … scope creep\"（D234 起 Haiku 暂停）\n10-29 D226、D228\n评法\n合理的模糊和不同措辞可以接受；金标例子教的是判断逻辑，不是唯一答案；两套既有标准（证据层次、90 题评审）都要用；来源、用途、准入分开判\n10-29\n今天\n\"之前没有对齐一个hedge fund analyst 应该要用什么标准来抽研究观点\"\n10-29 D229\n标准不漂移\n\"1-5 都可以，按照这套标准来开始做测量，这条标准不要漂移\"\n10-29 D231\n新加坡\n\"新加坡那条就随意吧\"\n10-29 D233\n今年\n按公司财年或自然年写都算对\n10-29 D236\n三条提议\n\"好都按照你的提议来。但是你的prompt 改动要确认不是hardcode for nvidia 你在建构的是hedge fund brain\"\n10-29 D237\n通用\n\"It needs to be general purpose as a hedge fund brain\"\n10-29 D238\n直接读\n\"你根本不要再给我跑什么三轮了。每一篇的问题明显地出错的你不要再用工具尺子了，你直接下去看\"\n10-29 D240\n不完整的证据\n\"我不是和你说过不要这么死板吗？这个证据显然是有意义的，不一定什么条件数据时间都要满足，你应该把它记录下来，只是可能档位低一点\"\n10-29 D241\n这份标准\n\"几个大类型的账本 - 开题标准和档次 这两个要完整的定义好写好\"；\"档位根据信息量作定标 -以上有你自己trace 过的golden set 来做为改prompt测试标准\"\n10-29 D243\n签字\n第 5 条：\"这种典型的主张背景属于常识，基本上抽出来也没有太多的意义除非带数字和具体的叙事不然都是属于不采纳\"；方向：\"不是什么事情都是惊弓之鸟需要抽取，杂音还是很大的，我们整体应该倾向谨慎不要看什么都是线索的感觉，但是同时我们要敏感一些新的信息带来的增量不要错过\"；\"其他几条的意见我都ok，sign off\"\n10-29 D242\n这一页\n\"你给我一个网页，这个同时也是你这个环节重要的PRD\"\n10-30 D248\n颗粒度\n七条各选 A（第 6 条\"A， A\"）；原则：\"我觉得核心就是这个颗粒度的把控要符合一个高端的hedge fund analyst 的思维。不要搜集一堆鸡毛蒜皮的事情，要看重要的大事，合并整合关键的信息，这个是这个步骤的原则\"\n11\n在用的标准之间的冲突，怎么摆平\n编号对应盘点报告\ninventory-2026-10-29.md\n的 B1–B20，B21 是后来加的。\"按已拍的\"是已有决定，只是没落实或没写全；文首 12 条的决定记在 D243；\"我定\"只限措辞和记录约定。\n#\n冲突\n处理\n依据\nB1\n共同量的名字和定义：D191 改名并扩到价格；v16 起提示词和代码用\nindustry_aggregate\n、几家公司合计、一个日历年\n按 D191 落实为\nshared_quantity\n，期间仍用自然年；提示词和代码要跟着改\nD191、D243；PRD 06\nB2\n主驱动量含不含产品节点\n含，写成 A、B 两类\nD236（在 D184 之后）\nB3\n约束性义务：一笔或一个计划一题（D210），还是一组入股是一个判断（D201 规则 G）\n按 D210 \"一笔或一个计划\"；一笔交易属不属于已开的计划，由匹配判；同一计划的几种说法是一题；够不上公司规模的单笔交易不开题，陈述留不留照第 5 节判\nD210、D236、D238、D248\nB4\n开题测试三套，提示词和代码都没用\n第 1 版 §6 四个测试加评审三条写法检查（第 4 节）；测试 4 和库比的部分归匹配\n第 1 版 §6；评审 §1；D211\nB5\nPRD 05 还写着\nfolds_into\n、第 5 块\n抽取不看库；PRD 05 过时段落签字后改指这一页\nD211\nB6\n没家的证据扔不扔\n产品里扔，评估读被扔的、分四种归因；两者不冲突\nD209；第 1 版 §7\nB7\n归档规则三个版本\n以 D207 为准；归档提示里点名 NVIDIA 的那句按 D237 改成通用（后续一步）\nD207、D237\nB8\n分量几级、\"档位\"一词两义\n四级；\"档位\"在这一页叫分量，问题的叫结果档\n第 1 版 §10.3；D243 第 1 条\nB9\n几套\"不要\"的类\n抽取只有一套：按 5.3 各行及其边界判，判为不收的分量是\"不收\"；\"不可用\"属于判断这一步（第 7 节）；\"无关陈述\"用定稿金标计数\n第 1 版 §2、§4；D221；D240；D243 第 12 条\nB10\n股价、目标价、重述、新加坡各处判得不同\n财报后股价反应可选（第 1 版 §10.2）；在研公司的估值倍数、市值、孤零零的目标价可选；重提的过去实际数可选；新加坡可选\n第 1 版 §10.2；D231；D243 第 4、6 条\nB11\n主题怎么连到公司\n一对全部的主题块；文章明写的依赖照记；D208 的通道未建\nD191、D195\nB12\n研究组、驱动层\n研究组不是问题，不代替开题判断；七类驱动是重要性的理由；词汇表只禁 \"thesis driver\"\nD165、D208；评审 §6\nB13\nPRD 06 的重复和替代说法早于 D202、D204\n这一页按 D202、D204 写（第 2 节、5.5）；PRD 06 签字后更新\nD202、D204\nB14\n组成部分算不算同一题\n同一总量的另一种人群或口径是同一题；有自己驱动、答案能分开走、分析员会分别下注的是另一题，要过独立意义测试\nD190；第 1 版 §6；D248\nB15\n标的五档必须叫\"非常看多……\"（代码），还是写业务结果（D186）\n这一页按 D186 写；代码是另一模块，D186 未实现照旧记\nD186\nB16\n转述的公告算事实还是当事人陈述\n事实（role-v29 原文：\"an announcement or completed deal the item reports is a fact even when the reporter names no source\"）；混合的一条按主要说法定层\nPRD 06；D238、D239；D243 第 9 条\nB17\n代码按自然年拒公司明年的财年量\n已修：年限按公司财年数，测试\ntests/test_fiscal_year_horizon.py\nPRD 06 梯子第 5 条；D243\nB18\n\"线索\"\"背景\"一词多义\n\"线索\"只指研究用途的一级（第 7 节）；\"背景\"只指 5.3 那一行\nD221\nB19\n谁把候选并进已开问题\n匹配和碰撞裁决；共同量由代码\nD190、D197、D211\nB20\nAGENTS §8 指向两套标准，D229 又说第 1 版是唯一尺子\n这一页签字后取代第 1 版，AGENTS §8 指向这一页；对两套既有标准（PRD 06 证据层次、90 题评审）的指向保留，它们是这一页的出处\nD228、D229、D243\nB21\nPRD 06 State 写判断要 \"reconsider all evidence still in force\"，D198 之后只显示新到的和依据\n按 D198（在后）；PRD 06 签字后更新\nD198\n12\n校验记录和出处\n三轮独立读者仿真\n每一轮把标准和一组事先定死的案例交给互相看不到答案的读者：作者先写好自己的答案并记 sha256；Claude 的不同模型和 Codex 各读一份。读者只读标准和案例，不读决策、金标、提示词。几方不一致的案例，读原因，追到标准里的一句话，改那一句；下一轮换没见过的读者，加没见过的案例。共 192 个陈述、40 个问题；陈述里 152 个来自测试文章，40 个来自银行、药企、零售、航空、公用事业、矿业、保险等其他行业。\n第三轮，一对读者\n新案例：留不留\n新案例：连分量\n难案例复核：留不留\n新问题\n作者–Codex\n96%\n87%\n84%\n5/8\n作者–Claude sonnet\n94%\n83%\n91%\n6/8\n作者–Claude fable\n93%\n81%\n89%\n6/8\nCodex–sonnet\n94%\n83%\n86%\n7/8\nCodex–fable\n93%\n83%\n84%\n7/8\nsonnet–fable\n98%\n89%\n95%\n8/8\n新案例上留不留的一致率从第二轮的 80–94% 升到 93–98%，连分量从 72–87% 升到 81–89%。剩下的分歧几乎都在核心和辅助的界线，由 D243 第 1 条定下判法。三位读者判对、作者判错的，记作作者的错：最大客户带来的收入和中国销售占比不该开题（独立意义测试），还有两处陈述。记录在\nsimulation-log.md\n。\n十一轮漂移检查\nCodex 只读，逐条对照签过的第 1 版、全部决策、PRD 06，找\"作者自己加的规则\"、和决策矛盾的地方、引错的原话。每一轮的发现都改掉并记下处理。第一轮查出 18 处自己加的规则和 9 处和决策矛盾，比如\"名单外公司的量不开题\"违反 D155、D187，\"抽取要和库里比重复\"违反 D211，把达标线缩短，把\"在谈的金额\"排除出核心。最后三轮只剩措辞层面的问题；第十一轮的发现在 2.14 版改掉，没有再复查。\n36\n2.3\n约20\n2.4\n约10\n2.5\n6\n2.6\n8\n2.7\n7\n2.8\n7\n2.9\n6\n2.10\n5\n2.11\n6\n2.12\n5\n2.13\n被检查的版本；颜色浅的两根是约数\n出处\nPRD 06（Two thesis kinds、Ladder、Four evidence tiers、State、Succession）；PRD 05；第 1 版标准（D229）；\nresearch_notes/对冲基金论点粒度与金标/codex-challenger.md\n§1 八条和 §6、\nindustry-debate-standards.md\n（主分支）；决定 D155、D165、D184、D185、D186、D187、D190、D191、D195、D197、D198、D199、D201、D202、D204、D207、D208、D209、D210、D211、D215、D216、D218、D220、D221、D226、D228、D229、D231、D233、D236、D237、D238、D240、D241、D242。\n文件\n这一页：\ndocs/prd/05-extraction-standard.html\n。改它之前先在\ndocs/DECISIONS.md\n记下 Alex 的原话；改完跑一次只读的漂移检查。\n起草过程和证据：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-ledger-standard/\n：\nstandard-v2.md\n到\nstandard-v2.14.md\n、\nsimulation-log.md\n、\nreview-drift-v2.*.md\n、\ninventory-2026-10-29.md\n、\ngolden-v3/\n。\n定稿金标：\ngolden-v3/golden-v3-signed.json\n（sha256 见第 8 节），由\ngolden-v3/finalize.py\n从起草稿定稿。\n第 1 版：\ndocs/research/2026-10-29-analyst-standard/standard.md\n（D229），已由这一页取代，留作出处。\nHivemind · 抽取与开题标准 · 第 5 版 · 2026-10-29 Alex 签字（D243），2026-10-30 按 Alex 逐个选的七条修订（D248），2026-10-31 按 Alex 定的证据四条界线和计数单位修订（D250）。页首的 standard-version 是规则的版本号：改任何一条规则都要升它，金标随之重定，打分程序遇到版本对不上就拒绝打分（D244）。\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
